window.MORE=(window.MORE||[]).concat([
{id:"scimeth",t:"The Scientific Method",c:"History of Science",i:"🔬",p:[["What it is","The scientific method is a way of learning about the world by asking a question, proposing an explanation called a hypothesis, testing it with observations or experiments and revising it when the evidence disagrees."],["Early roots","The Arab scholar Ibn al-Haytham, writing in Cairo around 1020, tested ideas about light and vision with careful experiments. In the 1600s Francis Bacon argued that knowledge should be built from observation rather than from ancient authorities."],["Testing ideas","A good hypothesis makes predictions that could turn out to be wrong. The philosopher Karl Popper called this falsifiability and argued that it separates science from claims that cannot be tested."],["Peer review","Scientists share methods and results so others can check and repeat them. Journals such as the Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society, first published in 1665, made this exchange regular."]],r:["galileo","goldage"]},
{id:"copern",t:"The Copernican Revolution",c:"History of Science",i:"☀️",p:[["The old view","For about 1,400 years most European and Islamic astronomers used the system of Ptolemy, who placed a motionless Earth at the center of the universe, with the Sun and planets circling it."],["Copernicus","The Polish astronomer Nicolaus Copernicus placed the Sun at the center instead. His book On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres was published in 1543, the year he died."],["Kepler","Using the precise observations of Tycho Brahe, Johannes Kepler showed in 1609 that planets move in ellipses, not perfect circles, which made the Sun-centered model far more accurate."],["Why it mattered","The shift showed that careful observation could overturn long-held ideas, and it led directly to the work of Galileo and Newton."]],r:["galileo","solar"]},
{id:"galileo",t:"Galileo Galilei",c:"History of Science",i:"🔭",p:[["Life","Galileo Galilei was born in Pisa, Italy, in 1564. He taught mathematics at the universities of Pisa and Padua and became one of the founders of modern physics and astronomy."],["The telescope","In 1609 and 1610 he built improved telescopes and saw mountains on the Moon, the phases of Venus and four moons orbiting Jupiter, now called the Galilean moons. These findings supported the idea that Earth was not the center of everything."],["Motion","Galileo studied falling bodies and rolling balls and found that, without air resistance, objects fall with the same acceleration regardless of their weight."],["Trial","In 1633 the Roman Inquisition found him suspect of heresy for defending the Sun-centered model, and he spent the rest of his life under house arrest. He died in 1642."]],r:["copern","newton"]},
{id:"goldage",t:"Science in the Islamic Golden Age",c:"History of Science",i:"📜",p:[["The House of Wisdom","From the 800s, scholars in Baghdad translated Greek, Persian and Indian works into Arabic at a center of learning later known as the House of Wisdom, preserving and extending ancient knowledge."],["Mathematics","Al-Khwarizmi wrote a book on solving equations around 820 whose title gave us the word algebra, and his name gave us the word algorithm. Hindu-Arabic numerals spread to Europe through these texts."],["Medicine","Ibn Sina, known in Europe as Avicenna, wrote the Canon of Medicine in about 1025. It was used as a medical textbook in Europe and the Middle East for centuries."],["Optics and astronomy","Ibn al-Haytham explained that we see because light enters the eye, and observatories such as the one at Maragha, founded in 1259, produced improved star tables."]],r:["alg","scimeth"]},
{id:"alchemy",t:"From Alchemy to Chemistry",c:"History of Science",i:"⚗️",p:[["Alchemy","For centuries alchemists in China, the Islamic world and Europe tried to turn common metals into gold and to find an elixir of long life. They failed, but they developed useful tools such as distillation."],["Boyle","In 1661 Robert Boyle published The Sceptical Chymist, arguing that matter is made of tiny particles and that ideas should be tested by experiment."],["Lavoisier","The French chemist Antoine Lavoisier showed that burning is a reaction with oxygen and that mass is conserved in chemical reactions. He is often called the father of modern chemistry."],["The periodic table","In 1869 Dmitri Mendeleev arranged the known elements by atomic weight and left gaps for elements not yet discovered, correctly predicting their properties."]],r:["atom","acid"]},
{id:"pasteur",t:"Louis Pasteur and Germ Theory",c:"History of Science",i:"🦠",p:[["Germ theory","Germ theory states that many diseases are caused by microorganisms. Before it was accepted, many people blamed disease on bad air, called miasma."],["Pasteurization","In the 1860s the French chemist Louis Pasteur showed that microbes spoil wine and milk, and that gentle heating kills them. This process, pasteurization, still makes milk safe to drink."],["Koch","The German doctor Robert Koch identified the bacteria that cause anthrax, tuberculosis (in 1882) and cholera, and set out rules for proving that a germ causes a disease."],["Vaccines","Pasteur developed vaccines against chicken cholera, anthrax and rabies. In 1885 he successfully treated a boy, Joseph Meister, who had been bitten by a rabid dog."]],r:["microbe","vacc","germtheory"]},
{id:"curie",t:"Marie Curie",c:"History of Science",i:"☢️",p:[["Early life","Maria Skłodowska was born in Warsaw in 1867. Women could not attend university in Russian-ruled Poland, so she moved to Paris in 1891 to study physics and mathematics."],["Radioactivity","With her husband Pierre Curie she studied the rays given off by uranium, which she named radioactivity, and in 1898 they discovered two new elements, polonium and radium."],["Two Nobel Prizes","She shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics and won the 1911 Nobel Prize in Chemistry alone. She remains the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences."],["Legacy","During World War I she organized mobile X-ray units for the wounded. She died in 1934 from an illness linked to her long exposure to radiation."]],r:["atom","nuclear"]},
{id:"currents",t:"Edison, Tesla and the War of the Currents",c:"History of Science",i:"💡",p:[["Edison","Thomas Edison developed a practical, long-lasting light bulb in 1879 and opened the Pearl Street power station in New York in 1882, supplying direct current (DC)."],["Tesla","Nikola Tesla, a Serbian-American inventor, designed motors and systems for alternating current (AC), whose voltage can be raised and lowered with transformers to send power over long distances."],["The rivalry","In the late 1880s Edison’s company warned that AC was dangerous, while George Westinghouse backed Tesla’s system. AC lit the 1893 Chicago World’s Fair and powered the Niagara Falls plant from 1895."],["Result","Alternating current became the standard for power grids worldwide, though direct current is used today in batteries, electronics and some long undersea cables."]],r:["elec","battery"]},
{id:"penicil",t:"The Discovery of Penicillin",c:"History of Science",i:"💊",p:[["A lucky accident","In 1928 the Scottish scientist Alexander Fleming returned from a holiday to find that a mold had killed the bacteria growing on one of his dishes. He named the substance it produced penicillin."],["Making it work","Penicillin was hard to purify. In 1940 and 1941 Howard Florey, Ernst Chain and their team at Oxford turned it into a drug and showed that it could cure infections in people."],["Mass production","During World War II American companies learned to grow the mold in huge tanks, and penicillin saved many wounded soldiers. Fleming, Florey and Chain shared the 1945 Nobel Prize."],["Resistance","Overusing antibiotics lets resistant bacteria survive and spread. Fleming himself warned about this in his Nobel lecture, and resistance is now a major global health concern."]],r:["microbe","pasteur"]},
{id:"hubble",t:"Edwin Hubble and the Expanding Universe",c:"History of Science",i:"🌌",p:[["Island universes","In the early 1900s astronomers debated whether the Milky Way was the whole universe. In 1924 Edwin Hubble measured the distance to the Andromeda nebula and showed it was a separate galaxy far beyond our own."],["Redshift","Light from distant galaxies is stretched toward the red end of the spectrum, which shows they are moving away from us."],["Hubble’s law","In 1929 Hubble showed that the farther away a galaxy is, the faster it recedes. The Belgian priest and physicist Georges Lemaître had reached a similar conclusion in 1927. Together their work showed that the universe is expanding."],["Legacy","An expanding universe pointed back to a hot, dense beginning, the Big Bang. The Hubble Space Telescope, launched in 1990, is named in his honor."]],r:["bigbang","milky"]}]);
