// People, part 2: Filipino leaders of the 1800s and 1900s.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"balagtas",t:"Francisco Balagtas",i:"📜",w:"Francisco Balagtas",a:["Francisco Balagtas"],born:"2 April 1788, Panginay, Bigaa, Bulacan",died:"20 February 1862, Orion, Bataan",nat:"Filipino",known:"Writing the epic poem Florante at Laura",r:["phlit","delpilar"],
l:"Francisco Balagtas was a Tagalog poet whose long poem Florante at Laura is one of the greatest works of Philippine literature. Called the Prince of Tagalog Poets, he showed that Tagalog could be a language of fine poetry, and his name lives on in the balagtasan, a Filipino poetic debate.",
d:[["1788","Born in Panginay, Bigaa, Bulacan"],["c. 1835","Imprisoned in Manila"],["1838","Florante at Laura is published"],["1842","Marries Juana Tiambeng in Orion, Bataan"],["1862","Dies in Orion, Bataan"]],
s:[["Early life","Francisco Balagtas, also known as Francisco Baltazar, was born on 2 April 1788 in Bigaa, Bulacan, to a blacksmith’s family. As a boy he moved to Tondo, Manila, where he worked as a houseboy for a wealthy relative in exchange for schooling."],
["Education","Balagtas studied at the Colegio de San José and San Juan de Letran, learning Latin, Spanish, philosophy and religion. He began writing poems for friends and neighbors and was coached by José de la Cruz, known as Huseng Sisiw, a famous Tondo poet."],
["Love and prison","In Pandacan, Balagtas fell in love with María Asunción Rivera, known as Celia. A rich rival, Mariano Capule, is said to have used his wealth to have Balagtas imprisoned. While in prison, Balagtas wrote Florante at Laura, dedicating it to Celia."],
["Florante at Laura","Florante at Laura, published in 1838, is an awit, a metrical romance in 12-syllable lines grouped in four-line stanzas. Set in the kingdom of Albania, it tells of the hero Florante, betrayed by the evil Adolfo, and his love for Laura. It also contains hidden criticism of Spanish rule."],
["Hidden meanings","Because writing openly against Spain was dangerous, Balagtas set his story in a far-off land. Scholars and readers, including José Rizal, saw in its descriptions of a land suffering under tyranny and betrayal a picture of the Philippines under colonial rule."],
["Later life","Balagtas moved to Orion, Bataan, where he married Juana Tiambeng in 1842 and had several children. He worked as a court clerk and translator. He was jailed again after a dispute, then released. He continued writing plays and poems, many now lost."],
["Death","Balagtas died on 20 February 1862 in Orion, Bataan, aged 73. Legend says he asked that none of his children become poets, to spare them the hardships he had endured. His tomb in Orion is a historical site."],
["The balagtasan","The balagtasan, a poetic debate in verse named after him, began in 1924. Two poets argue opposite sides of a topic before an audience, with a moderator. The first famous balagtasan featured José Corazón de Jesús and Florentino Collantes."],
["Influence on Filipino writing","Florante at Laura became a model for Tagalog poetry. Lines from it are still quoted, and it is studied in high schools across the country. Writers including José Rizal admired it. It helped establish Tagalog as a literary language."],
["Remembering Balagtas","His hometown, Bigaa, was renamed Balagtas in his honor. His birthday, 2 April, is celebrated as Balagtas Day. The Gawad Balagtas award recognizes outstanding writers, and his image appeared on the old 10-centavo coin."]],
sp:["A famous stanza","One of the most quoted parts of Florante at Laura is the hero’s lament about the land of Albania, ruled by cruelty and lies, where virtue is buried and evil rewarded. Many readers in the 1800s understood it as a description of their own country. José Rizal is said to have carried a copy of the poem with him in Europe."],
g:[["Awit","A metrical romance in 12-syllable lines"],["Stanza","A group of lines in a poem"],["Balagtasan","A poetic debate named after Balagtas"],["Allegory","A story with a hidden meaning"]],
f:["The town of Bigaa, Bulacan, is now called Balagtas.","Florante at Laura has 399 stanzas.","Balagtas was taught by the poet Huseng Sisiw."]},

{id:"aguinaldo",t:"Emilio Aguinaldo",i:"🎖️",w:"Emilio Aguinaldo",a:["Emilio Aguinaldo","Aguinaldo"],born:"22 March 1869, Kawit, Cavite",died:"6 February 1964, Quezon City",nat:"Filipino",known:"Declaring Philippine independence and leading the First Philippine Republic",r:["malolos","independence","phrev","mabini"],
l:"Emilio Aguinaldo was a revolutionary leader who declared Philippine independence from Spain on 12 June 1898 and became president of the First Philippine Republic. He led Filipino forces against Spain and then the United States. His role in the death of Andrés Bonifacio remains controversial.",
d:[["1869","Born in Kawit, Cavite"],["1897","Elected president at the Tejeros Convention"],["1897","Pact of Biak-na-Bato; exile in Hong Kong"],["12 June 1898","Declares independence in Kawit"],["1899","Inaugurated president of the First Philippine Republic"],["1901","Captured by American forces in Palanan"],["1964","Dies aged 94"]],
s:[["Early life","Emilio Aguinaldo was born on 22 March 1869 in Kawit, Cavite, into a well-off family. He left school early to help with the family business. At 26 he became gobernadorcillo, town head, of Kawit and joined the Katipunan in 1895."],
["Rise in the revolution","When the revolution began in 1896, Aguinaldo won victories against Spanish forces in Cavite, becoming a hero. The Katipunan in Cavite split into rival factions, the Magdalo, led by Aguinaldo’s cousin, and the Magdiwang, which supported Bonifacio."],
["Tejeros and Bonifacio","At the Tejeros Convention in March 1897, Aguinaldo was elected president of a new revolutionary government, defeating Bonifacio. Bonifacio rejected the result. He was arrested, tried by a military court and executed in May 1897. Historians debate how much Aguinaldo was responsible."],
["Biak-na-Bato","In December 1897 Aguinaldo agreed to the Pact of Biak-na-Bato with Spain, accepting money and exile in Hong Kong in exchange for ending the fighting. Neither side fully kept the agreement, and Aguinaldo used the funds to buy weapons."],
["Independence","In 1898, during the Spanish–American War, Aguinaldo returned with American help. On 12 June 1898, from the window of his house in Kawit, he proclaimed Philippine independence, and the Philippine flag was raised and the national anthem played for the first time."],
["The First Republic","The Malolos Congress wrote a constitution, and on 23 January 1899, Aguinaldo was inaugurated as president of the First Philippine Republic, the first constitutional republic in Asia. However, Spain had sold the Philippines to the United States in the Treaty of Paris."],
["War with the United States","Fighting broke out between Filipino and American forces in February 1899. Aguinaldo’s army was pushed north and turned to guerrilla warfare. On 23 March 1901, American troops captured him in Palanan, Isabela, using a trick, and he swore allegiance to the United States."],
["Later life","Aguinaldo retired to farming but remained a public figure. In 1935 he ran for president of the Commonwealth and lost to Manuel L. Quezon. During the Japanese occupation he cooperated with the Japanese, for which he was briefly jailed after the war and later pardoned."],
["A long life","Aguinaldo lived to see independence recognized by the United States in 1946 and the changing of Independence Day to 12 June in 1962. He died on 6 February 1964, aged 94. His house in Kawit is now the Aguinaldo Shrine."],
["His legacy","Aguinaldo is honored as the first president of the Philippines and as the leader who proclaimed independence. His role in the deaths of Bonifacio and Antonio Luna and his later cooperation with the Japanese make him a debated figure. Most Filipinos, however, see his 1898 declaration as a founding moment."]],
sp:["The Kawit balcony","On 12 June 1898, a crowd gathered at Aguinaldo’s house in Kawit to hear the Act of the Declaration of Philippine Independence, read by Ambrosio Rianzares Bautista. The flag, sewn in Hong Kong by Marcela Agoncillo, her daughter and Delfina Herbosa de Natividad, was unfurled, and the Marcha Nacional Filipina by Julián Felipe was played. Interestingly, the famous balcony of the house was added later; the declaration was made from a window."],
g:[["Declaration","A formal public announcement"],["Republic","A state without a monarch"],["Guerrilla warfare","Fighting in small, mobile groups"],["Inauguration","A ceremony that begins a leader’s term"]],
f:["Aguinaldo was 29 when he declared independence.","He lived to 94, longer than most leaders of his time.","Independence Day was moved to 12 June in 1962."]},

{id:"quezon",t:"Manuel L. Quezon",i:"🏛️",w:"Manuel L. Quezon",a:["Manuel L. Quezon","Manuel Quezon"],born:"19 August 1878, Baler, Tayabas (now Aurora)",died:"1 August 1944, Saranac Lake, New York, USA",nat:"Filipino",known:"Serving as president of the Philippine Commonwealth",r:["commonwealth","japocc","phlang"],
l:"Manuel L. Quezon was the president of the Commonwealth of the Philippines from 1935 to 1944, preparing the country for full independence. A powerful speaker and skilled politician, he championed a national language and social justice and led the government in exile during the Second World War.",
d:[["1878","Born in Baler"],["1899","Fights in the Philippine–American War"],["1909","Becomes resident commissioner in Washington"],["1916","Becomes president of the new Philippine Senate"],["1935","Elected president of the Commonwealth"],["1937","Proclaims a Tagalog-based national language"],["1944","Dies in the United States"]],
s:[["Early life","Manuel Luis Quezon y Molina was born on 19 August 1878 in Baler, then part of Tayabas province, now Aurora. His parents were teachers. He studied at the Colegio de San Juan de Letran and law at the University of Santo Tomas."],
["Soldier and lawyer","During the Philippine–American War, Quezon joined Aguinaldo’s army and became a major. After surrendering in 1901, he finished his law studies, passing the bar with high marks in 1903, and became a provincial prosecutor and then governor of Tayabas."],
["In Washington","In 1909 Quezon became one of the Philippines’ resident commissioners in the United States Congress. He campaigned for the Jones Act of 1916, which promised independence once a stable government was established, and created an elected Philippine Senate."],
["Senate president","Quezon served as president of the Philippine Senate from 1916 to 1935, becoming the most powerful Filipino politician. He led missions to Washington and helped win the Tydings–McDuffie Act of 1934, which set up the Commonwealth and promised independence after ten years."],
["Commonwealth president","Quezon was elected president in September 1935, defeating Emilio Aguinaldo, and took office on 15 November 1935. He built up national defense, launched social justice programs, supported women’s right to vote, approved in a 1937 plebiscite, and promoted land reform."],
["National language","Quezon believed Filipinos needed a common language. In 1937 he proclaimed Tagalog as the basis of the national language. He is called the Father of the National Language, and National Language Month, now Buwan ng Wika, is held in August, his birth month."],
["Helping refugees","Quezon supported a plan to welcome Jewish refugees fleeing Nazi persecution in Europe. Between 1937 and 1941 about 1,300 Jewish refugees settled in the Philippines, and Quezon offered land in Marikina for a settlement."],
["War and exile","When Japan invaded in December 1941, Quezon moved to Corregidor with General Douglas MacArthur, then escaped to Australia and the United States. He led the Commonwealth government in exile in Washington. He suffered from tuberculosis and died on 1 August 1944 at Saranac Lake, New York."],
["Quezon City","Quezon planned a new capital city in the hills northeast of Manila, with wide avenues and space for government buildings. Founded in 1939 and named after him, Quezon City served as the national capital from 1948 to 1976 and is now the country’s most populous city."],
["His legacy","Quezon is remembered for laying the foundations of an independent Philippines, though critics note he concentrated power in his own hands. His famous words, I would rather have a government run like hell by Filipinos than a government run like heaven by Americans, express his drive for self-rule."]],
sp:["Run like hell by Filipinos","Quezon is often quoted as saying he would prefer a government run like hell by Filipinos to one run like heaven by Americans, because a Filipino government could always be changed by Filipinos. The words express his belief that self-government, even imperfect, was better than rule by others. The quote remains popular in debates about Philippine independence and national identity."],
g:[["Commonwealth","A self-governing state linked to another country"],["Resident commissioner","A territory’s representative in the US Congress"],["Plebiscite","A direct vote by citizens on an issue"],["Government in exile","A government operating from abroad"]],
f:["Quezon City was the capital of the Philippines from 1948 to 1976.","Quezon’s face appears on the 20-peso bill and coin.","Quezon province and Quezon City are both named after him."]},

{id:"magsaysay",t:"Ramon Magsaysay",i:"🤝",w:"Ramon Magsaysay",a:["Ramon Magsaysay","Magsaysay"],born:"31 August 1907, Iba, Zambales",died:"17 March 1957, Mount Manunggal, Cebu",nat:"Filipino",known:"Popular presidency as the Champion of the Masses",r:["commonwealth","ph"],
l:"Ramon Magsaysay was the seventh president of the Philippines, serving from 1953 until his death in a plane crash in 1957. A former mechanic and guerrilla fighter, he was famous for his honesty, closeness to ordinary people and success against the Huk rebellion. He was called the Champion of the Masses.",
d:[["1907","Born in Iba, Zambales"],["1942–1945","Guerrilla leader during the Japanese occupation"],["1950","Becomes secretary of national defense"],["1953","Elected president in a landslide"],["1957","Killed in a plane crash in Cebu"],["1957","Ramon Magsaysay Award established"]],
s:[["Early life","Ramon del Fierro Magsaysay was born on 31 August 1907 in Iba, Zambales. His father was a carpenter and teacher. He studied engineering at the University of the Philippines and commerce at the José Rizal College, working as a chauffeur to support himself."],
["Mechanic and manager","Magsaysay worked as a mechanic and later as manager of a bus company. He loved machines and was known for being practical and hardworking. He married Luz Banzon in 1933, and they had three children."],
["War hero","When Japan invaded in 1941, Magsaysay joined the military and later led guerrilla forces in Zambales. By the end of the war, he commanded about 10,000 guerrillas. After liberation he was appointed military governor of Zambales."],
["Congressman","In 1946 Magsaysay was elected to the House of Representatives. He worked to secure benefits for war veterans and traveled to the United States to lobby for them. He became known for his energy and plain speaking."],
["Defeating the Huks","As defense secretary from 1950, Magsaysay fought the Hukbalahap, or Huk, communist rebellion. He reformed the army, punished abuses and offered land and help to rebels who surrendered. The rebellion weakened sharply, and his popularity soared."],
["President","In 1953 Magsaysay ran for president and won by a landslide. He opened Malacañang Palace to ordinary people, traveled widely to the provinces and set up a complaints office. He promoted rural development, land reform, roads and artesian wells."],
["Foreign policy","Magsaysay was a strong ally of the United States. In 1954 the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization, SEATO, was founded in Manila. Critics argued that he was too close to the Americans, while supporters valued his firm stand against communism."],
["Death","On 17 March 1957, the presidential plane Mt. Pinatubo crashed into Mount Manunggal in Cebu, killing Magsaysay and 24 others; one passenger, a journalist, survived. More than two million people are said to have attended his funeral in Manila."],
["The Ramon Magsaysay Award","Established in 1957, the Ramon Magsaysay Award is often called Asia’s Nobel Prize. It honors people and organizations in Asia for outstanding public service. Winners have included Mother Teresa, the Dalai Lama and many Filipinos."],
["His legacy","Magsaysay is remembered as one of the most beloved Filipino presidents, a man of the people who showed that honest leadership was possible. Monuments to him stand in many towns. Avenues and towns across the country carry his name."]],
sp:["Malacañang for the people","Before Magsaysay, Malacañang Palace was mostly closed to the public. He opened its gates so that farmers, workers and ordinary citizens could bring their problems directly to the president. He often wore a simple barong and ate with visitors. He also set up the Presidential Complaints and Action Commission so that complaints could be answered quickly. These gestures made him a symbol of a government that listened to the people."],
g:[["Guerrilla","A fighter in an irregular force"],["Rebellion","An armed uprising against a government"],["Landslide","An election won by a very large margin"],["Land reform","Redistributing land to farmers"]],
f:["Magsaysay was the first Philippine president born in the 20th century.","He was 49 when he died.","The Magsaysay Award is presented in Manila each year."]},

{id:"marcos",t:"Ferdinand Marcos",i:"⚖️",w:"Ferdinand Marcos",a:["Ferdinand Marcos","martial law"],born:"11 September 1917, Sarrat, Ilocos Norte",died:"28 September 1989, Honolulu, Hawaii, USA",nat:"Filipino",known:"Ruling under martial law until ousted by People Power in 1986",r:["edsa","ninoy","cory","phecon"],
l:"Ferdinand Marcos was the tenth president of the Philippines, ruling from 1965 to 1986. In 1972 he declared martial law and ruled with almost unlimited power. His supporters point to roads, schools and other projects built in his time; his rule is also remembered for widespread human rights abuses, corruption and debt. He was removed by the People Power Revolution in 1986.",
d:[["1917","Born in Sarrat, Ilocos Norte"],["1965","Elected president"],["1969","Re-elected"],["21 September 1972","Signs Proclamation 1081, declaring martial law"],["1981","Lifts martial law formally but keeps wide powers"],["February 1986","Ousted by the EDSA People Power Revolution"],["1989","Dies in exile in Hawaii"]],
s:[["Early life","Ferdinand Emmanuel Edralin Marcos was born on 11 September 1917 in Sarrat, Ilocos Norte. He studied law at the University of the Philippines. In 1939 he was convicted of the murder of a political rival of his father, but he argued his own appeal before the Supreme Court and was acquitted in 1940."],
["War record","During the Second World War, Marcos served in the army. He later claimed to have been a highly decorated guerrilla leader. Historians and United States military records have found that many of these claims were false or greatly exaggerated."],
["Rise to power","Marcos was elected to the House of Representatives in 1949 and the Senate in 1959. In 1954 he married Imelda Romuáldez. In 1965 he was elected president, and in 1969 he became the first president to win a second full term, in an election marked by heavy spending and violence."],
["Martial law","On 21 September 1972, citing threats from communist rebels and unrest, Marcos signed Proclamation 1081, placing the country under martial law; he announced it on 23 September. Congress was closed, opposition leaders and journalists were arrested, and newspapers and broadcasters were shut down or controlled."],
["Human rights","Under martial law, thousands of people were detained without trial. Amnesty International and other groups documented torture, killings and disappearances. In 2013 the Philippine government passed a law to compensate victims; a board recognized more than 11,000 claims."],
["The economy","The Marcos years saw new roads, bridges, schools and cultural buildings, such as the Cultural Center of the Philippines. Economic growth was strong in the 1970s, but foreign debt rose sharply and wealth was concentrated among close associates. In the early 1980s the economy fell into a severe crisis."],
["Corruption","After Marcos fell, investigators found evidence that he, his family and associates had taken billions of dollars from the country. Swiss courts returned hundreds of millions of dollars in deposits to the Philippines. Imelda Marcos became famous for her lavish lifestyle, including a collection of hundreds of pairs of shoes."],
["Downfall","The assassination of opposition leader Ninoy Aquino in 1983 sparked mass protests. Marcos called a snap election in February 1986 against Corazon Aquino. Widespread fraud led to the EDSA People Power Revolution, and on 25 February 1986 Marcos and his family fled to Hawaii."],
["After exile","Marcos died in Honolulu on 28 September 1989. His body was returned to Ilocos Norte in 1993 and buried in the Libingan ng mga Bayani, the Heroes’ Cemetery, in 2016, a decision that sparked protests. His son, Ferdinand Marcos Jr., was elected president in 2022."],
["A divided legacy","Marcos remains one of the most debated figures in Philippine history. Supporters remember stability and building projects; victims and historians emphasize the abuses, censorship and plunder of martial law. Teaching this period accurately is widely considered important for understanding Philippine democracy."]],
sp:["Remembering martial law","The Human Rights Victims Reparation and Recognition Act of 2013 officially recognized victims of human rights violations during the Marcos regime and set up a memorial commission. The Bantayog ng mga Bayani in Quezon City honors people who fought the dictatorship, with names carved on a Wall of Remembrance. Each September, Filipinos mark the anniversary of the declaration of martial law with discussions, exhibits and protests, so that its lessons are not forgotten."],
g:[["Martial law","Military rule replacing normal civil law"],["Dictatorship","Rule by one person with unlimited power"],["Human rights","Basic rights and freedoms of every person"],["Snap election","An election called earlier than scheduled"]],
f:["Martial law officially lasted from 1972 to 1981.","Marcos ruled for more than 20 years, longer than any other Philippine president.","EDSA, where People Power took place, is Metro Manila’s main highway."]},

{id:"ninoy",t:"Ninoy Aquino",i:"🕊️",w:"Benigno Aquino Jr.",a:["Ninoy Aquino","Benigno Aquino Jr."],born:"27 November 1932, Concepcion, Tarlac",died:"21 August 1983, Manila International Airport",nat:"Filipino",known:"Opposing martial law; his assassination sparked People Power",r:["cory","marcos","edsa"],
l:"Benigno Aquino Jr., known as Ninoy, was a journalist, senator and the leading opponent of Ferdinand Marcos. Jailed for more than seven years under martial law and then exiled, he was shot dead on the airport tarmac as he returned to Manila in 1983. His death united the opposition and led to the People Power Revolution.",
d:[["1932","Born in Concepcion, Tarlac"],["1950","Covers the Korean War as a young reporter"],["1955","Elected mayor of Concepcion at 22"],["1967","Elected senator"],["1972","Arrested when martial law is declared"],["1980","Allowed to go to the United States for heart surgery"],["21 August 1983","Assassinated on his return to Manila"]],
s:[["Early life","Benigno Simeon Aquino Jr. was born on 27 November 1932 in Concepcion, Tarlac, into a political family. His father and grandfather were politicians. He was a bright and restless student and began working as a journalist while still a teenager."],
["Young reporter","At 17, Ninoy covered the Korean War for the Manila Times, earning a Philippine Legion of Honor award. He later helped negotiate the surrender of Huk rebel leader Luis Taruc in 1954, gaining national attention."],
["Youngest politician","Ninoy became mayor of Concepcion at 22, vice governor of Tarlac at 27 and governor at 29. In 1967, at 34, he became the youngest elected senator in Philippine history. He married Corazon Cojuangco in 1954, and they had five children."],
["Critic of Marcos","In the Senate, Ninoy became the sharpest critic of President Marcos, warning that Marcos planned to rule indefinitely. He was widely expected to win the presidential election due in 1973, which never took place."],
["Prison","When martial law was declared in 1972, Ninoy was among the first arrested. He was held for more than seven years, much of it in solitary confinement, and a military court sentenced him to death in 1977. He went on a 40-day hunger strike in protest."],
["Exile","In 1980, after a heart attack, Ninoy was allowed to go to the United States for surgery. He lived in Boston with his family, teaching at Harvard and MIT and speaking against the dictatorship. In 1983 he decided to return home, despite warnings that he would be killed."],
["Assassination","On 21 August 1983, Ninoy flew into Manila International Airport. Moments after soldiers escorted him off the plane, he was shot in the head on the stairs to the tarmac. A man named Rolando Galman, blamed by the military, was also killed. Courts later convicted soldiers of the murder."],
["A nation awakens","About two million people joined Ninoy’s funeral procession, one of the largest in Philippine history. Protests grew, and his widow, Corazon Aquino, became the leader of the opposition. Within three years, the People Power Revolution ended the Marcos regime."],
["The airport","In 1987 Manila International Airport was renamed Ninoy Aquino International Airport, NAIA, in his honor. 21 August is a national holiday, Ninoy Aquino Day. His face appears on the 500-peso bill alongside his wife, Corazon."],
["His legacy","Ninoy is remembered as a symbol of courage and sacrifice for democracy. His son Benigno Aquino III served as president from 2010 to 2016. His words and example continue to inspire Filipinos who defend freedom and the rule of law."]],
sp:["The Filipino is worth dying for","In a speech he prepared to deliver on his arrival in Manila, Ninoy wrote that he had returned to join in the struggle for freedom through nonviolence. Earlier, he had told an audience in the United States that the Filipino is worth dying for. He never delivered his arrival speech, but its words, published after his death, became a rallying cry for the movement that restored democracy in 1986."],
g:[["Opposition","Politicians who challenge those in power"],["Solitary confinement","Imprisonment alone in a cell"],["Hunger strike","Refusing food as a protest"],["Tarmac","The paved area of an airport"]],
f:["Ninoy became a senator at 34.","He was in prison for more than seven years.","Ninoy Aquino Day, 21 August, is a national holiday."]}
);

;
