// People, part 10: writers, artists, champions and campaigners from Africa, Asia and Latin America.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"maathai",t:"Wangari Maathai",i:"🌳",w:"Wangari Maathai",a:["Wangari Maathai"],born:"1 April 1940, Ihithe, Kenya",died:"25 September 2011, Nairobi, Kenya",nat:"Kenyan",known:"Founding the Green Belt Movement and winning the Nobel Peace Prize",r:["trees","kenyacountry","climchange","hrights"],
l:"Wangari Maathai was a Kenyan biologist and environmental and political activist. In 1977 she founded the Green Belt Movement, which helped women plant tens of millions of trees to restore land, provide firewood and fight poverty. She campaigned for democracy and human rights at great personal risk, and in 2004 she became the first African woman to win the Nobel Peace Prize.",
d:[["1940","Born in Ihithe, in Kenya’s central highlands"],["1960","Wins a scholarship to study in the United States"],["1971","Becomes the first woman in East and Central Africa to earn a doctorate"],["1977","Founds the Green Belt Movement"],["1989","Leads protests against a tower in Uhuru Park"],["2002","Elected to Kenya’s parliament"],["2004","Wins the Nobel Peace Prize"]],
s:[["Childhood in the highlands","Wangari Muta Maathai was born in 1940 in a farming village in the highlands of central Kenya, then a British colony. As a girl she fetched water from a stream near a fig tree, where she watched frogs’ eggs and tadpoles. Unusually for girls of the time, she was sent to school, where she excelled."],
["Studying abroad","In 1960 Maathai was one of about 300 Kenyan students chosen to study in the United States in a program known as the Kennedy Airlift. She earned a degree in biology from Mount St. Scholastica College in Kansas and a master’s degree from the University of Pittsburgh, then continued her studies in Germany."],
["A pioneering scientist","Back in Kenya, Maathai studied veterinary anatomy at the University of Nairobi, and in 1971 she became the first woman in East and Central Africa to earn a doctorate. She became the first woman to head a university department there, while campaigning for equal benefits for women staff."],
["Listening to rural women","Through the National Council of Women of Kenya, Maathai heard rural women describe their problems: streams drying up, less firewood, poor harvests and hungry families. She saw that these problems came from cutting down forests, and that planting trees could help solve them."],
["The Green Belt Movement","In 1977 Maathai planted seven trees in a Nairobi park to launch the Green Belt Movement. It paid women a small amount for each seedling that survived, giving them income while restoring the land. The movement grew to thousands of groups and has planted more than 50 million trees in Kenya."],
["Standing up to power","Maathai’s campaigns brought her into conflict with the government of President Daniel arap Moi. In 1989 she led protests that stopped the building of a 60-story tower in Nairobi’s Uhuru Park. She was called a madwoman and was beaten unconscious by police at one protest, arrested and jailed, but she would not give up."],
["Democracy","Maathai joined the movement for multiparty democracy in Kenya and supported mothers of political prisoners who went on hunger strike in 1992. When the opposition won elections in 2002, she was elected to parliament with an overwhelming majority and became assistant minister for the environment."],
["The Nobel Prize","In 2004 Maathai won the Nobel Peace Prize for her contribution to sustainable development, democracy and peace, the first African woman and the first environmentalist to receive it. When she heard the news, she planted a Nandi flame tree. She said that peace depends on protecting the environment, because conflicts often begin over scarce resources."],
["A global voice","Maathai spoke around the world about forests, climate change and the rights of women. She helped start the United Nations Billion Tree Campaign in 2006, wrote books including her memoir Unbowed and became a UN Messenger of Peace. She also championed the protection of the Congo Basin forests."],
["Her legacy","Maathai died of cancer in Nairobi in 2011, at the age of 71. At her wish, she was not buried in a wooden coffin, to save trees, but in one made of bamboo, water hyacinth and papyrus. The Green Belt Movement continues her work, and Kenya and the world remember her each year on Wangari Maathai Day."]],
sp:["The hummingbird","Maathai often told a story. A great fire breaks out in a forest, and all the animals flee and watch helplessly. Only a tiny hummingbird flies to the stream again and again, carrying a drop of water in its beak to the fire. The other animals laugh and ask what it thinks it can do. The hummingbird answers: I am doing the best I can. Maathai said she wanted to be like the hummingbird."],
g:[["Deforestation","The cutting down of forests"],["Seedling","A young plant grown from a seed"],["Activist","A person who campaigns for change"],["Sustainable development","Progress that meets present needs without harming the future"]],
f:["Wangari Maathai was the first African woman to win the Nobel Peace Prize.","The Green Belt Movement has planted more than 50 million trees.","She was buried in a coffin made of bamboo, water hyacinth and papyrus."]},

{id:"achebe",t:"Chinua Achebe",i:"📚",w:"Chinua Achebe",a:["Chinua Achebe"],born:"16 November 1930, Ogidi, Nigeria",died:"21 March 2013, Boston, United States",nat:"Nigerian",known:"Writing Things Fall Apart, the most widely read African novel",r:["novel","nigeriacountry","decol"],
l:"Chinua Achebe was a Nigerian novelist, poet and critic, often called the father of modern African literature. His first novel, Things Fall Apart, published in 1958, tells the story of an Igbo community transformed by the arrival of British colonizers and missionaries. Translated into about 60 languages, it is the most widely read book in modern African literature. Achebe challenged the way Africa had been portrayed in European writing.",
d:[["1930","Born in Ogidi, eastern Nigeria"],["1953","Graduates from University College, Ibadan"],["1958","Publishes Things Fall Apart"],["1967–1970","Supports Biafra during the Nigerian Civil War"],["1975","Delivers his lecture criticizing Heart of Darkness"],["2007","Wins the Man Booker International Prize"],["2013","Dies in Boston"]],
s:[["Between two worlds","Albert Chinualumogu Achebe was born in 1930 in Ogidi, an Igbo town in eastern Nigeria, then a British colony. His parents were Christian converts, and his father taught for a missionary society, but the family still lived among traditional Igbo customs, festivals and stories. Achebe grew up at the crossroads of the two worlds he would later write about."],
["Education","Achebe was a gifted student and won a place at Government College in Umuahia, one of the best schools in Nigeria, and then at University College, Ibadan. He studied English, history and theology. Reading novels by Europeans about Africa, such as Joyce Cary’s Mister Johnson, he was struck by how they misrepresented Africans and decided to tell the story from the inside."],
["Things Fall Apart","Achebe’s first novel, published in 1958, tells of Okonkwo, a proud and powerful man in the Igbo village of Umuofia, and how his life and his community fall apart as British colonial officials and Christian missionaries arrive. The title comes from W. B. Yeats’s poem The Second Coming: Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold."],
["A new kind of story","Things Fall Apart showed Igbo society as complex and rich, with its own laws, religion, arts and debates, rather than as the savage backdrop of colonial novels. Achebe wrote in English but filled his prose with Igbo proverbs and storytelling. He famously wrote that proverbs are the palm oil with which words are eaten."],
["More novels","Achebe followed with No Longer at Ease in 1960, about Okonkwo’s grandson in modern Lagos, Arrow of God in 1964, about a priest under colonial rule, and A Man of the People in 1966, a satire of political corruption that seemed to predict Nigeria’s first military coup, which happened just as it was published."],
["Editor and teacher","As founding editor of the African Writers Series from 1962, Achebe helped publish more than 100 books by African writers, including Ngũgĩ wa Thiong’o and Steve Biko, making African literature available cheaply across the continent. He also worked for the Nigerian Broadcasting Corporation and taught at universities in Nigeria and the United States."],
["The Biafran War","In 1967 the Igbo-majority eastern region declared independence as Biafra, beginning the Nigerian Civil War. Achebe supported Biafra and traveled abroad to seek support for it. The war and a blockade caused a terrible famine before Biafra surrendered in 1970. Achebe wrote poems and later a memoir, There Was a Country, about the war."],
["Criticizing Conrad","In a famous 1975 lecture, Achebe argued that Joseph Conrad’s celebrated novel Heart of Darkness portrayed Africa as a place of savagery that existed only as a backdrop for Europeans, and called Conrad racist. The lecture caused fierce debate and changed how many readers and teachers approach colonial literature."],
["Later life","In 1990 a car accident left Achebe paralyzed from the waist down, and he spent his later years teaching in the United States, at Bard College and Brown University. He twice turned down Nigerian national honors in protest at the country’s political problems. In 2007 he won the Man Booker International Prize for his body of work."],
["His legacy","Achebe died in Boston in 2013 at 82. Things Fall Apart has sold more than 20 million copies and is taught in schools around the world. Nelson Mandela said that reading Achebe in prison brought the walls down. Generations of African writers, from Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie onward, describe him as their inspiration."]],
sp:["The African Writers Series","In 1962 the British publisher Heinemann launched the African Writers Series, with Achebe as its first editor, starting with a paperback edition of Things Fall Apart. The books, with distinctive orange covers, were cheap enough for students and readers across Africa. Over the following decades the series published hundreds of novels, plays and poems by writers from Nigeria, Kenya, South Africa, Senegal and many other countries."],
g:[["Igbo","A people and language of southeastern Nigeria"],["Colonialism","The rule of one country over another territory and its people"],["Proverb","A short traditional saying that expresses a truth"],["Satire","Writing that uses humor to criticize"]],
f:["Things Fall Apart has been translated into about 60 languages.","Its title comes from a poem by W. B. Yeats.","Achebe edited the African Writers Series, which published hundreds of African books."]},

{id:"garciamarquez",t:"Gabriel García Márquez",i:"🦋",w:"Gabriel García Márquez",a:["Gabriel García Márquez"],born:"6 March 1927, Aracataca, Colombia",died:"17 April 2014, Mexico City, Mexico",nat:"Colombian",known:"One Hundred Years of Solitude and magical realism; Nobel Prize in Literature, 1982",r:["novel","spanish","argentinacountry"],
l:"Gabriel García Márquez was a Colombian novelist, short-story writer and journalist, one of the most celebrated writers of the 20th century. His novel One Hundred Years of Solitude, published in 1967, tells the story of the Buendía family in the imaginary town of Macondo and made magical realism famous around the world. He won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1982. Affectionately called Gabo, he is a national hero in Colombia.",
d:[["1927","Born in Aracataca, Colombia"],["1948","Begins working as a journalist"],["1955","Publishes his first novel, Leaf Storm"],["1967","Publishes One Hundred Years of Solitude"],["1982","Wins the Nobel Prize in Literature"],["1985","Publishes Love in the Time of Cholera"],["2014","Dies in Mexico City"]],
s:[["Raised by grandparents","Gabriel García Márquez was born in 1927 in Aracataca, a small town near Colombia’s Caribbean coast. He spent his first years with his maternal grandparents. His grandfather, a veteran of a civil war, told him about battles and history, while his grandmother told ghost stories and tales of omens in a calm, matter-of-fact voice, which later shaped his style."],
["Journalist","García Márquez began studying law in Bogotá but left to become a journalist, writing for newspapers in Cartagena, Barranquilla and Bogotá. In 1955 he wrote a series of articles about a sailor who survived ten days adrift on a raft, revealing that the navy ship had been carrying smuggled goods, which angered the government. He was sent to Europe as a correspondent."],
["Early books","His first novel, Leaf Storm, appeared in 1955, followed by No One Writes to the Colonel and In Evil Hour, as well as short stories. They introduced the fictional town of Macondo, inspired by Aracataca. He married Mercedes Barcha in 1958, and they lived in Venezuela, Cuba, the United States and Mexico."],
["One Hundred Years of Solitude","In 1965, driving to Acapulco, García Márquez suddenly saw how to write the novel he had been planning for years. He shut himself away for about 18 months, while his wife pawned their belongings to keep the family going. Published in 1967, the book follows seven generations of the Buendía family and the rise and fall of Macondo."],
["Magical realism","In magical realism, amazing events are told as if they were completely ordinary. In One Hundred Years of Solitude, a girl rises to heaven while folding sheets, it rains for nearly five years, and yellow butterflies follow a lovesick man. Mixed with real history, such as civil wars and a massacre of banana workers, the magic helps show the strangeness of Latin American reality."],
["A global success","One Hundred Years of Solitude sold out its first printing within weeks and has since sold tens of millions of copies in dozens of languages. It became the best-known book of the Latin American Boom, a wave of writers in the 1960s and 1970s, including Julio Cortázar, Carlos Fuentes and Mario Vargas Llosa, who gained worldwide readers."],
["More great books","García Márquez went on to write The Autumn of the Patriarch, about a dictator, Chronicle of a Death Foretold, about a murder everyone knows will happen, Love in the Time of Cholera, a story of love lasting more than 50 years, and The General in His Labyrinth, about the last days of Simón Bolívar."],
["The Nobel Prize","In 1982 García Márquez won the Nobel Prize in Literature for novels and short stories in which the fantastic and the realistic are combined in a richly composed world of imagination. He accepted it wearing a white liqui liqui, a traditional Caribbean suit, rather than a tuxedo, and gave a speech about the solitude of Latin America."],
["Politics and journalism","García Márquez was a man of the left and a friend of Fidel Castro, which drew criticism, and he worked behind the scenes in peace talks between Colombia’s government and rebel groups. He never stopped being a journalist, founding a magazine and a foundation to train young Latin American journalists."],
["His legacy","García Márquez died in Mexico City in 2014 at the age of 87. Colombia declared three days of national mourning. His face appears on Colombia’s 50,000-peso note, and his work has inspired writers from Toni Morrison to Salman Rushdie. A television series of One Hundred Years of Solitude, filmed in Colombia, was released in 2024."]],
sp:["Macondo","Macondo, the town at the heart of One Hundred Years of Solitude, is founded by José Arcadio Buendía beside a river of clear water flowing over stones like prehistoric eggs. It grows, is visited by gypsies bringing ice and magnets, suffers civil wars and a banana boom, and is finally swept away by a whirlwind. The name came from a banana plantation García Márquez saw as a boy, and Macondo has become a symbol of Latin America itself."],
g:[["Magical realism","A style in which magical events are told as if they were ordinary"],["Novel","A long work of fiction"],["Latin American Boom","A wave of Latin American novelists who won worldwide fame in the 1960s and 1970s"],["Correspondent","A journalist who reports from a distant place"]],
f:["One Hundred Years of Solitude has sold tens of millions of copies.","García Márquez accepted his Nobel Prize wearing a traditional Caribbean suit.","He appears on Colombia’s 50,000-peso note."]},

{id:"tagore",t:"Rabindranath Tagore",i:"🪶",w:"Rabindranath Tagore",a:["Rabindranath Tagore"],born:"7 May 1861, Calcutta (now Kolkata), India",died:"7 August 1941, Calcutta",nat:"Indian (Bengali)",known:"Poetry and songs, and becoming the first non-European to win the Nobel Prize in Literature",r:["indiacountry","haikuart","gandhi"],
l:"Rabindranath Tagore was a Bengali poet, writer, composer, painter and educator from India. In 1913 he became the first non-European to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, for his collection of poems Gitanjali. He wrote thousands of songs, as well as novels, plays and essays, and founded a university at Santiniketan. His songs became the national anthems of both India and Bangladesh.",
d:[["1861","Born in Calcutta"],["1878","Travels to England to study"],["1901","Founds a school at Santiniketan"],["1910","Publishes Gitanjali in Bengali"],["1913","Wins the Nobel Prize in Literature"],["1921","Founds Visva-Bharati University"],["1941","Dies in Calcutta"]],
s:[["A remarkable family","Rabindranath Tagore was born in 1861 in Calcutta, the youngest of 13 surviving children in a wealthy, cultured family. His father, Debendranath, was a religious reformer, and his brothers and sisters included writers, musicians and the first Indian member of the elite Indian Civil Service. The family home was full of music, theater and debate."],
["A reluctant student","Tagore disliked the strict schools of his time and learned mostly at home, from tutors and his own reading. He wrote his first poems at about eight. At 17 he was sent to England to study law, but he left without a degree, having enjoyed English literature and music more than his classes."],
["Poet of Bengal","Tagore wrote in Bengali and transformed its literature, introducing new forms of poetry, short stories and prose written in everyday language. Managing his family’s estates in rural Bengal in the 1890s, he came to know village life closely, and his short stories about ordinary people are among the first great Bengali stories."],
["Gitanjali","In 1910 Tagore published Gitanjali, Song Offerings, a collection of devotional poems. On a visit to England in 1912 he translated some into English prose poems, which the Irish poet W. B. Yeats and others greatly admired. Published with an introduction by Yeats, the English Gitanjali made Tagore famous in the West."],
["The Nobel Prize","In 1913 Tagore won the Nobel Prize in Literature, the first person from outside Europe to win any Nobel Prize in literature. The committee praised his profoundly sensitive, fresh and beautiful verse. The award brought him worldwide fame, and he traveled and lectured in Europe, the Americas and Asia."],
["Songs","Tagore composed more than 2,000 songs, known as Rabindra Sangeet, which remain deeply loved in Bengal. He wrote both the words and the music. Jana Gana Mana became India’s national anthem, Amar Shonar Bangla became Bangladesh’s, and Sri Lanka’s anthem was written by one of his students and may have been inspired by his work."],
["Santiniketan","In 1901 Tagore founded a school at Santiniketan, west of Calcutta, where children learned outdoors under trees, close to nature, with art, music and drama as well as books. In 1921 it grew into Visva-Bharati University, which aimed to bring together the cultures of East and West. Among its students was the filmmaker Satyajit Ray."],
["A voice against injustice","Tagore supported Indian independence but feared narrow nationalism. In 1919, after British troops massacred hundreds of unarmed people at Amritsar, he gave up the knighthood the British had given him in protest. He was a friend and sometimes critic of Gandhi, whom he is said to have called Mahatma, great soul."],
["Painter","In his late sixties, Tagore began to paint, and he produced more than 2,000 paintings and drawings, often bold, mysterious figures and faces. Exhibitions of his work were held in Paris, London, Berlin, Moscow and New York, and he is considered a pioneer of modern Indian art."],
["His legacy","Tagore died in Calcutta in 1941 at the age of 80. He wrote about 50 volumes of poetry, as well as novels, plays and essays. Celebrated across South Asia, he is remembered as one of the greatest figures of modern Indian culture, a bridge between East and West, and his birthday is celebrated every year in India and Bangladesh."]],
sp:["Two national anthems","Rabindranath Tagore is the only person to have written the national anthems of two countries. Jana Gana Mana, first sung in 1911, was adopted as India’s national anthem in 1950. Amar Shonar Bangla, My Golden Bengal, written in 1905 when the British divided Bengal, became the national anthem of Bangladesh in 1972. Both are still sung daily in schools across the two countries."],
g:[["Bengali","A language spoken in Bangladesh and eastern India"],["Anthem","A song adopted as a symbol of a nation"],["Nobel Prize","An international award for achievement in literature, science or peace"],["Devotional","Expressing religious love and worship"]],
f:["Tagore wrote the national anthems of both India and Bangladesh.","He composed more than 2,000 songs.","He began painting in his late sixties and made more than 2,000 artworks."]},

{id:"murasaki",t:"Murasaki Shikibu",i:"🖌️",w:"Murasaki Shikibu",a:["Murasaki Shikibu"],born:"c. 973, Kyoto, Japan",died:"c. 1014 or later",nat:"Japanese",known:"Writing The Tale of Genji, often called the world’s first novel",r:["japan","japancountry","novel","haikuart"],
l:"Murasaki Shikibu was a Japanese writer and lady-in-waiting at the imperial court in the Heian period, around the year 1000. She wrote The Tale of Genji, a long story of the life and loves of a handsome prince, which is often called the world’s first novel and is considered the greatest masterpiece of Japanese literature. She also kept a diary and wrote poetry.",
d:[["c. 973","Born into the Fujiwara clan in Heian-kyō (Kyoto)"],["c. 998","Marries Fujiwara no Nobutaka"],["c. 1001","Her husband dies; she may begin writing Genji"],["c. 1005","Enters the service of Empress Shōshi"],["c. 1008","Keeps her court diary"],["c. 1014","Last known records of her life"]],
s:[["A scholar’s daughter","Murasaki Shikibu, whose real name is unknown, was born around 973 into a minor branch of the powerful Fujiwara family. Her father, Fujiwara no Tametoki, was a scholar and poet. While he taught her brother Chinese classics, she listened and learned faster, and her father reportedly said it was a pity she was not born a boy."],
["Women and writing","In Heian Japan, men of the court wrote mainly in Chinese, the language of government and scholarship. Women wrote in Japanese, using the new kana scripts. This meant that some of the greatest early Japanese literature, including diaries, poetry and stories, was written by women."],
["Marriage and loss","Around 998 she married Fujiwara no Nobutaka, an older official, and they had a daughter, who also became a poet. Her husband died around 1001, probably in an epidemic. Many scholars believe she began writing The Tale of Genji in her grief, sharing chapters with friends."],
["At court","Around 1005, her writing having become known, she was invited to serve Empress Shōshi, the young consort of Emperor Ichijō and daughter of the most powerful man at court, Fujiwara no Michinaga. She became a lady-in-waiting and tutor, secretly teaching the empress Chinese poetry. At court she was nicknamed Murasaki, after a character in her story."],
["The Tale of Genji","The Tale of Genji follows Genji, the brilliant son of an emperor who is made a commoner, through his many romances, political rise and fall, and old age, and then continues with the next generation. It has 54 chapters, more than 1,000 pages in translation, and about 400 characters, yet it explores their thoughts and feelings with great subtlety."],
["The sadness of things","A central idea in Genji is mono no aware, the gentle sadness felt at the passing of beautiful things, like falling cherry blossoms. The story is filled with poems, seasons, music, perfume and the elegant life of the court, but also with loneliness, jealousy and the knowledge that nothing lasts."],
["Her diary","Murasaki Shikibu also kept a diary describing court life in 1008 to 1010, including the birth of the empress’s son. It gives sharp, honest portraits of court ladies, including a famously critical one of the writer Sei Shōnagon, author of The Pillow Book. Her poems were collected and appear in imperial anthologies."],
["The first novel?","Because it tells a long, connected story centered on the inner lives of realistic characters, The Tale of Genji is often called the world’s first novel, written about 600 years before Don Quixote. Scholars debate the label, since longer stories existed earlier, but they agree it is one of the great works of world literature."],
["Readers through the ages","Genji was copied by hand for centuries and illustrated in famous painted scrolls in the 1100s. It shaped Japanese art, poetry, theater and design for a thousand years. It was translated into English by Arthur Waley in the 1920s and since then into dozens of languages, and it has inspired manga, films and an annual celebration in Kyoto."],
["Her legacy","Little is known of Murasaki Shikibu’s later life; she may have died around 1014 or later. Yet her book has outlived the court she described by a thousand years. Japan featured a scene from Genji and her portrait on the 2,000-yen note issued in 2000, and in 2024 her life was the subject of a major Japanese television drama."]],
sp:["The Genji scrolls","In the early 1100s, artists created illustrated handscrolls of The Tale of Genji, combining painted scenes with passages of elegant calligraphy. The scenes are viewed from above as if the roofs were removed, a style called blown-away roof, showing the characters inside their houses. About 20 scenes survive and are among Japan’s National Treasures, the oldest surviving illustrated Japanese narrative scrolls."],
g:[["Heian period","The era of Japanese history from 794 to 1185, with its capital at Heian-kyō, now Kyoto"],["Lady-in-waiting","A woman who serves and accompanies an empress or queen"],["Kana","Japanese syllable scripts"],["Mono no aware","The gentle sadness felt at the passing of things"]],
f:["The Tale of Genji has about 400 characters.","It was written about 600 years before Don Quixote.","Murasaki Shikibu’s real name is unknown."]},

{id:"pele",t:"Pelé",i:"⚽",w:"Pelé",a:["Pelé"],born:"23 October 1940, Três Corações, Brazil",died:"29 December 2022, São Paulo, Brazil",nat:"Brazilian",known:"Winning three World Cups and becoming football’s first global superstar",r:["soccer","brazilcountry","olymp"],
l:"Pelé, born Edson Arantes do Nascimento, was a Brazilian footballer widely regarded as one of the greatest players of all time. He is the only player to have won three FIFA World Cups, in 1958, 1962 and 1970, and he scored more than 1,000 goals in his career. Pelé became football’s first global superstar and an ambassador for the sport around the world.",
d:[["1940","Born in Três Corações, Minas Gerais"],["1956","Joins Santos at 15"],["1958","Wins the World Cup in Sweden at 17"],["1962","Wins a second World Cup"],["1969","Scores his 1,000th goal"],["1970","Wins a third World Cup in Mexico"],["1975","Joins the New York Cosmos"]],
s:[["A poor childhood","Edson Arantes do Nascimento was born in 1940 in Três Corações, in southeastern Brazil, and grew up in Bauru, in São Paulo state, in poverty. His father, Dondinho, was a footballer whose career was cut short by injury. Edson shined shoes to earn money and practiced with a sock stuffed with newspaper or a grapefruit instead of a ball."],
["The name Pelé","He got the nickname Pelé at school, perhaps from mispronouncing the name of a local goalkeeper, Bilé. At first he disliked it. Playing for local youth teams, he was spotted by a former Brazil player, Waldemar de Brito, who took him at 15 to Santos, a top club near São Paulo."],
["Santos","Pelé made his debut for Santos in 1956 and soon became its star, playing there for nearly 18 years. With dazzling skill, speed, power and an instinct for goal, he led Santos to many Brazilian titles and two Copa Libertadores and two Intercontinental Cup trophies. European clubs tried to buy him, but the Brazilian government declared him a national treasure to keep him home."],
["Sweden 1958","At 17, Pelé was the youngest player at the 1958 World Cup in Sweden. He scored a hat trick in the semifinal against France and two goals in the final against the hosts, including a famous one where he flicked the ball over a defender and volleyed it in. Brazil won its first World Cup, and Pelé wept on the goalkeeper’s shoulder."],
["Two more World Cups","Pelé was injured early in the 1962 World Cup, but Brazil won again. In 1966 in England he was fouled repeatedly and Brazil went out early. In 1970 in Mexico, in one of the greatest teams ever, Pelé scored in the final against Italy, a 4–1 win, and Brazil became the first country to win three World Cups."],
["The thousandth goal","On 19 November 1969, at the Maracanã Stadium in Rio de Janeiro, Pelé scored his 1,000th goal, by his count, from a penalty kick. The game stopped as fans and photographers flooded the field. He dedicated the goal to the children of Brazil. Counting friendly matches, he scored 1,279 goals in 1,363 games, a world record."],
["The beautiful game","Pelé called football o jogo bonito, the beautiful game, and helped popularize the phrase. He was known for sportsmanship and joy as well as skill. It is said that in 1969 a ceasefire was declared during the Nigerian Civil War so that people could watch him play in an exhibition match, though historians debate the story."],
["New York Cosmos","In 1975 Pelé came out of retirement to join the New York Cosmos in the North American Soccer League, helping to popularize football in the United States. He played his farewell match in 1977 between the Cosmos and Santos, playing one half for each team."],
["After football","After retiring, Pelé became a worldwide ambassador for football and for causes such as UNICEF. From 1995 to 1998 he served as Brazil’s minister of sport, pushing for a law to protect players’ rights. He also acted in films, including Escape to Victory in 1981."],
["His legacy","Pelé died in São Paulo in 2022 at the age of 82, and Brazil declared three days of mourning. FIFA named him a co-winner of its Player of the Century award in 2000. His name, a dictionary in Brazil now records, has become an adjective meaning someone exceptional, unique and incomparable, like Pelé."]],
sp:["Brazil 1970","The Brazil team that won the 1970 World Cup in Mexico, the first shown worldwide on color television, is often called the greatest football team ever. With Pelé, Jairzinho, who scored in every match, Rivelino, Tostão and captain Carlos Alberto, it played attacking, joyful football. Its fourth goal in the final against Italy, a flowing move ending with Carlos Alberto’s shot from Pelé’s pass, is one of the most celebrated goals in history."],
g:[["World Cup","The international football championship held every four years by FIFA"],["Hat trick","Three goals scored by one player in a match"],["Striker","An attacking player whose main job is to score goals"],["Exhibition match","A game played for show, not for a competition"]],
f:["Pelé is the only player to have won three World Cups.","He was 17 when he won his first World Cup.","He scored 1,279 goals in 1,363 games, including friendlies."]}
);

;
/* data/people11.js */
// People, part 11: lives of care and faith from Jamaica, Switzerland, Pakistan and South Africa.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"seacole",t:"Mary Seacole",i:"🩺",w:"Mary Seacole",a:["Mary Seacole"],born:"1805, Kingston, Jamaica",died:"14 May 1881, London, England",nat:"Jamaican-British",known:"Nursing soldiers in the Crimean War and running the British Hotel near the front",r:["nightingale","firstaid","pandemic"],
l:"Mary Seacole was a Jamaican-born businesswoman and healer who cared for soldiers during the Crimean War of the 1850s. Rejected when she offered to join the official nursing service, she traveled to Crimea at her own expense and set up the British Hotel near the front line, where she sold food and supplies and treated the sick and wounded, sometimes on the battlefield itself. Her memoir made her famous, and today she is honored as a pioneer of nursing.",
d:[["1805","Born in Kingston, Jamaica"],["1850","Treats cholera patients in Panama"],["1854","Travels to London to volunteer for the Crimean War"],["1855","Opens the British Hotel near Balaclava"],["1857","Publishes her autobiography"],["2016","A statue of her is unveiled in London"]],
s:[["A healer’s daughter","Mary Jane Grant was born in Kingston, Jamaica, then a British colony, in 1805. Her father was a Scottish soldier, and her mother was a free Jamaican woman who ran a boarding house for sick soldiers and sailors. Her mother was a doctress, a healer skilled in traditional Caribbean and African medicines, and Mary learned from her."],["Travels and trade","As a young woman, Mary traveled to the Bahamas, Haiti, Cuba and Britain, learning about different medicines and trading goods. In 1836 she married Edwin Horatio Seacole, who died in 1844. She took over the boarding house, and her skills became well known as she nursed patients through epidemics of cholera and yellow fever."],["Cholera in Panama","In 1850 Seacole went to Panama, where her brother ran a hotel, and arrived during a cholera outbreak. She treated many patients and even studied the body of a child who had died to learn more about the disease. She later ran businesses in Panama before returning to Jamaica, where she nursed victims of a yellow fever epidemic in 1853."],["Rejected","In 1854 Britain, France and the Ottoman Empire went to war with Russia in Crimea, on the Black Sea. Reports told of soldiers dying of disease in terrible conditions. Seacole traveled to London and offered to serve as a nurse, but the War Office and other groups turned her down. She believed prejudice against her color was part of the reason."],["Going anyway","Undeterred, Seacole decided to go at her own expense. With a relative of her late husband, Thomas Day, she set up a business and sailed to Crimea in 1855. On the way she visited the hospital at Scutari run by Florence Nightingale, where she was given a bed for the night."],["The British Hotel","Near Balaclava, Seacole built the British Hotel, using salvaged materials. It was a store, restaurant and place of rest for officers and soldiers, selling food, drink and supplies. From it she gave out medicines and care, and soldiers called her Mother Seacole."],["On the battlefield","Seacole often rode out to the battlefield with bags of bandages and medicines, treating wounded soldiers of both sides, sometimes under fire. She was among the first women to enter Sevastopol after it fell in 1855. The war correspondent William Howard Russell wrote of her as a warm and successful physician."],["Hard times","When the war ended suddenly in 1856, Seacole was left with unsold stock and debts, and she returned to London bankrupt. Soldiers and newspapers rallied to help her, and in 1857 a four-day festival in her honor was held in London, raising money for her."],["Her book","In 1857 Seacole published her autobiography, Wonderful Adventures of Mrs. Seacole in Many Lands, one of the first autobiographies by a Black woman published in Britain. It sold well and made her famous. She spent her later years between Jamaica and London and died in London in 1881."],["Remembered","After her death, Seacole was largely forgotten for about a century. From the 1970s, nurses, historians and Caribbean communities in Britain revived her story. In 2004 she was voted the greatest Black Briton in a public poll, and in 2016 a statue of her was unveiled outside St Thomas’ Hospital in London."]],
sp:["Nightingale and Seacole","Florence Nightingale and Mary Seacole both cared for soldiers in the Crimean War, but in different ways. Nightingale ran hospitals in Scutari, in present-day Istanbul, far from the fighting, and transformed nursing into a profession. Seacole worked near the front, as a businesswoman and healer. Today both are celebrated, and historians continue to discuss their very different contributions."],
g:[["Doctress","A traditional healer in the Caribbean"],["Crimean War","A war from 1853 to 1856 between Russia and an alliance including Britain, France and the Ottoman Empire"],["Cholera","A disease spread through dirty water"],["Autobiography","A book a person writes about their own life"]],
f:["Soldiers called Mary Seacole Mother Seacole.","She traveled to the Crimean War at her own expense.","In 2004 she was voted the greatest Black Briton."]},

{id:"dunant",t:"Henry Dunant",i:"⛑️",w:"Henry Dunant",a:["Henry Dunant"],born:"8 May 1828, Geneva, Switzerland",died:"30 October 1910, Heiden, Switzerland",nat:"Swiss",known:"Founding the Red Cross and inspiring the Geneva Conventions; first Nobel Peace Prize, 1901",r:["firstaid","hrights","un"],
l:"Henry Dunant was a Swiss businessman and humanitarian whose experience of a battlefield led to the founding of the Red Cross. After seeing thousands of wounded soldiers left without care at the Battle of Solferino in 1859, he wrote a book calling for volunteer relief societies and an international agreement to protect the wounded. His ideas led to the International Committee of the Red Cross and the first Geneva Convention. In 1901 he shared the first Nobel Peace Prize.",
d:[["1828","Born in Geneva"],["1859","Witnesses the aftermath of the Battle of Solferino"],["1862","Publishes A Memory of Solferino"],["1863","Helps found the International Committee of the Red Cross"],["1864","First Geneva Convention signed"],["1901","Shares the first Nobel Peace Prize"]],
s:[["A caring family","Jean-Henri Dunant was born in Geneva in 1828 into a religious and charitable family. His parents helped orphans, the poor and prisoners, and as a young man he visited prisons and helped found the Geneva branch of the Young Men’s Christian Association, the YMCA."],["A businessman in Algeria","Dunant trained as a banker and set up a company in French-ruled Algeria to grow wheat and run a mill. When he could not get the rights to the land and water he needed, he decided to ask the French emperor, Napoleon III, in person. In 1859 he set off to find him in northern Italy, where the emperor was at war."],["Solferino","On 24 June 1859, the armies of France and Sardinia fought the Austrian army at Solferino, in northern Italy. In about 15 hours of fighting, around 40,000 soldiers were killed or wounded. Dunant arrived in the evening and found thousands of wounded men lying untreated in the heat, without water, food or doctors."],["Tutti fratelli","Dunant organized local people, mostly women, in the town of Castiglione to care for the wounded, giving them water, washing wounds and writing letters to families. He paid for supplies and persuaded the French to release captured Austrian doctors to help. The women helped soldiers of both sides, saying tutti fratelli, all are brothers."],["A Memory of Solferino","Back in Geneva, Dunant could not forget what he had seen. In 1862 he published A Memory of Solferino, describing the horrors of the battle and proposing that every country form volunteer relief societies, trained in peacetime to care for the wounded in war, and that nations sign an agreement to protect them. He sent the book to leaders across Europe."],["The Red Cross","In 1863 Dunant and four other citizens of Geneva formed the committee that became the International Committee of the Red Cross. Its emblem, a red cross on a white background, reversed the colors of the Swiss flag. The next year, 12 countries signed the first Geneva Convention, agreeing to protect wounded soldiers and those who cared for them."],["Ruin","Dunant neglected his business in Algeria, and in 1867 it collapsed. He was declared bankrupt, and the scandal forced him to leave the Red Cross committee and Geneva. He lived for years in poverty, wandering through Europe, and was almost forgotten."],["Rediscovered","In 1887 Dunant settled in the small Swiss town of Heiden, and in 1892 he moved into its hospital. In 1895 a journalist found him there and wrote about him, and the story spread across Europe. Dunant received messages and support from around the world."],["The first Nobel Peace Prize","In 1901 Dunant shared the first Nobel Peace Prize with the French peace campaigner Frédéric Passy. Dunant did not spend the prize money on himself; he left much of it to charities in Switzerland and Norway. He died in Heiden in 1910."],["The movement today","The International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement now has national societies in almost every country, with millions of volunteers. They provide first aid, disaster relief, blood donation and help for refugees and prisoners of war. Dunant’s birthday, 8 May, is celebrated as World Red Cross and Red Crescent Day."]],
sp:["Three emblems","The red cross, red crescent and red crystal are the protective emblems of the movement. They mark medical workers, hospitals and ambulances in war, and attacking them is a war crime. The red crescent was first used by the Ottoman Empire in the 1870s and is used today in many Muslim-majority countries. The red crystal, a red square standing on its point, was adopted in 2005 as an emblem with no religious or national meaning."],
g:[["Humanitarian","A person who works to improve the lives of others and reduce suffering"],["Geneva Conventions","International treaties protecting people in war"],["Emblem","A symbol that represents a group or idea"],["Relief","Help given to people in need"]],
f:["Henry Dunant shared the first Nobel Peace Prize in 1901.","The Red Cross emblem reverses the colors of the Swiss flag.","World Red Cross and Red Crescent Day is held on his birthday, 8 May."]},

{id:"edhi",t:"Abdul Sattar Edhi",i:"🚑",w:"Abdul Sattar Edhi",a:["Abdul Sattar Edhi"],born:"28 February 1928, Bantva, India",died:"8 July 2016, Karachi, Pakistan",nat:"Pakistani",known:"Founding the Edhi Foundation, which runs one of the world’s largest volunteer ambulance networks",r:["pakistancountry","teresa","firstaid"],
l:"Abdul Sattar Edhi was a Pakistani humanitarian who founded the Edhi Foundation, a charity that runs ambulances, shelters, orphanages, clinics and homes for people in need across Pakistan. Living simply and helping anyone regardless of religion, ethnicity or background, he became known as the Angel of Mercy. His ambulance service grew into one of the largest volunteer ambulance networks in the world.",
d:[["1928","Born in Bantva, Gujarat, in British India"],["1947","Moves to Karachi after the partition of India"],["1951","Opens a free dispensary in Karachi"],["1957","Uses donations to buy his first ambulance during a flu epidemic"],["1997","Edhi ambulance service listed by Guinness as the largest volunteer ambulance network"],["2016","Dies in Karachi and receives a state funeral"]],
s:[["A mother’s lessons","Abdul Sattar Edhi was born in 1928 in Bantva, a town in Gujarat, in British India. His mother gave him two coins each day for school, one for himself and one to give to someone in need. When he was 11, she became paralyzed and mentally ill, and he cared for her until her death when he was 19. The experience shaped his life."],["To Karachi","After the partition of India in 1947, Edhi’s family moved to Karachi, in the new country of Pakistan. He worked as a street peddler and cloth seller. Seeing that the poor had no one to care for them, he decided to devote his life to helping them."],["A free dispensary","In 1951 Edhi opened a small free dispensary in Karachi, where he slept on a bench outside so that he could help anyone at any hour. He learned basic nursing and persuaded a doctor to help. He collected donations by standing on street corners, a practice he continued all his life."],["The first ambulance","During an influenza epidemic in Karachi in 1957, Edhi set up tents to give out medicine. Donations poured in, and he bought his first ambulance, an old van that he drove himself. Over the following decades, the Edhi ambulance network grew to well over a thousand vehicles across Pakistan, and it later added air ambulances."],["Bilquis Edhi","In 1966 Edhi married Bilquis, a nurse at his dispensary. She became his partner in the work, running maternity homes and orphanages and arranging adoptions. Together they cared for tens of thousands of abandoned children. Bilquis Edhi received many honors for her own work and died in 2022."],["The cradles","Outside Edhi centers, the foundation placed cradles with a sign saying do not kill, leave the baby in the cradle. Parents who could not care for a child could leave the baby safely there, with no questions asked. Thousands of babies were saved this way and raised in Edhi homes or adopted."],["Help for everyone","The Edhi Foundation helps the homeless, the mentally ill, drug users, runaway children, abused women and victims of disasters. It washes and buries unclaimed bodies, which others refused to touch. Edhi insisted on helping people of every religion and background, saying that humanity was his religion."],["A simple life","Edhi lived in a small room beside his office and owned only two sets of simple clothes. He refused government funding and large donations that came with conditions, relying on gifts from ordinary people. When asked why, he said that he wanted the work to belong to the people."],["Beyond Pakistan","The Edhi Foundation also sent help abroad, for example to people affected by war and disasters in neighboring countries, and Edhi donated to relief after Hurricane Katrina in the United States in 2005. Edhi received many awards, including the Ramon Magsaysay Award in 1986 and the Lenin Peace Prize, and he was nominated several times for the Nobel Peace Prize."],["His legacy","Edhi died in Karachi in 2016 at the age of 88, and was given a state funeral. In keeping with his wishes, he was buried in the clothes he died in, and his eyes were donated, though his other organs were too damaged by illness. His son Faisal Edhi and other family members continue to run the foundation."]],
sp:["Humanity is my religion","When Edhi was criticized for helping people of other faiths, he answered that his religion was humanitarianism, which is the basis of all religions. Edhi ambulances picked up anyone who needed help, whether Muslim, Hindu or Christian, rich or poor, and the foundation did not ask questions before helping. This simple rule earned him trust across Pakistan’s many communities."],
g:[["Dispensary","A place where medicines and basic treatment are given"],["Partition","The division of British India into India and Pakistan in 1947"],["Orphanage","A home for children whose parents cannot care for them"],["State funeral","A public funeral organized by the government to honor someone"]],
f:["The Edhi ambulance network is one of the largest volunteer ambulance services in the world.","Edhi owned only two sets of clothes.","He was buried in the clothes he died in, as he wished."]},

{id:"tutu",t:"Desmond Tutu",i:"🕊️",w:"Desmond Tutu",a:["Desmond Tutu"],born:"7 October 1931, Klerksdorp, South Africa",died:"26 December 2021, Cape Town, South Africa",nat:"South African",known:"Opposing apartheid, chairing the Truth and Reconciliation Commission; Nobel Peace Prize, 1984",r:["mandela","southafricacountry","hrights","gandhi"],
l:"Desmond Tutu was a South African Anglican archbishop and one of the world’s best-known campaigners for human rights. He used his position in the church to oppose apartheid, South Africa’s system of racial segregation, through nonviolent means, and won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1984. After apartheid ended, he chaired the Truth and Reconciliation Commission, which helped the country face the crimes of the past. He called South Africa the Rainbow Nation.",
d:[["1931","Born in Klerksdorp, South Africa"],["1961","Ordained as an Anglican priest"],["1978","Becomes general secretary of the South African Council of Churches"],["1984","Wins the Nobel Peace Prize"],["1986","Becomes the first Black Archbishop of Cape Town"],["1996","Begins chairing the Truth and Reconciliation Commission"]],
s:[["Early life","Desmond Mpilo Tutu was born in 1931 in Klerksdorp, a mining town west of Johannesburg. His father was a teacher and his mother a cook and cleaner. As a boy he caught tuberculosis and spent many months in hospital, where he was visited by the white Anglican priest Trevor Huddleston, an opponent of racism, who greatly influenced him."],["Teacher and priest","Tutu trained as a teacher, but in 1957 he resigned in protest against the Bantu Education Act, which gave Black children an inferior education. He studied theology, became a priest in 1961 and studied in London. In 1975 he became the first Black Dean of Johannesburg."],["A voice against apartheid","Under apartheid, the white minority government denied Black South Africans the vote, forced them to live in separate areas and imprisoned leaders such as Nelson Mandela. After the Soweto uprising of 1976, when police shot students protesting against being taught in Afrikaans, Tutu spoke out ever more strongly."],["Church leader","As general secretary of the South African Council of Churches from 1978, Tutu called for an end to apartheid and for other countries to apply economic sanctions on South Africa. The government took away his passport for a time, but he refused to be silenced and insisted on nonviolence."],["The Nobel Prize","In 1984 Tutu was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize for his role as a unifying leader in the nonviolent campaign against apartheid. The prize brought worldwide attention to the struggle. In 1986 he became the first Black Archbishop of Cape Town, head of the Anglican Church in southern Africa."],["Marching for peace","Tutu led marches and protests and often stepped in to calm angry crowds and protect people from violence. In 1989 he helped lead a march of tens of thousands through Cape Town. When Nelson Mandela was released from prison in 1990, he spent his first night of freedom at Tutu’s home."],["The Rainbow Nation","In 1994 South Africa held its first election in which all races could vote, and Mandela became president. Tutu described the new South Africa as a Rainbow Nation, in which people of all colors could live together in peace, an image that became widely used."],["Truth and Reconciliation","From 1996 Tutu chaired the Truth and Reconciliation Commission, which heard testimony from thousands of victims of apartheid-era violence. People who confessed fully to political crimes could apply for amnesty. Tutu sometimes wept during the hearings. The commission became a model for other countries recovering from conflict."],["A wider mission","After retiring as archbishop in 1996, Tutu campaigned around the world on issues such as poverty, HIV and AIDS, climate change and the rights of gay and lesbian people. In 2007 he helped Nelson Mandela found The Elders, a group of senior figures who work for peace and human rights, and became its first chair. He was known for his laughter, dancing and humor."],["His legacy","Tutu died in Cape Town on 26 December 2021 at the age of 90. At his request, his funeral was simple, with an inexpensive coffin, and his body was aquamated, an environmentally friendly alternative to cremation. He is remembered as the moral conscience of South Africa, a man who never stopped speaking out against injustice wherever he saw it."]],
sp:["Ubuntu","Tutu often explained the African idea of ubuntu, found in the Nguni languages of southern Africa. It means that a person is a person through other people: our humanity is bound up with the humanity of others. He believed that ubuntu made forgiveness possible, because harming others also harms oneself, and that it was the foundation for healing South Africa after apartheid."],
g:[["Apartheid","South Africa’s system of racial segregation from 1948 to the early 1990s"],["Archbishop","A senior bishop who leads a church in a large region"],["Reconciliation","Restoring friendly relations after a conflict"],["Amnesty","An official pardon for crimes"]],
f:["Desmond Tutu won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1984.","Nelson Mandela spent his first night of freedom at Tutu’s home.","Tutu coined the phrase Rainbow Nation for South Africa."]}
);

;
/* data/people12.js */
// People, part 12: founders of world religions and great thinkers of Asia and Europe.
// Religious figures are described by what historians know and by what believers hold, without taking sides.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"buddha",t:"The Buddha",i:"☸️",w:"The Buddha",a:["Siddhartha Gautama","Shakyamuni"],born:"c. 563 BCE or c. 480 BCE (dates debated), Lumbini, in present-day Nepal",died:"c. 483 BCE or c. 400 BCE, Kushinagar, India",nat:"Shakya clan, ancient India",known:"Founding Buddhism and teaching the Middle Way and the Four Noble Truths",r:["buddh","indianphil","indus","ashoka"],
l:"The Buddha, born Siddhartha Gautama, was a spiritual teacher in ancient India whose teachings became Buddhism, one of the world’s major religions with roughly 500 million followers today. According to Buddhist tradition, he was a prince who gave up his comfortable life to seek an end to suffering, reached enlightenment while meditating under a tree and spent the rest of his life teaching. Scholars agree he was a real person, though they debate his exact dates, placing his life in either the 500s or the 400s BCE.",
d:[["c. 563 or 480 BCE","Born in Lumbini, according to tradition"],["Age 29","Leaves his palace to seek the end of suffering"],["Age 35","Reaches enlightenment at Bodh Gaya"],["Soon after","Gives his first sermon at Sarnath"],["c. 483 or 400 BCE","Dies at Kushinagar, aged about 80"],["c. 250 BCE","Emperor Ashoka spreads Buddhism across India and beyond"]],
s:[["A prince is born","According to tradition, Siddhartha Gautama was born in the garden of Lumbini, in what is now southern Nepal, to Suddhodana, a ruler of the Shakya clan, and Queen Maya. A wise man predicted he would become either a great king or a great spiritual teacher. Wanting a king, his father raised him in luxury and shielded him from hardship."],["The four sights","Buddhist tradition says that as a young man, Siddhartha left the palace and saw an old man, a sick man and a dead body, and learned that everyone faces old age, sickness and death. He then saw a calm wandering holy man. Troubled, at about age 29 he left his wife, Yasodhara, and young son, Rahula, to search for the cause of suffering."],["Searching","For about six years Siddhartha studied with teachers and practiced extreme self-denial, eating so little he nearly died. Concluding that neither luxury nor harsh self-punishment led to truth, he chose a balanced path between them, which Buddhists call the Middle Way."],["Enlightenment","At Bodh Gaya, in northeastern India, Siddhartha sat beneath a fig tree, later called the Bodhi tree, and vowed to meditate until he understood the truth. Buddhists believe that there he reached enlightenment, or awakening, and became the Buddha, meaning the awakened one, at about age 35."],["The first sermon","In a deer park at Sarnath, near Varanasi, the Buddha gave his first teaching to five former companions. He taught the Four Noble Truths: life involves suffering; suffering is caused by craving; suffering can end; and the way to end it is the Noble Eightfold Path of right understanding, speech, action, effort, mindfulness and more."],["Teaching for 45 years","The Buddha traveled across the plains of the Ganges River teaching people of all backgrounds, rich and poor, rejecting the idea that birth determined a person’s spiritual worth. He founded the Sangha, a community of monks, and later, according to tradition, of nuns as well."],["His death","At about 80, the Buddha fell ill at Kushinagar. Tradition records his last words as advising his followers that all things change and to strive on with diligence. Buddhists call his death parinirvana, a final release from the cycle of rebirth. His remains were divided and placed in monuments called stupas."],["How Buddhism spread","The Buddha’s teachings were passed down orally and later written in languages such as Pali and Sanskrit. Emperor Ashoka of India promoted Buddhism in the 200s BCE. It spread to Sri Lanka, Southeast Asia, China, Korea, Japan and Tibet, developing into traditions such as Theravada and Mahayana."],["Buddhism in Southeast Asia","Buddhism is the main religion of Thailand, Myanmar, Cambodia, Laos and Sri Lanka and is widely practiced in Vietnam and Singapore. In the Philippines, Buddhism is followed mainly by some Chinese Filipinos, and Hindu-Buddhist influences from ancient trade appear in finds such as the Golden Tara of Agusan."],["What historians know","The earliest written records of the Buddha’s life come from centuries after his death, so historians separate likely facts from later legends. Most scholars accept that he was a real teacher from the Shakya clan who lived in northern India and taught a path to end suffering. Older sources date his death to about 483 BCE; many modern scholars prefer about 400 BCE."]],
sp:["The Bodhi tree","The fig tree under which the Buddha is said to have been enlightened became sacred to Buddhists. According to tradition, a cutting from it was taken to Sri Lanka in the 200s BCE by Sanghamitta, daughter of Emperor Ashoka, and planted at Anuradhapura. That tree, the Jaya Sri Maha Bodhi, is still alive and is often described as the oldest human-planted tree with a known planting date. A descendant of the tree grows today at the Mahabodhi Temple in Bodh Gaya, which pilgrims visit from around the world."],
g:[["Enlightenment","In Buddhism, awakening to the truth about life and freedom from suffering"],["Middle Way","A balanced path between luxury and extreme self-denial"],["Sangha","The community of Buddhist monks and nuns"],["Stupa","A dome-shaped monument holding sacred remains"]],
f:["Buddha means the awakened one.","He gave his first sermon at Sarnath.","Scholars debate whether he lived in the 500s or 400s BCE."]},

{id:"jesus",t:"Jesus",i:"✝️",w:"Jesus",a:["Jesus of Nazareth","Jesus Christ","Isa (in Islam)"],born:"c. 6–4 BCE, Bethlehem (according to the Gospels), Judea",died:"c. 30–33 CE, Jerusalem",nat:"Jewish, from Galilee in the Roman Empire",known:"The central figure of Christianity; his teachings of love and forgiveness",r:["christ","judaism","rome","islam"],
l:"Jesus of Nazareth was a Jewish preacher and teacher in Roman-ruled Judea and Galilee in the early first century CE. He taught love of God and neighbor, forgiveness and care for the poor, gathered followers and was crucified in Jerusalem on the orders of the Roman governor Pontius Pilate. Christians believe he is the Son of God and the Messiah, or Christ, and that he rose from the dead; Christianity, based on his life and teachings, is the world’s largest religion, with about 2.4 billion followers. Muslims honor him as a prophet, and historians almost universally agree he was a real person.",
d:[["c. 6–4 BCE","Born, according to the Gospels, in Bethlehem"],["c. 28–30 CE","Baptized by John the Baptist; begins his ministry in Galilee"],["c. 30–33 CE","Enters Jerusalem before the Passover festival"],["c. 30–33 CE","Crucified under Pontius Pilate"],["c. 65–100 CE","The four Gospels are written"],["380 CE","Christianity becomes the official religion of the Roman Empire"]],
s:[["Birth and childhood","The Gospels of Matthew and Luke say Jesus was born in Bethlehem to Mary, who was engaged to Joseph, a carpenter, and that he was raised in Nazareth, a village in Galilee. Christians believe his birth was miraculous. Little is recorded of his childhood except a story of the 12-year-old Jesus discussing scripture with teachers in the Temple in Jerusalem."],["His world","Jesus lived in a land ruled by the Roman Empire, where Jewish people practiced their faith, studied scripture and worshipped at the Temple in Jerusalem. Many hoped for a Messiah, a leader sent by God. Jesus spoke Aramaic and was raised in the Jewish faith."],["Baptism and ministry","When he was about 30, Jesus was baptized by John the Baptist in the Jordan River. He then traveled through Galilee and Judea for perhaps one to three years, teaching in towns and countryside. He chose twelve close followers, the apostles, including Peter, and attracted many other men and women as disciples."],["Teachings","Jesus taught that people should love God and love their neighbors as themselves, forgive others, help the poor and outcast, and even love their enemies. In the Sermon on the Mount he blessed the poor, the meek and peacemakers. He taught through parables, such as the Good Samaritan and the Prodigal Son, stories with moral and spiritual lessons."],["Miracles","The Gospels describe Jesus healing the sick, giving sight to the blind, feeding thousands with a few loaves and fish and raising the dead. Christians understand these as signs of God’s power; historians note that many people of his time regarded him as a healer and wonder-worker."],["Arrest and crucifixion","Jesus went to Jerusalem for the Passover festival, where crowds welcomed him. His teachings and following worried some religious leaders and the Roman authorities. After a final meal with his disciples, the Last Supper, he was arrested, questioned and sentenced by Pontius Pilate to crucifixion, a Roman form of execution, and died on a cross."],["The resurrection","The Gospels say that on the third day after his death his tomb was found empty and that he appeared to his followers alive. Christians believe Jesus rose from the dead and celebrate this at Easter. This belief became the center of the Christian faith, which his followers, such as Peter and Paul, spread across the Roman world."],["Views of Jesus","Christians believe Jesus is the Son of God and Savior. In Islam, Jesus, called Isa, is a revered prophet and Messiah born of the Virgin Mary, but not divine, and Muslims believe he was not crucified. Judaism regards him as a Jewish teacher but not the Messiah. Many people of other faiths and none admire his moral teachings."],["The historical Jesus","The main sources for Jesus’s life are the four Gospels, written decades after his death, and the letters of Paul. Non-Christian writers, such as the Jewish historian Josephus and the Roman historian Tacitus, also mention him. Historians widely agree he was baptized by John and crucified under Pilate, while matters of faith, such as miracles and the resurrection, are beyond what history can prove or disprove."],["Jesus and the Philippines","Christianity came to the Philippines with the Spanish in the 1500s, and the first Mass was held in Limasawa in 1521. Today the Philippines is the largest Christian-majority country in Asia, with about 80 percent of people Catholic. Events in Jesus’s life are marked by festivals such as Simbang Gabi and Christmas, Holy Week with the Pabasa, and the procession of the Black Nazarene in Manila."]],
sp:["Why the years are counted from Jesus","In 525 CE a monk named Dionysius Exiguus created a system of counting years from the birth of Jesus, which he called Anno Domini, Latin for in the year of the Lord. The system spread across Europe and later the world. Ironically, historians think Dionysius miscalculated, and Jesus was probably born a few years before 1 CE, around 6 to 4 BCE, before the death of King Herod. Today many people use the neutral terms BCE and CE, before the Common Era and Common Era, for the same years."],
g:[["Messiah","A savior or leader sent by God; Christ comes from the Greek word for it"],["Apostle","One of the twelve close followers of Jesus"],["Parable","A short story that teaches a moral or spiritual lesson"],["Gospel","One of four books telling the life of Jesus; the word means good news"]],
f:["Christianity is the world’s largest religion.","Muslims honor Jesus as the prophet Isa.","Jesus was probably born around 6 to 4 BCE."]},

{id:"muhammad",t:"Muhammad",i:"☪️",w:"Muhammad",a:["The Prophet Muhammad","Muhammad ibn Abdullah"],born:"c. 570, Mecca, Arabia",died:"8 June 632, Medina, Arabia",nat:"Arab, of the Quraysh tribe",known:"The founder of Islam, regarded by Muslims as the last prophet of God",r:["islam","saudicountry","islamicart","middleeast"],
l:"Muhammad was an Arab religious, social and political leader who founded Islam. Muslims believe he was the final prophet sent by God, Allah in Arabic, and that the Quran was revealed to him by the angel Gabriel over 23 years. Born in Mecca around 570, he began preaching about 610, moved with his followers to Medina in 622 and, by his death in 632, had united much of the Arabian Peninsula under Islam. Today Islam has about 2 billion followers. Out of respect, Muslims traditionally do not make pictures of him and add a blessing when saying his name.",
d:[["c. 570","Born in Mecca"],["c. 595","Marries Khadijah"],["610","Receives his first revelation in the cave of Hira, according to Islamic belief"],["622","The Hijra: migrates to Medina; the Islamic calendar begins"],["630","Returns to Mecca with his followers"],["632","Gives his Farewell Sermon and dies in Medina"]],
s:[["Early life","Muhammad was born in Mecca, a trading town in western Arabia, into the Quraysh tribe. His father, Abdullah, died before he was born, and his mother, Aminah, died when he was about six. He was raised by his grandfather and then his uncle Abu Talib, and as a young man he worked in the caravan trade."],["Al-Amin","Muhammad became known for honesty and was called al-Amin, the trustworthy. At about 25 he married Khadijah, a wealthy widow and merchant who had employed him. They had several children, including Fatimah. Khadijah was his strong supporter for 25 years until her death."],["The first revelation","Muhammad often went to meditate in a cave called Hira on a mountain near Mecca. Muslims believe that in about 610, when he was around 40, the angel Gabriel appeared to him there and told him to recite the first verses of the Quran. Khadijah was the first to believe in his message."],["Preaching in Mecca","Muhammad taught that there is only one God and that people should worship God alone, pray, be generous to the poor and treat others justly. Mecca was then a center of worship of many gods, and powerful leaders opposed his message. His followers, some of them poor or enslaved, faced persecution, and some fled to Abyssinia, now Ethiopia, where its Christian king protected them."],["The Hijra","In 622, facing danger in Mecca, Muhammad and his followers migrated to the town of Yathrib, later called Medina, the city of the Prophet, where he had been invited to settle disputes. This migration, the Hijra, marks the start of the Islamic calendar. In Medina, Muhammad became a religious and political leader and set out rules for the community, known as the Constitution of Medina."],["Conflict and return to Mecca","Muhammad’s community in Medina fought several battles with the Quraysh of Mecca, including Badr in 624 and Uhud in 625. In 628 the two sides agreed to the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah. In 630 Muhammad entered Mecca with a large following almost without fighting, removed the idols from the Kaaba and pardoned many former enemies."],["Farewell and death","In 632 Muhammad made a pilgrimage to Mecca and gave his Farewell Sermon, urging equality among believers, the rights of women and fair dealing. He died in Medina later that year, in the house of his wife Aisha, and was buried there. The Prophet’s Mosque in Medina contains his tomb."],["The Quran and Sunnah","Muslims believe the Quran is the word of God revealed to Muhammad, and it was compiled into a book soon after his death. His sayings and actions, recorded in collections called hadith, form the Sunnah, his example, which guides Muslim life alongside the Quran."],["After Muhammad","After Muhammad’s death, his companion Abu Bakr became the first caliph, or successor. Disagreement over who should lead contributed to the later division between Sunni and Shia Muslims. Within a century, Islam had spread from Spain to Central Asia, and later, through traders and scholars, to Southeast Asia."],["Islam in the Philippines","Islam reached the southern Philippines through Arab, Malay and other traders and missionaries from the 1300s. Tradition says the missionary Karim ul-Makhdum built a mosque in Simunul, Tawi-Tawi, around 1380, the oldest in the country. Sultanates were founded in Sulu and Maguindanao. Today Muslims, about 6 percent of Filipinos, live mainly in Mindanao, and Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha are national holidays."]],
sp:["Why there are no pictures","Most Muslims believe that Muhammad, like other prophets, should not be shown in pictures, to prevent him from being worshipped instead of God and out of deep respect. Instead, Islamic art honors him with beautiful calligraphy of his name and with descriptions in words, called hilya, that tell of his appearance and character. Some Persian and Turkish manuscripts from past centuries did show him, often with his face veiled, but the tradition of not depicting him is widely observed today. For this reason, this encyclopedia shows calligraphy or a symbol for him rather than a portrait."],
g:[["Prophet","A person believed to bring messages from God"],["Quran","The holy book of Islam"],["Hijra","Muhammad’s migration from Mecca to Medina in 622"],["Caliph","A successor to Muhammad as leader of the Muslim community"]],
f:["The Islamic calendar begins with the Hijra of 622.","Muslims believe the Quran was revealed over 23 years.","The oldest mosque in the Philippines is in Simunul, Tawi-Tawi."]},

{id:"confucius",t:"Confucius",i:"📜",w:"Confucius",a:["Kong Qiu","Kongzi","Master Kong"],born:"551 BCE, Qufu, state of Lu, China",died:"479 BCE, Qufu, state of Lu, China",nat:"Chinese",known:"Teaching ethics, family loyalty and good government; the founder of Confucianism",r:["chinesephil","china","ethics","education"],
l:"Confucius was a Chinese teacher and philosopher whose ideas about morality, family, education and good government have shaped East Asian societies for more than 2,000 years. Living in a time of war and disorder, he taught that a peaceful society depends on people being kind, respectful and honest, and on rulers governing by good example. His sayings, collected by his students in the Analects, became the basis of Confucianism, which influenced China, Korea, Japan, Vietnam and Chinese communities worldwide.",
d:[["551 BCE","Born in the state of Lu"],["c. 530 BCE","Begins teaching students"],["c. 500 BCE","Serves as an official in Lu"],["c. 497 BCE","Leaves Lu and travels for about 14 years"],["479 BCE","Dies in Qufu"],["136 BCE","Han Emperor Wu makes Confucian learning the basis of government"]],
s:[["A humble start","Confucius, known in Chinese as Kongzi, Master Kong, was born in 551 BCE in the state of Lu, in present-day Shandong province. His father, an old soldier, died when he was three, and he grew up poor. He loved learning and studied history, poetry, music and ceremonies while working as a clerk and a manager of granaries and herds."],["Times of trouble","Confucius lived in the Spring and Autumn period, when China was divided into rival states and the power of the Zhou kings had faded. Wars, betrayals and greedy rulers were common. Confucius looked back to the early Zhou dynasty as a golden age and sought to restore order through virtue."],["A great teacher","Confucius began teaching young men in his 20s or 30s and is said to have had about 3,000 students over his life. He accepted students regardless of wealth, saying he would teach anyone who brought even a small gift. He is often called China’s first professional teacher."],["Official and wanderer","Around 500 BCE, Confucius served as an official in Lu, possibly as minister of justice. Disappointed by its rulers, he left at about 55 and traveled for some 14 years among other states, hoping a ruler would put his ideas into practice. None did, and he returned home to teach and edit classic texts until his death in 479 BCE."],["Ren and li","Two key ideas in Confucius’s teaching are ren, often translated as humaneness or kindness, and li, proper conduct, ritual and good manners. He taught a version of the Golden Rule: do not do to others what you would not want done to yourself."],["Family and filial piety","Confucius taught that society is built on good relationships, such as those between ruler and subject, parent and child, husband and wife, older and younger siblings, and friends. Filial piety, respect and care for parents and ancestors, was central. These values remain important in many Asian families, including Filipino ones."],["Ruling by example","Confucius believed rulers should govern by virtue rather than by force or harsh laws. A good ruler, he said, is like the North Star, around which other stars turn. He also taught that officials should be chosen for their ability and character, not their birth."],["The Analects","Confucius did not write a book of his own teachings. After his death, his students and their followers collected his sayings and conversations in the Analects. It opens with the line: Is it not a joy to learn and to practice what you have learned? It became one of the most influential books in history."],["Confucianism through the ages","The Han dynasty made Confucian learning the basis of government in the 100s BCE. From 605 CE until 1905, Chinese officials were chosen through examinations based on Confucian classics. Confucian ideas spread to Korea, Japan and Vietnam. They were attacked in China during the Cultural Revolution of the 1960s and 1970s but have since been revived."],["Praise and criticism","Admirers credit Confucianism with valuing education, family, social harmony and good government. Critics argue that it encouraged rigid hierarchy, obedience to authority and the inferior position of women. Debates about Confucius’s legacy continue in China and elsewhere."]],
sp:["Teachers’ Day","Confucius is honored as the greatest teacher in Chinese history. In Taiwan, his traditional birthday, 28 September, is celebrated as Teachers’ Day, with ceremonies at Confucian temples featuring music, dance and offerings. His hometown of Qufu contains a large temple, the mansion of his descendants and a cemetery where he and many of his descendants are buried; together they are a UNESCO World Heritage Site. His family tree, kept for over 80 generations, is considered one of the longest in the world."],
g:[["Ren","Humaneness or kindness, a key Confucian virtue"],["Li","Proper conduct, ritual and good manners"],["Filial piety","Respect and care for parents and ancestors"],["Analects","The collection of Confucius’s sayings"]],
f:["Confucius was born in 551 BCE.","His sayings were collected by students in the Analects.","Chinese officials were chosen by Confucian exams until 1905."]},

{id:"laozi",t:"Laozi",i:"☯️",w:"Laozi",a:["Lao-tzu","Lao Tse","Old Master"],born:"6th century BCE, according to tradition, state of Chu, China",died:"Unknown",nat:"Chinese",known:"The traditional author of the Daodejing and founder of Taoism",r:["tao","chinesephil","china","confucius"],
l:"Laozi, whose name means Old Master, is the legendary Chinese philosopher traditionally credited with writing the Daodejing, the founding text of Taoism. According to tradition, he lived in the 500s BCE and was an older contemporary of Confucius. Very little is known about him for certain, and many historians think he may be a legendary figure or that the Daodejing was compiled by several authors over time. Whatever its origins, the Daodejing, with its teachings about the Dao, or the Way, simplicity and harmony with nature, is one of the most translated books in the world.",
d:[["500s BCE","Laozi lives, according to tradition"],["c. 300 BCE","Oldest known version of the Daodejing, the Guodian bamboo slips, is written"],["c. 100 BCE","Sima Qian writes the earliest biography of Laozi"],["100s CE","Laozi is worshipped as a god in religious Taoism"],["1973","Silk copies of the Daodejing are found at Mawangdui"],["1993","The Guodian bamboo slips are discovered"]],
s:[["Who was Laozi?","The earliest account of Laozi comes from the historian Sima Qian, writing around 100 BCE, centuries after Laozi’s supposed life. Sima Qian said Laozi’s name was Li Er, that he was born in the state of Chu and that he served as a keeper of archives at the court of the Zhou kings. But Sima Qian himself admitted uncertainty and mentioned other possible identities."],["Meeting Confucius","A famous story says the young Confucius visited Laozi to ask about rituals. Laozi advised him to give up his pride and ambitions. Confucius was said to have told his students that Laozi was like a dragon, impossible to fully understand. Historians doubt the meeting happened, but it shows how the two traditions were compared."],["Leaving the world","According to legend, Laozi, saddened by the decline of the Zhou dynasty, left China riding a water buffalo, heading west. At the border pass, the gatekeeper Yinxi recognized him as a sage and asked him to write down his wisdom. Laozi wrote the Daodejing, about 5,000 Chinese characters long, then departed and was never seen again."],["The Daodejing","The Daodejing, also called the Laozi or Tao Te Ching, has 81 short chapters of poetic, puzzling wisdom. Its title means the Classic of the Way and Virtue. It opens by saying that the Dao that can be spoken of is not the eternal Dao, suggesting that the deepest truth cannot be put into words."],["The Dao","The Dao, or the Way, is the natural force or order behind everything in the universe. Laozi taught that people should live in harmony with the Dao, flowing like water, which is soft yet can wear away the hardest rock."],["Wu wei","A key idea of the Daodejing is wu wei, often translated as effortless action or non-action. It means acting naturally and without forcing things, rather than doing nothing. The text suggests that the best rulers govern lightly, so that people barely notice them."],["Simplicity and balance","The Daodejing praises simplicity, humility and contentment, warning that greed and ambition cause trouble. It sees opposites, such as hard and soft or strong and weak, as connected, an idea symbolized later by yin and yang. The text values the empty space of a bowl or room, which makes it useful."],["Laozi as a god","In religious Taoism, which developed from the 100s CE, Laozi came to be worshipped as a god, Taishang Laojun, the Supreme Old Lord, believed to be an embodiment of the Dao. Taoist temples across China, Taiwan, Southeast Asia and the Philippines honor him."],["Ancient manuscripts","Archaeologists have found early copies of the Daodejing. In 1973, silk manuscripts from about 200 BCE were discovered in a tomb at Mawangdui, and in 1993 bamboo slips from about 300 BCE were found at Guodian. These finds show the text existed by then, though in somewhat different forms, supporting the idea that it developed over time."],["Worldwide influence","The Daodejing has been translated into many languages, more than almost any other book apart from religious scriptures. Its ideas have influenced Chinese art, poetry, medicine and martial arts such as tai chi, as well as Zen Buddhism, and writers, thinkers and leaders around the world."]],
sp:["The water teacher","The Daodejing often uses water as an example of the Dao. Water, it says, benefits all things without competing with them and flows to low places that others avoid. Nothing in the world is softer or weaker than water, yet nothing is better at overcoming the hard and strong. Through this image, the text teaches that gentleness, patience and humility can be more powerful than force, a lesson that has inspired readers for more than 2,000 years."],
g:[["Dao","The Way, the natural order of the universe"],["Wu wei","Effortless action, acting without forcing"],["Daodejing","The Classic of the Way and Virtue, Taoism’s key text"],["Sage","A wise person"]],
f:["Laozi means Old Master.","The Daodejing has 81 short chapters.","Historians debate whether Laozi was a real person."]},

{id:"socrates",t:"Socrates",i:"🏛️",w:"Socrates",a:["Socrates of Athens"],born:"c. 470 BCE, Athens, Greece",died:"399 BCE, Athens, Greece",nat:"Athenian (Greek)",known:"Founding Western philosophy through questioning; the Socratic method",r:["greece","ethics","logic","democ"],
l:"Socrates was a philosopher of ancient Athens who is regarded as one of the founders of Western philosophy. He wrote nothing himself, but spent his life questioning people in the streets and marketplace about justice, courage, goodness and how to live well, showing them that they knew less than they thought. His method of teaching through questions, the Socratic method, is still used today. In 399 BCE an Athenian jury sentenced him to death, and he died by drinking poison. We know him mainly through the writings of his students, especially Plato.",
d:[["c. 470 BCE","Born in Athens"],["432–429 BCE","Fights as a soldier at Potidaea"],["423 BCE","Mocked in Aristophanes’ comedy The Clouds"],["406 BCE","Opposes an illegal mass trial of generals"],["399 BCE","Tried, sentenced to death and dies by drinking hemlock"],["c. 387 BCE","His student Plato founds the Academy"]],
s:[["An ordinary Athenian","Socrates was born around 470 BCE in Athens, the son of a stonemason and a midwife. He was famous for being unattractive, with a snub nose and bulging eyes, for walking barefoot and for caring little about money or comfort. He married Xanthippe and had three sons."],["Soldier and citizen","Socrates served as a foot soldier in the Peloponnesian War between Athens and Sparta and was praised for his bravery and endurance. As a citizen, he stood up for the law even when unpopular, refusing to take part in illegal acts ordered both by the Athenian assembly and by the Thirty Tyrants who briefly ruled the city."],["The oracle","According to Plato, a friend asked the Oracle of Delphi whether anyone was wiser than Socrates, and the oracle said no. Puzzled, Socrates questioned politicians, poets and craftsmen and found that they believed they knew things they did not. He concluded that he was wiser only because he knew that he did not know."],["The Socratic method","Socrates taught by asking questions. He would ask someone to define an idea, such as courage or justice, then ask more questions that revealed problems with the answer, leading both to think more deeply. This method of questioning and examining ideas is still used in classrooms and law schools."],["The examined life","Socrates believed the most important thing was to care for one’s soul by seeking wisdom and virtue. He taught that no one does wrong knowingly and that wrongdoing comes from ignorance. Plato records him saying that the unexamined life is not worth living."],["The trial","In 399 BCE, Socrates was charged with impiety, not believing in the city’s gods and introducing new ones, and with corrupting the youth of Athens. Some of his former associates had been involved in an antidemocratic government, which may have made him unpopular. A jury of 500 citizens found him guilty by a narrow margin."],["His defense","Plato’s Apology records Socrates’ speech in his defense. Rather than beg for mercy, he insisted that he had served Athens like a gadfly, a stinging insect, waking the city up with his questions. When asked to propose a penalty, he suggested he be given free meals as a public benefactor. The jury sentenced him to death."],["Death","Socrates spent a month in prison awaiting execution. His friend Crito offered to help him escape, but Socrates refused, arguing that he must obey the laws of Athens. Surrounded by friends, he calmly drank a cup of hemlock, a poisonous plant, and died. Plato’s Phaedo describes his last day."],["Plato and Xenophon","Socrates wrote nothing, so we know him through others, especially his student Plato, who wrote dialogues in which Socrates is the main speaker, and the soldier and writer Xenophon. Because Plato also used Socrates to voice his own ideas, scholars debate how much of the Socrates of the dialogues reflects the real man. This puzzle is called the Socratic problem."],["Legacy","Through Plato, and Plato’s student Aristotle, Socrates shaped Western philosophy so much that thinkers before him are called the pre-Socratics. His willingness to question everything, his concern with how to live and his death for his principles have made him a symbol of free thought and integrity."]],
sp:["The gadfly of Athens","At his trial, Socrates compared Athens to a large, noble horse that had grown lazy and needed to be stirred into life, and himself to a gadfly that God had attached to the city to sting it awake. All day long, he said, he went about urging, persuading and criticizing people. He warned the jurors that if they killed him, they would not easily find another like him. His image of the philosopher as a gadfly, an annoying but useful critic of society, is still used today."],
g:[["Philosophy","The study of basic questions about knowledge, life and right and wrong; the word means love of wisdom"],["Socratic method","Teaching and learning by asking and answering questions"],["Hemlock","A poisonous plant used for executions in ancient Athens"],["Dialogue","A written conversation between characters"]],
f:["Socrates wrote nothing himself.","He was sentenced to death in 399 BCE.","Thinkers before him are called pre-Socratics."]}
);

