// People, part 13: a philosopher, a composer and four leaders of the 20th century.
// Political leaders are described by what they did, with the main praise and criticism historians give.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"kant",t:"Immanuel Kant",i:"🧠",w:"Immanuel Kant",a:["Immanuel Kant"],born:"22 April 1724, Königsberg, Prussia (now Kaliningrad, Russia)",died:"12 February 1804, Königsberg, Prussia",nat:"Prussian (German)",known:"Critique of Pure Reason, the categorical imperative and ideas for lasting peace",r:["enlightenment","ethics","logic","germanycountry"],
l:"Immanuel Kant was a German philosopher of the Enlightenment and one of the most influential thinkers in history. In works such as the Critique of Pure Reason, he asked what humans can know and argued that our minds actively shape how we experience the world. In ethics, he taught that we should act only on principles we could want everyone to follow and should treat people always as ends in themselves, never merely as tools. His ideas about human dignity, freedom and peace between nations helped inspire modern human rights and international organizations.",
d:[["1724","Born in Königsberg"],["1755","Proposes that the solar system formed from a cloud of gas"],["1770","Becomes professor of logic and metaphysics"],["1781","Publishes the Critique of Pure Reason"],["1785","Publishes the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals"],["1795","Publishes Perpetual Peace"]],
s:[["A craftsman’s son","Kant was born in 1724 in Königsberg, a port city in Prussia, the fourth of nine children of a harness maker. His family belonged to Pietism, a strict and devout Protestant movement. A bright student, he entered the University of Königsberg at 16 and studied philosophy, mathematics and science."],["A quiet life","Kant spent almost his whole life in and around Königsberg and never traveled far from it. He worked as a private tutor and lecturer for years before becoming a professor in 1770. He never married. He was a popular teacher and a sociable dinner host who enjoyed conversation."],["The famous walks","Kant followed a strict daily routine. A well-known story says he took his afternoon walk at exactly the same time each day, so regularly that neighbors could set their clocks by him. The path he walked was later called the Philosopher’s Walk."],["Early science","Before his famous philosophy, Kant wrote about science. In 1755 he proposed that the solar system formed from a spinning cloud of gas, an idea later developed by Pierre-Simon Laplace and known as the nebular hypothesis, a forerunner of modern theories of how stars and planets form. He also suggested that some fuzzy patches in the sky were distant galaxies."],["The Critique of Pure Reason","In 1781, at 57, Kant published the Critique of Pure Reason, one of the most important and difficult books in philosophy. He tried to combine two rival views: that knowledge comes from reason, and that it comes from experience. He argued that while knowledge begins with experience, the mind supplies basic forms, such as space, time and cause and effect, that organize what we perceive."],["What we cannot know","Kant argued that we can know things only as they appear to us, not as they are in themselves. Questions about God, the soul and free will cannot be settled by reason alone, he said, though we may hold beliefs about them for moral reasons. He called his change in thinking a Copernican revolution in philosophy."],["The categorical imperative","Kant’s ethics center on duty and reason rather than consequences. His categorical imperative says: act only according to a rule you could want to become a universal law for everyone. For example, lying is wrong because if everyone lied, no one could trust anyone. He also said we must treat every person as an end in themselves, never merely as a means."],["Dare to know","In a 1784 essay, What Is Enlightenment?, Kant described enlightenment as humanity’s emergence from immaturity, the inability to think for oneself. He urged people: dare to know, have the courage to use your own understanding. The essay became a motto of the Enlightenment."],["Perpetual peace","In 1795 Kant wrote Perpetual Peace, proposing that nations could end war by becoming republics with citizens’ rights, forming a federation of free states and respecting the rights of visitors. His ideas helped inspire the League of Nations, the United Nations and the European Union."],["Influence and criticism","Kant influenced nearly all later Western philosophy, as well as law, human rights and political thought. Critics have challenged his strict ethics, such as his view that lying is always wrong, and in recent years scholars have examined racist and sexist views in some of his writings, debating how they relate to his universal moral ideas."]],
sp:["Kant and the murderer at the door","Kant’s belief that lying is always wrong led to a famous puzzle. Suppose a murderer comes to your door asking where your friend is hiding. Should you lie to save your friend? In a 1797 essay, Kant argued that even then, lying is wrong. Many readers find this conclusion hard to accept, and the example is still debated in ethics classes around the world as a test of whether moral rules can ever have exceptions."],
g:[["Categorical imperative","Kant’s rule to act only on principles everyone could follow"],["Enlightenment","An 18th-century movement emphasizing reason and freedom"],["Metaphysics","The study of the basic nature of reality"],["Federation","A union of states with a shared government or agreement"]],
f:["Kant never traveled far from Königsberg.","He published the Critique of Pure Reason at 57.","His Perpetual Peace inspired the idea of the United Nations."]},

{id:"bach",t:"Johann Sebastian Bach",i:"🎹",w:"Johann Sebastian Bach",a:["J. S. Bach"],born:"31 March 1685, Eisenach, Saxe-Eisenach (Germany)",died:"28 July 1750, Leipzig, Saxony (Germany)",nat:"German",known:"Composing the Brandenburg Concertos, The Well-Tempered Clavier and the St Matthew Passion",r:["baroque","musicw","beethoven","mozart"],
l:"Johann Sebastian Bach was a German composer and organist of the Baroque era, widely regarded as one of the greatest composers in history. He wrote more than 1,000 works, including organ and keyboard music such as The Well-Tempered Clavier, orchestral works such as the Brandenburg Concertos, and sacred choral music such as the St Matthew Passion and the Mass in B minor. Celebrated in his lifetime mainly as an organist, Bach became famous as a composer after his death, and his mastery of harmony and counterpoint has influenced musicians ever since.",
d:[["1685","Born in Eisenach"],["1705","Walks about 400 km to Lübeck to hear the organist Buxtehude"],["1721","Presents the Brandenburg Concertos"],["1723","Becomes music director in Leipzig"],["1727","The St Matthew Passion is first performed"],["1750","Dies in Leipzig"]],
s:[["A musical family","Bach was born in 1685 in Eisenach, in central Germany, into a family of musicians going back generations. His father taught him the violin. Both his parents died by the time he was ten, and he went to live with his older brother Johann Christoph, an organist, who taught him keyboard."],["A young organist","As a teenager, Bach sang in a church choir in Lüneburg and studied music. At 18 he became an organist in Arnstadt. In 1705 he took leave and walked about 400 kilometers to Lübeck to hear the famous organist Dietrich Buxtehude, staying months longer than allowed, which angered his employers."],["Weimar","From 1708 Bach worked at the court of the Duke of Weimar, where he wrote much of his great organ music, such as toccatas and fugues. When he asked to leave for a better job in 1717, the duke had him jailed for about a month before releasing him."],["Köthen and the concertos","At the court of Prince Leopold of Köthen, from 1717 to 1723, Bach wrote instrumental music, including the six Brandenburg Concertos, presented in 1721 to a nobleman, the cello suites and works for solo violin. His first wife, Maria Barbara, died in 1720, and in 1721 he married Anna Magdalena, a singer."],["Leipzig","In 1723 Bach became Thomaskantor in Leipzig, responsible for music at the city’s main churches and teaching at the St Thomas School. He held this demanding job for 27 years, writing a new cantata, a work for choir and orchestra, for many Sundays, and often clashing with city officials."],["Sacred masterpieces","In Leipzig, Bach wrote his greatest religious works: the St John Passion and St Matthew Passion, telling the story of the death of Jesus, the Christmas Oratorio and the Mass in B minor. A devout Lutheran, he often wrote the initials SDG, Soli Deo Gloria, glory to God alone, on his scores."],["The art of counterpoint","Bach was a master of counterpoint, combining several independent melodies so they fit together in harmony. His fugues, in which a theme is passed between voices, are especially admired. The Well-Tempered Clavier, two sets of 24 preludes and fugues in every major and minor key, became essential study for keyboard players."],["Family life","Bach had 20 children with his two wives, though only ten survived to adulthood. Several sons became famous composers, including Carl Philipp Emanuel Bach and Johann Christian Bach, who influenced the young Mozart. His home was full of music, with family members copying scores and performing."],["Later years","In his last years, Bach wrote complex works such as the Goldberg Variations, the Musical Offering for King Frederick the Great of Prussia and the unfinished Art of Fugue. His eyesight failed, and after unsuccessful eye operations he died in Leipzig in 1750."],["Rediscovery and legacy","After his death, Bach’s music was seen as old-fashioned, though composers such as Mozart and Beethoven studied it. In 1829 the young composer Felix Mendelssohn conducted the St Matthew Passion in Berlin, sparking a Bach revival. Today his music is performed worldwide, from concert halls to jazz and pop arrangements, and is studied by every serious musician."]],
sp:["Bach in space","In 1977, NASA launched the Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 spacecraft, each carrying a gold-plated record of sounds and music from Earth in case they were ever found by intelligent life. Bach is the composer with the most pieces on the Golden Record, three in total, including the first movement of Brandenburg Concerto No. 2. The scientist Lewis Thomas joked that sending all of Bach would be the best message, though it would be boasting. Voyager 1 is now in interstellar space, carrying Bach’s music farther than any other human creation."],
g:[["Counterpoint","Combining two or more independent melodies"],["Fugue","A piece in which a theme is repeated and developed by several voices"],["Cantata","A work for voices and instruments, often religious"],["Organist","A musician who plays the pipe organ"]],
f:["Bach wrote more than 1,000 works.","He had 20 children.","His music is on the Voyager Golden Record."]},

{id:"churchill",t:"Winston Churchill",i:"🎩",w:"Winston Churchill",a:["Sir Winston Churchill"],born:"30 November 1874, Blenheim Palace, England",died:"24 January 1965, London, England",nat:"British",known:"Leading Britain as prime minister during World War II",r:["ww2","ukcountry","wwi","coldwar"],
l:"Winston Churchill was a British statesman, soldier and writer who served as prime minister of the United Kingdom from 1940 to 1945 and from 1951 to 1955. He is best known for leading Britain during World War II, when his speeches and refusal to make peace with Nazi Germany rallied the British people at a time when Britain stood almost alone. He also won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1953. Many regard him as one of the greatest wartime leaders; others criticize his defense of the British Empire, his role in the Gallipoli campaign and his government’s response to the Bengal famine of 1943.",
d:[["1874","Born at Blenheim Palace"],["1899","Escapes from a prisoner-of-war camp in the Boer War"],["1915","The Gallipoli campaign he championed fails"],["1940","Becomes prime minister"],["1946","Gives the Iron Curtain speech"],["1953","Wins the Nobel Prize in Literature"]],
s:[["An aristocratic childhood","Winston Leonard Spencer Churchill was born in 1874 at Blenheim Palace, the home of his grandfather, the Duke of Marlborough. His father, Lord Randolph Churchill, was a politician, and his mother, Jennie Jerome, was American. He did poorly at school but loved history and the English language."],["Soldier and journalist","Churchill trained at the Royal Military College, Sandhurst, and served in Cuba, India, Sudan and South Africa, often working as a war correspondent. In 1899, during the Boer War, he was captured by the Boers but escaped, traveling hundreds of kilometers to safety, which made him famous."],["Early politics","Churchill was elected to Parliament in 1900. He switched from the Conservative to the Liberal Party in 1904 and back again in 1924. As a Liberal minister, he helped introduce reforms such as labor exchanges and unemployment insurance. In 1911 he became First Lord of the Admiralty, in charge of the navy."],["World War I","In World War I, Churchill backed the 1915 campaign to seize the Dardanelles and Gallipoli in Turkey, hoping to knock the Ottoman Empire out of the war. It failed with heavy losses among British, Australian, New Zealand, French and Ottoman troops. Churchill resigned from the Admiralty and served as an officer on the Western Front before returning to government."],["The wilderness years","In the 1930s, out of government, Churchill warned repeatedly about the danger of Nazi Germany and urged Britain to rearm, while the government pursued appeasement. He also strongly opposed self-rule for India. Many considered his career over."],["Their finest hour","In May 1940, as Germany invaded France, Churchill became prime minister of a coalition government. He refused to negotiate with Hitler and inspired Britain with speeches promising to fight on the beaches and calling the Battle of Britain the nation’s finest hour. Britain held out against German air attacks."],["Leading the Allies","Churchill built a close partnership with US President Franklin D. Roosevelt and allied with the Soviet leader Joseph Stalin after Germany invaded the Soviet Union in 1941. The three leaders met at conferences such as Tehran and Yalta to plan the war and the postwar world. Germany surrendered in May 1945."],["Defeat and return","Despite his wartime popularity, Churchill’s Conservative Party lost the 1945 general election to the Labour Party, as voters chose its program of social reform. In 1946 he warned of an Iron Curtain dividing Europe, a key moment at the start of the Cold War. He returned as prime minister from 1951 to 1955."],["Writer and painter","Churchill wrote many books, including a six-volume history of World War II and A History of the English-Speaking Peoples, and won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1953. He was also a keen painter, producing more than 500 paintings. He died in 1965 and was given a state funeral."],["A debated legacy","Supporters see Churchill as the leader who saved Britain and helped save Europe from Nazi tyranny. Critics point to his opposition to Indian independence, his views on race and empire, his handling of strikes and Irish affairs, and the Bengal famine of 1943, in which about 3 million people died while his government gave low priority to food relief; historians debate how much responsibility he bears. These debates continue in Britain, India and elsewhere."]],
sp:["Escape from Pretoria","In November 1899, the 24-year-old war correspondent Winston Churchill was captured when Boer forces ambushed an armored train in South Africa. Held in a prisoner-of-war camp in Pretoria, he climbed over a wall one night and escaped alone. With a price on his head, he jumped onto a freight train, hid in a coal mine with the help of a British mine manager and finally escaped to Portuguese East Africa, now Mozambique, hidden among bales of wool. His adventure made him a national hero and helped launch his political career."],
g:[["Prime minister","The head of government in the United Kingdom and many other countries"],["Appeasement","Giving in to demands to avoid conflict"],["Coalition","A government formed by several parties"],["Iron Curtain","The divide between communist Eastern Europe and the West"]],
f:["Churchill won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1953.","His mother was American.","He produced more than 500 paintings."]},

{id:"fdr",t:"Franklin D. Roosevelt",i:"🎙️",w:"Franklin D. Roosevelt",a:["FDR","Franklin Delano Roosevelt"],born:"30 January 1882, Hyde Park, New York, USA",died:"12 April 1945, Warm Springs, Georgia, USA",nat:"American",known:"Leading the United States through the Great Depression with the New Deal and through World War II",r:["depression","ww2","usacountry","commonwealth"],
l:"Franklin Delano Roosevelt, known as FDR, was the 32nd president of the United States, serving from 1933 until his death in 1945, the only president elected four times. He led the country through the Great Depression with the New Deal, a series of programs that created jobs, regulated banks and began Social Security, and through most of World War II. He did this while partly paralyzed by an illness he contracted in 1921. Supporters credit him with saving American democracy and helping defeat fascism; critics argue the New Deal expanded government too much, and his order to imprison Japanese Americans in 1942 is widely condemned.",
d:[["1882","Born in Hyde Park, New York"],["1921","Struck by a paralytic illness, diagnosed as polio"],["1933","Becomes president and launches the New Deal"],["1934","Signs the Tydings–McDuffie Act, setting a path to Philippine independence"],["1941","Leads the US into World War II after Pearl Harbor"],["1945","Dies in office in Warm Springs, Georgia"]],
s:[["A privileged youth","Roosevelt was born in 1882 into a wealthy family in Hyde Park, New York, the only child of James and Sara Roosevelt. He was educated by tutors, then at Groton School, Harvard University and Columbia Law School. His fifth cousin, Theodore Roosevelt, was president from 1901 to 1909."],["Eleanor","In 1905 Franklin married Eleanor Roosevelt, Theodore’s niece and his distant cousin. They had six children, five of whom survived infancy. Eleanor became a powerful figure in her own right, traveling widely, campaigning for the poor, women and civil rights, and later helping write the Universal Declaration of Human Rights."],["Early career","Roosevelt was elected to the New York State Senate in 1910 and served as assistant secretary of the navy during World War I. In 1920 he ran unsuccessfully for vice president as a Democrat."],["Illness","In 1921, at 39, Roosevelt fell ill with a disease diagnosed as polio, though some doctors today think it may have been Guillain-Barré syndrome. He lost the use of his legs. He worked hard to recover, founded a treatment center at Warm Springs, Georgia, and learned to stand with leg braces. He used a wheelchair in private but rarely let the public see it."],["The Great Depression","After the stock market crash of 1929, the United States fell into the Great Depression, with about a quarter of workers unemployed by 1933. Roosevelt, elected governor of New York in 1928, won the presidency in 1932. In his first inaugural address, he declared that the only thing we have to fear is fear itself."],["The New Deal","In his first hundred days, Roosevelt and Congress passed laws to stabilize the banks and create jobs. New Deal programs built roads, dams, schools and parks, employed millions of young men in the Civilian Conservation Corps and brought electricity to rural areas. The Social Security Act of 1935 created pensions for the elderly and unemployment insurance."],["Fireside chats","Roosevelt spoke directly to Americans in radio broadcasts called fireside chats, explaining his policies in plain language. Millions listened at home, and the chats built public trust. He was one of the first leaders to use radio so effectively."],["World War II","As war spread in Europe and Asia, Roosevelt helped Britain with supplies through the Lend-Lease program. After Japan attacked Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941, calling it a date which will live in infamy, he led the United States into the war. He worked closely with Churchill and Stalin and helped plan the United Nations, a name he suggested."],["Roosevelt and the Philippines","In 1934 Roosevelt signed the Tydings–McDuffie Act, which created the Commonwealth of the Philippines in 1935 under President Manuel L. Quezon and promised independence after ten years. In July 1941 he placed Filipino and American forces under General Douglas MacArthur. During the Japanese occupation, Quezon’s government-in-exile worked from the United States. Promised benefits for Filipino veterans were later cut by the Rescission Act of 1946, a long-standing grievance."],["Death and legacy","Roosevelt was elected to a record fourth term in 1944 but died of a stroke in April 1945, weeks before Germany’s surrender. The 22nd Amendment, ratified in 1951, limited later presidents to two terms. Historians often rank him among the greatest presidents, while critics fault the New Deal’s cost and expansion of federal power, his attempt to enlarge the Supreme Court in 1937, slow action to help Jewish refugees and Executive Order 9066, which sent about 120,000 Japanese Americans to internment camps."]],
sp:["Hiding the wheelchair","Few Americans knew how severely Franklin Roosevelt was disabled. He used a wheelchair he designed from a kitchen chair, but in public he was usually photographed seated or standing with braces, leaning on an aide’s arm, and the press generally agreed not to photograph him being carried or in his wheelchair. Of thousands of photographs of him, only a handful show him in a wheelchair. Today, a statue at the Franklin Delano Roosevelt Memorial in Washington, D.C., added in 2001, shows him in his wheelchair, after disability rights campaigners urged that his disability be honestly portrayed."],
g:[["New Deal","Roosevelt’s programs to fight the Great Depression"],["Social Security","A government program providing pensions and support"],["Fireside chat","A radio talk by Roosevelt to the American people"],["Internment","Imprisoning people, often civilians, during wartime"]],
f:["FDR is the only US president elected four times.","He signed the act creating the Philippine Commonwealth.","He suggested the name United Nations."]},

{id:"lenin",t:"Vladimir Lenin",i:"🚩",w:"Vladimir Lenin",a:["Vladimir Ilyich Ulyanov"],born:"22 April 1870, Simbirsk, Russian Empire",died:"21 January 1924, Gorki, near Moscow, Soviet Union",nat:"Russian",known:"Leading the Bolshevik Revolution of 1917 and founding the Soviet state",r:["russrev","socialism","coldwar","ussrfall"],
l:"Vladimir Lenin was a Russian revolutionary and political theorist who led the Bolshevik party to power in the October Revolution of 1917 and founded the Soviet state, which in 1922 became the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics, the Soviet Union. He developed a version of Marxism, later called Leninism, in which a disciplined party of professional revolutionaries would lead the workers. Supporters saw him as a champion of workers and peasants against tsarist rule and capitalism; critics point to the one-party dictatorship, secret police and terror he established, which paved the way for Joseph Stalin’s rule.",
d:[["1870","Born in Simbirsk"],["1887","His brother Alexander is executed for plotting to kill the tsar"],["1903","Leads the Bolshevik faction of Russian Marxists"],["1917","Returns to Russia and leads the October Revolution"],["1922","The Soviet Union is founded"],["1924","Dies at Gorki"]],
s:[["Early life","Vladimir Ilyich Ulyanov was born in 1870 in Simbirsk, on the Volga River, into an educated family. His father was a school inspector. In 1887 his older brother, Alexander, was hanged for taking part in a plot to assassinate Tsar Alexander III, an event that deeply affected him."],["Becoming a revolutionary","Expelled from Kazan University for joining a student protest, Lenin studied law on his own and passed the exams. He became a follower of Karl Marx, who taught that workers would overthrow capitalism. In 1895 he was arrested for revolutionary activity and exiled to Siberia, where he married fellow revolutionary Nadezhda Krupskaya. He adopted the name Lenin around 1901."],["The Bolsheviks","Living mostly abroad in exile, Lenin argued in his 1902 pamphlet What Is to Be Done? that a small, disciplined party of professional revolutionaries must lead the working class. In 1903 the Russian Marxists split over this; Lenin’s faction became known as the Bolsheviks, meaning majority, and their rivals as the Mensheviks, minority."],["War and revolution","World War I brought heavy defeats and hunger to Russia. In February 1917, protests in Petrograd forced Tsar Nicholas II to abdicate, and a Provisional Government took over. Lenin, in Switzerland, was allowed by Germany to travel through its territory in a sealed train, as Germany hoped he would take Russia out of the war."],["The October Revolution","Back in Russia, Lenin demanded peace, land and bread and all power to the soviets, councils of workers and soldiers. On 7 November 1917, 25 October in the old Russian calendar, the Bolsheviks seized power in Petrograd in a nearly bloodless takeover. Lenin became head of the new government."],["Peace and civil war","In March 1918 Lenin agreed to the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk with Germany, giving up vast territories to end Russia’s part in the war. A brutal civil war followed from 1918 to about 1921 between the Bolsheviks’ Red Army and the anti-Bolshevik Whites, along with foreign forces. Millions died from fighting, famine and disease."],["Terror and dictatorship","Lenin’s government closed the elected Constituent Assembly in 1918, banned other parties and created the Cheka, a secret police. After an assassination attempt on Lenin in 1918, the government launched the Red Terror, executing thousands. Peasants’ grain was seized, contributing to the famine of 1921 and 1922 in which millions died."],["The New Economic Policy","In 1921, facing economic collapse and revolts, Lenin introduced the New Economic Policy, allowing small private businesses and farmers to sell their produce, while the state kept control of large industry. In 1922 the Soviet Union was formally founded, uniting Russia with Ukraine, Belarus and the Transcaucasian republics."],["Illness and death","Lenin suffered several strokes from 1922. In notes later called his Testament, he warned that Joseph Stalin was too rude and should be removed as party general secretary, but this advice was not followed. Lenin died in January 1924. His body was embalmed and placed in a mausoleum on Red Square in Moscow, where it remains on display."],["Legacy","Lenin’s ideas inspired communist movements and revolutions around the world in the 20th century, including in China, Vietnam and Cuba. Supporters praise his opposition to imperialism and inequality and his vision of a workers’ state. Critics argue that the one-party rule, repression and terror he introduced led directly to the abuses of Stalin’s era. After the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991, many statues of Lenin were taken down, though his tomb still draws visitors."]],
sp:["The sealed train","In April 1917, Lenin was living in exile in Zurich when he heard the tsar had fallen. Germany, at war with Russia, saw a chance to weaken its enemy and agreed to let Lenin and about 30 other revolutionaries travel across Germany in a train whose carriage was treated as sealed, so they would not mix with Germans. Lenin reached Petrograd’s Finland Station on 16 April and was greeted by crowds. Within seven months, he was ruling Russia, and one of his first acts was to seek peace with Germany."],
g:[["Bolshevik","A member of Lenin’s revolutionary party"],["Soviet","A council of workers and soldiers; also used for the Soviet Union"],["Marxism","The ideas of Karl Marx about class struggle and communism"],["Cheka","The first Soviet secret police"]],
f:["Lenin was born Vladimir Ilyich Ulyanov.","His embalmed body is on display in Moscow.","The Soviet Union was founded in 1922."]},

{id:"mao",t:"Mao Zedong",i:"⭐",w:"Mao Zedong",a:["Mao Tse-tung","Chairman Mao"],born:"26 December 1893, Shaoshan, Hunan, China",died:"9 September 1976, Beijing, China",nat:"Chinese",known:"Founding the People’s Republic of China in 1949 and leading it until 1976",r:["modernchina","socialism","china","coldwar"],
l:"Mao Zedong was a Chinese communist revolutionary who founded the People’s Republic of China in 1949 and led it as chairman of the Chinese Communist Party until his death in 1976. He led the Communists through the Long March and to victory in the civil war, ending a century of foreign domination and disorder. His rule brought land reform, wider literacy and rising life expectancy, but also the Great Leap Forward, which caused a famine in which tens of millions died, and the Cultural Revolution, a decade of violent upheaval. The Chinese Communist Party officially judges that his achievements outweigh his mistakes, while many historians emphasize the immense human cost of his policies.",
d:[["1893","Born in Shaoshan, Hunan"],["1921","Attends the founding congress of the Chinese Communist Party"],["1934–1935","Leads the Long March"],["1949","Proclaims the People’s Republic of China"],["1958–1962","The Great Leap Forward and famine"],["1966–1976","The Cultural Revolution"]],
s:[["Peasant roots","Mao was born in 1893 in the village of Shaoshan in Hunan province, the son of a prosperous peasant farmer. He clashed with his strict father and loved reading, especially stories of rebels and heroes. In 1911 he briefly served as a soldier in the revolution that overthrew the Qing dynasty."],["Becoming a communist","Mao trained as a teacher and worked as a library assistant at Peking University, where he encountered Marxism. In 1921 he was one of the delegates at the founding congress of the Chinese Communist Party in Shanghai. Unlike many communists who focused on factory workers, Mao believed China’s vast peasant population would lead the revolution."],["Civil war","In 1927 the Nationalist leader Chiang Kai-shek turned on his communist allies, killing thousands. Mao fled to the countryside and built a rural base and a Red Army, developing guerrilla tactics. Nationalist forces attacked repeatedly."],["The Long March","In 1934, surrounded by Nationalist armies, the Communists broke out and began the Long March, a retreat of some 10,000 kilometers across mountains, rivers and marshes to northwestern China. Of about 86,000 who set out from the main base, fewer than 10,000 arrived in 1935. During the march Mao rose to leadership of the party."],["War and victory","During the Japanese invasion from 1937 to 1945, the Communists and Nationalists formed an uneasy alliance, while the Communists expanded their base areas. After Japan’s defeat, civil war resumed. The Communists won, and on 1 October 1949, Mao proclaimed the People’s Republic of China in Beijing. Chiang’s government retreated to Taiwan."],["Early rule","The new government redistributed land from landlords to peasants, a process in which hundreds of thousands to millions of landlords were killed. It promoted literacy, women’s rights, including banning arranged marriages, and public health. It also suppressed opponents in mass campaigns. China entered the Korean War in 1950."],["The Great Leap Forward","In 1958 Mao launched the Great Leap Forward to rapidly industrialize China and overtake Western economies. Peasants were organized into huge communes, and millions were sent to make steel in backyard furnaces. Unrealistic targets, false reports and seizure of grain caused a catastrophic famine from 1959 to 1961. Historians estimate that between about 15 and 45 million people died, with many estimates around 30 million."],["The Cultural Revolution","In 1966, to restore his authority and revive revolutionary spirit, Mao launched the Cultural Revolution. Young Red Guards, carrying the Little Red Book of his quotations, attacked teachers, officials and anyone seen as old-fashioned or disloyal. Temples, books and artworks were destroyed, schools closed and millions were persecuted, imprisoned or sent to the countryside; hundreds of thousands to more than a million died."],["Later years","In 1972 Mao welcomed US President Richard Nixon to Beijing, a historic opening to the West. China had also gained the United Nations seat held by Taiwan in 1971. Mao’s health declined, and he died in September 1976. His body lies in a mausoleum in Tiananmen Square, and his portrait still hangs at the Gate of Heavenly Peace."],["Judging Mao","In 1981 the Chinese Communist Party declared that Mao had made serious mistakes, especially the Cultural Revolution, but that his contributions far outweighed them; Deng Xiaoping summarized this as 70 percent right and 30 percent wrong. Supporters credit Mao with unifying China, ending foreign domination and raising life expectancy from about 35 years in 1949 to about 65 by 1976. Critics, including many historians, hold him responsible for the deaths of tens of millions and for crushing freedom. His influence also reached communist movements elsewhere, including the Communist Party of the Philippines, founded in 1968 on Maoist lines."]],
sp:["The Little Red Book","Quotations from Chairman Mao Tse-tung, known in the West as the Little Red Book, is a pocket-sized collection of Mao’s sayings first published in 1964 for the army. During the Cultural Revolution, every citizen was expected to own and study it, and Red Guards waved it at rallies. Estimates of the number of copies printed run to over a billion, making it one of the most printed books in history. Its slogans, such as political power grows out of the barrel of a gun, became famous worldwide."],
g:[["Communism","A system aiming at shared ownership of property under a single party"],["Long March","The Communists’ 10,000-kilometer retreat of 1934–1935"],["Commune","A large collective farm and community"],["Red Guards","Young militants of the Cultural Revolution"]],
f:["Mao proclaimed the People’s Republic of China on 1 October 1949.","The Great Leap Forward caused a famine in which tens of millions died.","His body lies in a mausoleum in Tiananmen Square."]}
);

