// People, part 14: philosophers, a poet, two founding leaders and three great artists and writers.
// Leaders are described by what they did, with the main praise and criticism historians give.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"plato",t:"Plato",i:"🏛️",w:"Plato",a:["Plato of Athens"],born:"c. 428 BCE, Athens, Greece",died:"c. 348 BCE, Athens, Greece",nat:"Athenian (Greek)",known:"The Republic, the theory of Forms and founding the Academy",r:["socrates","greece","ethics","democ"],
l:"Plato was a philosopher of ancient Athens, a student of Socrates and the teacher of Aristotle, and one of the most influential thinkers in history. He wrote dialogues, conversations in which Socrates usually leads the discussion, exploring justice, love, knowledge, the soul and the ideal state. His theory of Forms held that the world we see is only a shadow of a perfect, unchanging reality. Around 387 BCE he founded the Academy in Athens, often called the first university in the Western world. A modern philosopher remarked that Western philosophy is a series of footnotes to Plato.",
d:[["c. 428 BCE","Born in Athens"],["399 BCE","His teacher Socrates is executed"],["c. 387 BCE","Founds the Academy"],["c. 375 BCE","Writes the Republic"],["367 BCE","Visits Syracuse to advise its ruler"],["c. 348 BCE","Dies in Athens"]],
s:[["A noble youth","Plato was born around 428 BCE into a wealthy and noble Athenian family during the Peloponnesian War. His real name may have been Aristocles; Plato, meaning broad, may have been a nickname. As a young man, he hoped for a career in politics and wrote poetry."],["Meeting Socrates","Plato became a follower of Socrates, who questioned people about how to live well. When Athens executed Socrates in 399 BCE, Plato was deeply shaken and lost faith in the politics of his city. He left Athens for some years and traveled, possibly to Egypt, Italy and Sicily."],["The dialogues","Plato wrote about 35 dialogues, which have all survived, a rare fortune for an ancient author. In most of them Socrates questions others, so it is often hard to tell where Socrates’ ideas end and Plato’s begin. Famous dialogues include the Apology, describing Socrates’ trial, the Symposium, about love, and the Phaedo, about the soul."],["The theory of Forms","Plato argued that beyond the changing world of the senses lies a world of perfect, eternal Forms, such as Beauty, Justice and Goodness, and the true form of a circle. Everything we see is an imperfect copy. True knowledge, he believed, comes from reason, not the senses."],["The Republic","In the Republic, his most famous work, Plato asks what justice is and imagines an ideal city ruled by philosopher-kings, wise rulers trained for decades, with guardians to protect it and workers to produce goods. He argued that people should do the work that suits their nature."],["The Allegory of the Cave","In the Republic, Plato imagines prisoners chained in a cave, seeing only shadows on a wall and believing them to be real. One prisoner escapes, sees the sunlit world and returns to tell the others, who mock him. The story represents the journey from ignorance to knowledge and the role of the philosopher."],["The Academy","Around 387 BCE, Plato founded the Academy in a grove outside Athens, where students studied philosophy, mathematics and astronomy. Legend says its entrance bore the words: let no one ignorant of geometry enter. Aristotle studied there for about 20 years. A school continued in Plato’s tradition for centuries."],["Adventures in Sicily","Plato traveled three times to Syracuse in Sicily, hoping to turn its rulers, Dionysius I and later Dionysius II, into philosopher-kings. The attempts failed badly. According to one story, Plato was even sold into slavery and had to be ransomed by a friend."],["Atlantis","In his dialogues Timaeus and Critias, Plato told the story of Atlantis, a powerful island civilization that sank into the sea in a single day. Most scholars believe Plato invented Atlantis as a lesson about pride and power, but the legend has inspired countless stories and searches."],["Legacy and criticism","Plato’s ideas shaped Christian, Jewish and Islamic thought, Renaissance art and modern philosophy, mathematics and politics. Some modern thinkers, such as Karl Popper, criticized the Republic for favoring rule by a small elite, strict censorship and control over citizens’ lives, seeing in it the roots of authoritarian ideas. Others defend it as a thought experiment about justice."]],
sp:["Footnotes to Plato","In 1929 the British philosopher Alfred North Whitehead wrote that the safest general characterization of the European philosophical tradition is that it consists of a series of footnotes to Plato. He did not mean that later thinkers simply repeated Plato, but that Plato had raised nearly all the great questions of philosophy, about knowledge, reality, beauty, justice, love and death, that later philosophers would continue to debate. The remark is still often quoted to show Plato’s enormous influence."],
g:[["Dialogue","A written conversation between characters"],["Forms","In Plato’s philosophy, perfect, unchanging ideas behind things we see"],["Philosopher-king","A wise ruler trained in philosophy"],["Allegory","A story with a hidden meaning"]],
f:["Plato was Socrates’ student and Aristotle’s teacher.","He founded the Academy around 387 BCE.","He told the story of Atlantis."]},

{id:"aristotle",t:"Aristotle",i:"📐",w:"Aristotle",a:["Aristotle of Stagira","The Philosopher"],born:"384 BCE, Stagira, Chalcidice, Greece",died:"322 BCE, Chalcis, Euboea, Greece",nat:"Greek",known:"Founding logic and biology and writing on ethics, politics and science",r:["plato","greece","logic","alexander"],
l:"Aristotle was an ancient Greek philosopher and scientist, a student of Plato and the tutor of Alexander the Great. He wrote on an astonishing range of subjects, including logic, biology, physics, astronomy, ethics, politics, poetry and rhetoric, and founded the systematic study of many of them. His school in Athens, the Lyceum, was a center of research. For nearly 2,000 years his ideas dominated learning in the Islamic world and Europe, where he was known simply as the Philosopher. Some of his scientific ideas were later proved wrong, but his methods of observation and reasoning remain influential.",
d:[["384 BCE","Born in Stagira"],["367 BCE","Joins Plato’s Academy in Athens"],["343 BCE","Becomes tutor to the young Alexander"],["335 BCE","Founds the Lyceum in Athens"],["323 BCE","Leaves Athens after Alexander’s death"],["322 BCE","Dies in Chalcis"]],
s:[["Early life","Aristotle was born in 384 BCE in Stagira, in northern Greece. His father, Nicomachus, was a doctor at the court of the king of Macedon, which may have encouraged Aristotle’s interest in biology. He was orphaned young and raised by a guardian."],["Student of Plato","At 17, Aristotle went to Athens and joined Plato’s Academy, where he stayed for about 20 years, first as a student and then as a teacher. He admired Plato but came to disagree with his theory of Forms, believing that the forms of things exist within things themselves, not in a separate world."],["Tutor of Alexander","In 343 BCE King Philip II of Macedon invited Aristotle to tutor his teenage son, Alexander, who would become Alexander the Great. Aristotle taught him for a few years. How much he influenced the future conqueror is debated."],["The Lyceum","In 335 BCE Aristotle returned to Athens and founded his own school, the Lyceum. He taught while walking along its covered walkways, so his followers were called Peripatetics, from the Greek for walking around. The Lyceum collected books, specimens and records, like an early research institute."],["Logic","Aristotle created the first formal system of logic. He described the syllogism, a form of reasoning such as: all humans are mortal; Socrates is human; therefore Socrates is mortal. His logic remained the basis of the subject for more than 2,000 years."],["The first biologist","Aristotle studied hundreds of kinds of animals, dissecting them and observing their behavior, especially in the lagoon on the island of Lesbos. He classified animals into groups and made accurate observations, such as that dolphins breathe air and give birth to live young, though he also made errors."],["Ethics","In his Nicomachean Ethics, Aristotle argued that the goal of life is eudaimonia, often translated as happiness or flourishing, achieved by living according to virtue. He taught that virtue is a golden mean between extremes: courage, for example, lies between cowardice and recklessness."],["Politics and the arts","In his Politics, Aristotle called humans political animals, meant to live in communities, and compared different kinds of government. In his Poetics, he analyzed tragedy and storytelling, ideas still used by writers and filmmakers today."],["Mistakes and criticism","Some of Aristotle’s ideas were wrong, such as his belief that heavy objects fall faster than light ones and that the Earth is the center of the universe; Galileo and others later disproved them. Modern readers also criticize his views that some people are slaves by nature and that women are inferior to men."],["Influence through the ages","After the fall of Rome, Aristotle’s works were preserved and studied by scholars in the Islamic world, such as al-Farabi, Ibn Sina and Ibn Rushd, known in Europe as Averroes, the Commentator. From the 1100s, they were translated into Latin and shaped European universities and the thought of Thomas Aquinas. His influence is felt in science, logic and ethics today."]],
sp:["Leaving Athens","After Alexander the Great died in 323 BCE, anger against Macedonians boiled over in Athens. Aristotle, long associated with the Macedonian court, was charged with impiety, the same charge that had led to the death of Socrates. According to ancient writers, Aristotle left the city, saying he would not allow the Athenians to sin twice against philosophy. He went to his mother’s family estate in Chalcis on the island of Euboea, where he died the following year at about 62."],
g:[["Syllogism","A form of logical argument with two premises and a conclusion"],["Golden mean","The ideal middle point between two extremes"],["Peripatetic","A follower of Aristotle; also walking about"],["Eudaimonia","Happiness or flourishing, the goal of life for Aristotle"]],
f:["Aristotle tutored Alexander the Great.","He founded the Lyceum in Athens.","He created the first system of formal logic."]},

{id:"rumi",t:"Rumi",i:"🌀",w:"Rumi",a:["Jalal al-Din Muhammad Rumi","Mevlana","Mawlana"],born:"30 September 1207, Balkh region (present-day Afghanistan or Tajikistan)",died:"17 December 1273, Konya (present-day Türkiye)",nat:"Persian",known:"Sufi mystical poetry, the Masnavi and inspiring the whirling dervishes",r:["islam","poetry","turkeycountry","centralasia"],
l:"Jalal al-Din Rumi was a 13th-century Persian poet, Islamic scholar and Sufi mystic whose poems about love, longing and the soul’s search for God are loved around the world. Born in Central Asia, he fled the Mongol invasions with his family and settled in Konya, in present-day Türkiye. His friendship with the wandering mystic Shams of Tabriz transformed him into a poet. His great work, the Masnavi, is one of the most important books of Sufi literature. His followers founded the Mevlevi order, famous for the whirling dervishes.",
d:[["1207","Born in the Balkh region"],["c. 1218","His family flees the advancing Mongols"],["c. 1228","Settles in Konya"],["1244","Meets the mystic Shams of Tabriz"],["c. 1258","Begins composing the Masnavi"],["1273","Dies in Konya"]],
s:[["A scholar’s son","Rumi was born in 1207 in the region of Balkh, in present-day Afghanistan or Tajikistan, into a family of Islamic scholars. His father, Baha al-Din Walad, was a respected preacher and teacher. Rumi wrote mainly in Persian, the literary language of the region."],["Fleeing the Mongols","Around 1218, as Genghis Khan’s armies approached, Rumi’s family left their home. They traveled through Baghdad and Mecca and eventually settled in Konya, capital of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum, in Anatolia. The name Rumi means from Rum, the old name for Anatolia, once part of the Roman Empire."],["Teacher and scholar","After his father’s death, Rumi became a respected teacher of Islamic law and religion in Konya, with many students. He was a learned and serious scholar, not yet known as a poet."],["Shams of Tabriz","In 1244 Rumi met Shams of Tabriz, a wandering, unconventional mystic. Their intense spiritual friendship changed Rumi completely; he spent days in conversation with Shams and neglected his students, who grew jealous. Shams left, returned and then disappeared for good around 1248, possibly murdered."],["Becoming a poet","Grief and longing for Shams turned Rumi into a poet. He composed thousands of lyrical poems, many collected in the Divan-e Shams-e Tabrizi, named in honor of his friend. In them, the love between friends becomes a symbol of the soul’s love for God."],["The Masnavi","Rumi’s greatest work, the Masnavi, is a long poem of about 25,000 couplets in six books, filled with stories, fables, jokes and teachings about the spiritual path. It opens with the song of a reed flute, cut from its reed bed, crying out its longing to return. Some have called it the Quran in Persian."],["Sufism","Sufism is the mystical tradition within Islam, which seeks a direct, personal experience of God through love, devotion, prayer, music and remembrance of God. Rumi’s poetry is rooted in the Quran and Islamic teaching, expressed through images of love, wine, music and dance."],["The whirling dervishes","After Rumi’s death, his son Sultan Walad and followers founded the Mevlevi Sufi order. Its members, called whirling dervishes, perform the sema, a ceremony in which they turn slowly in white robes and tall hats to music, as a form of prayer and remembrance. The Mevlevi sema is recognized by UNESCO."],["Death","Rumi died in Konya on 17 December 1273. Mourners of many faiths, Muslims, Christians and Jews, attended his funeral. His followers call the anniversary of his death the Şeb-i Arus, his wedding night, the night his soul was united with God, marked each year in Konya by ceremonies of the whirling dervishes."],["Rumi today","Rumi’s tomb in Konya, under a green dome, is visited by millions of people. His poetry has been translated into many languages, and in English versions he has become one of the most popular poets in the United States. Some scholars warn that popular translations often remove the Islamic context of his work, which is central to its meaning."]],
sp:["The song of the reed","The Masnavi begins with the image of a reed flute. Cut from the reed bed where it grew, the flute cries out in a mournful voice, telling of separation and longing. Everyone who is far from their source, the poem says, longs to return to it. For Rumi, the reed is the human soul, separated from God and longing to return, and its music is the cry of love. The opening lines are among the most famous in Persian literature and are often recited with the haunting sound of the ney, the reed flute of the Mevlevi ceremonies."],
g:[["Sufism","The mystical tradition within Islam"],["Dervish","A member of a Sufi religious order"],["Masnavi","Rumi’s long spiritual poem"],["Sema","The whirling ceremony of the Mevlevi order"]],
f:["Rumi was born in Central Asia and died in Konya.","The Masnavi has about 25,000 couplets.","His followers founded the whirling dervishes."]},

{id:"ataturk",t:"Mustafa Kemal Atatürk",i:"🎖️",w:"Mustafa Kemal Atatürk",a:["Mustafa Kemal Pasha","Atatürk"],born:"1881, Salonica, Ottoman Empire (now Thessaloniki, Greece)",died:"10 November 1938, Istanbul, Türkiye",nat:"Turkish",known:"Founding the Republic of Turkey and leading sweeping secular reforms",r:["turkeycountry","ottoman","wwi","istanbul"],
l:"Mustafa Kemal Atatürk was a Turkish army officer and statesman who founded the Republic of Turkey, now Türkiye, in 1923 and served as its first president until his death in 1938. A hero of the Gallipoli campaign in World War I, he led the Turkish War of Independence after the defeat of the Ottoman Empire. As president, he carried out sweeping reforms to create a modern, secular nation-state, including adopting the Latin alphabet and granting women the vote. He is revered in Türkiye as the father of the nation, though critics point to his one-party rule and the suppression of opposition and of Kurdish uprisings.",
d:[["1881","Born in Salonica"],["1915","Leads Ottoman troops at Gallipoli"],["1919","Begins the Turkish War of Independence"],["1923","The Republic of Turkey is proclaimed"],["1928","Turkey adopts the Latin alphabet"],["1934","Women gain the vote in national elections; he is given the surname Atatürk"]],
s:[["Early life","Mustafa was born in 1881 in Salonica, a busy port of the Ottoman Empire, now Thessaloniki in Greece. A teacher gave him the extra name Kemal, meaning perfection. He attended military schools and became an army officer, joining reformers who wanted to modernize the declining empire."],["Gallipoli","In 1915, during World War I, Allied forces landed on the Gallipoli peninsula to seize the straits to Istanbul. Mustafa Kemal commanded Ottoman troops that held key heights against British, Australian, New Zealand and French forces. After months of fighting and huge losses on both sides, the Allies withdrew, and he became a national hero."],["The War of Independence","After the Ottoman defeat in 1918, the Allies occupied parts of the empire and planned to divide Anatolia. On 19 May 1919, Mustafa Kemal landed at Samsun and organized national resistance. His forces fought Greek, Armenian, French and other forces and won, leading to the Treaty of Lausanne in 1923, which recognized Turkey’s borders."],["The Republic","The sultanate was abolished in 1922, and on 29 October 1923 the Republic of Turkey was proclaimed, with Ankara as its capital and Mustafa Kemal as president. In 1924 the caliphate, the Islamic religious leadership held by Ottoman sultans, was also abolished."],["Secularism","Mustafa Kemal believed Turkey must become a modern, secular nation. Religious courts and schools were closed, Islam was removed as the state religion, and new law codes based on European models were introduced. The fez hat was banned in favor of Western hats."],["A new alphabet","In 1928 Turkey replaced the Arabic script with a new Latin-based alphabet suited to Turkish sounds. Mustafa Kemal traveled the country with a chalkboard, teaching the new letters himself. The reform helped greatly increase literacy."],["Women’s rights","Women gained equal rights in marriage and inheritance under the 1926 civil code, the vote in local elections in 1930 and in national elections in 1934, earlier than in many European countries. In 1935 eighteen women were elected to parliament."],["The name Atatürk","In 1934 a law required all Turks to adopt surnames. Parliament gave Mustafa Kemal the surname Atatürk, meaning father of the Turks, and forbade anyone else from using it."],["Criticism","Atatürk ruled through a single party, and opposition was restricted. His government harshly suppressed Kurdish revolts, including at Dersim in 1937 and 1938, with many deaths, and limited religious expression. Critics argue that his reforms were imposed from above, while supporters see them as necessary to build a modern state."],["Death and legacy","Atatürk died on 10 November 1938 at Dolmabahçe Palace in Istanbul at 9:05 in the morning. Every year at that moment, Türkiye observes a moment of silence, and traffic stops. His mausoleum, Anıtkabir, in Ankara, is visited by millions. Debates continue in Türkiye about his secular legacy."]],
sp:["The clocks at 9:05","Mustafa Kemal Atatürk died in Dolmabahçe Palace in Istanbul at 9:05 in the morning on 10 November 1938. According to tradition, the clocks in the palace were stopped at that moment, and some in the palace are still set to 9:05 today. Each year on 10 November at 9:05, sirens sound across Türkiye, and people stop wherever they are, cars halt on highways and pedestrians stand still for a minute of silence in his memory."],
g:[["Secularism","Separation of religion from government"],["Caliphate","Islamic religious and political leadership"],["Republic","A state with an elected head rather than a monarch"],["Anıtkabir","Atatürk’s mausoleum in Ankara"]],
f:["Atatürk founded the Republic of Turkey in 1923.","He introduced the Latin alphabet for Turkish in 1928.","Atatürk means father of the Turks."]},

{id:"nehru",t:"Jawaharlal Nehru",i:"🌹",w:"Jawaharlal Nehru",a:["Pandit Nehru","Chacha Nehru"],born:"14 November 1889, Allahabad (Prayagraj), British India",died:"27 May 1964, New Delhi, India",nat:"Indian",known:"Leading India’s independence movement and serving as its first prime minister",r:["gandhi","indiacountry","indrev","partition"],
l:"Jawaharlal Nehru was a leader of India’s independence movement and the country’s first prime minister, serving from independence in 1947 until his death in 1964. A close ally of Mahatma Gandhi, he spent about nine years in British prisons. As prime minister, he built India as a democratic, secular republic, promoted science, industry and education and helped found the Non-Aligned Movement of countries that avoided taking sides in the Cold War. Admirers credit him with building India’s democracy and institutions; critics fault his economic policies and his handling of relations with China and of Kashmir.",
d:[["1889","Born in Allahabad"],["1916","Meets Mahatma Gandhi"],["1929","Presides over the Congress session that demands full independence"],["1947","Becomes India’s first prime minister"],["1955","Attends the Bandung Conference of Asian and African nations"],["1962","India is defeated in a border war with China"]],
s:[["A privileged youth","Nehru was born in 1889 into a wealthy Kashmiri Brahmin family in Allahabad. His father, Motilal Nehru, was a successful lawyer and nationalist leader. Jawaharlal studied in England at Harrow School and Cambridge University and trained as a lawyer in London."],["Joining Gandhi","Returning to India in 1912, Nehru was drawn into the independence movement. He met Mahatma Gandhi in 1916 and became one of his closest followers, though he was more modern and socialist in outlook. Gandhi later named him his political heir."],["Freedom struggle","As president of the Indian National Congress in 1929, Nehru led the call for purna swaraj, complete independence. He took part in campaigns of civil disobedience and spent about nine years in British prisons, where he wrote books such as Glimpses of World History and The Discovery of India."],["Independence and partition","India became independent on 15 August 1947, but British India was partitioned into India and Pakistan, amid violence that killed hundreds of thousands and displaced millions. At midnight, Nehru gave his famous Tryst with Destiny speech, declaring that India would awake to life and freedom."],["Building democracy","As prime minister, Nehru led India through its first general elections in 1951 and 1952, then the largest democratic election ever held. India adopted a constitution guaranteeing equal rights and secular government, protecting religious minorities."],["Science and development","Nehru believed science and industry would lift India out of poverty. His government launched Five-Year Plans, built steel plants and large dams, which he called the temples of modern India, and founded institutions such as the Indian Institutes of Technology and the forerunner of India’s space program."],["Non-alignment","During the Cold War, Nehru refused to join either the American-led or the Soviet-led bloc. With leaders such as Egypt’s Nasser, Indonesia’s Sukarno and Yugoslavia’s Tito, he helped found the Non-Aligned Movement in 1961, giving newly independent nations a stronger voice."],["Setbacks","Disputes with Pakistan over Kashmir led to war in 1947 and continued tensions. Relations with China, at first friendly, collapsed into a border war in 1962, in which India was defeated, a heavy blow to Nehru in his final years."],["Children’s Day","Nehru was fond of children, who called him Chacha Nehru, Uncle Nehru. His birthday, 14 November, is celebrated as Children’s Day in India. His daughter, Indira Gandhi, and grandson, Rajiv Gandhi, also became prime ministers."],["Legacy","Supporters credit Nehru with keeping India democratic and united, building scientific and educational institutions and promoting secularism. Critics argue that his state-controlled economic policies slowed growth for decades, that he mishandled China and Kashmir and that he began a family political dynasty. His legacy remains strongly debated in Indian politics today."]],
sp:["A tryst with destiny","Just before midnight on 14 August 1947, Jawaharlal Nehru rose to speak in the Constituent Assembly in New Delhi. Long years ago, he said, India had made a tryst with destiny, and now the time had come to redeem that pledge. At the stroke of the midnight hour, when the world sleeps, India would awake to life and freedom. As the clock struck twelve, a conch shell was blown, and India became independent after nearly two centuries of British rule. The speech is one of the most famous of the 20th century."],
g:[["Prime minister","The head of government in a parliamentary democracy"],["Non-Aligned Movement","Countries that did not join either Cold War bloc"],["Partition","The division of British India into India and Pakistan in 1947"],["Purna swaraj","Complete independence"]],
f:["Nehru was India’s first prime minister.","His birthday is Children’s Day in India.","He spent about nine years in British prisons."]},

{id:"hokusai",t:"Katsushika Hokusai",i:"🌊",w:"Hokusai",a:["Hokusai","Gakyō Rōjin Manji"],born:"c. 31 October 1760, Edo (Tokyo), Japan",died:"10 May 1849, Edo (Tokyo), Japan",nat:"Japanese",known:"The Great Wave off Kanagawa and Thirty-Six Views of Mount Fuji",r:["japancountry","haikuart","fuji","tokyo"],
l:"Katsushika Hokusai was a Japanese artist of the Edo period, a master of ukiyo-e woodblock prints and painting, best known for The Great Wave off Kanagawa, one of the most famous images in the world. During a career lasting about 70 years, he produced tens of thousands of works, from landscapes and portraits to sketchbooks of everyday life, and used more than 30 different names. His series Thirty-Six Views of Mount Fuji transformed landscape art in Japan, and his work deeply influenced European artists such as Claude Monet and Vincent van Gogh.",
d:[["c. 1760","Born in Edo"],["1779","Publishes his first prints"],["1804","Paints a giant portrait of Daruma at a festival"],["1814","The first volume of the Hokusai Manga is published"],["c. 1831","Thirty-Six Views of Mount Fuji, including The Great Wave, is published"],["1849","Dies in Edo, aged about 88"]],
s:[["Early life","Hokusai was born around 1760 in Edo, now Tokyo. As a boy, he worked in a lending library and learned wood carving. At about 18 he became a pupil of the print artist Katsukawa Shunshō, making prints of kabuki actors."],["Ukiyo-e","Ukiyo-e, meaning pictures of the floating world, were colorful woodblock prints popular with ordinary townspeople in Edo. They showed actors, beautiful women, landscapes and legends. Artists drew the designs, carvers cut them into wooden blocks and printers pressed them onto paper, one block for each color."],["Many names","Hokusai changed his artistic name more than 30 times, often when he began a new style or period. Hokusai itself means North Studio. Late in life he called himself Gakyō Rōjin Manji, the old man mad about painting. He also moved house about 93 times."],["The Hokusai Manga","From 1814, Hokusai published the Hokusai Manga, sketchbooks filled with thousands of lively drawings of people, animals, plants, gods, ghosts and everyday scenes. They were used by students as models. The word manga, meaning whimsical sketches, is now used for Japanese comics."],["Thirty-Six Views of Mount Fuji","Around 1830 to 1832, in his seventies, Hokusai produced Thirty-Six Views of Mount Fuji, a series of landscape prints showing the sacred mountain from many places and in different seasons and weather, often with people working in the foreground. It was so popular that ten more prints were added."],["The Great Wave","The Great Wave off Kanagawa shows a towering wave with claw-like foam about to crash over three boats of rowers, with tiny Mount Fuji in the distance. It used Prussian blue, a new imported pigment. Thousands of copies were printed, and it has become one of the most recognized artworks in the world."],["Showman","Hokusai loved surprising crowds. In 1804 at a festival in Edo, he painted a portrait of the Buddhist monk Daruma on a sheet of paper so large, about 18 meters long, that people had to view it from a temple roof. He is also said to have painted birds on a grain of rice."],["Old man mad about painting","Hokusai believed he was always improving. In his seventies, he wrote that nothing he had drawn before the age of 70 was worth noticing, and that by 110 every dot and line would be alive. On his deathbed at about 88, he is said to have wished for five more years to become a true artist."],["Influence on the West","After Japan opened to the world in the 1850s, Japanese prints flooded into Europe. Hokusai’s bold compositions inspired the movement called Japonisme. Monet, Van Gogh, Degas and the composer Claude Debussy, whose orchestral work La Mer used The Great Wave on its cover, admired his work."],["Legacy","Today Hokusai’s works are treasured in museums worldwide. The Great Wave appears on Japan’s 1,000-yen banknote introduced in 2024, and his Mount Fuji views decorate the pages of Japanese passports. His daughter, Katsushika Ōi, was also a gifted painter who worked with him."]],
sp:["The Great Wave on a banknote","In July 2024, Japan introduced new banknotes for the first time in 20 years. On the back of the new 1,000-yen note is Hokusai’s The Great Wave off Kanagawa, the famous print of a giant wave towering over fishermen’s boats with Mount Fuji in the distance. Printed around 1831 using wooden blocks and costing about the price of a bowl of noodles at the time, the image now appears on money used by millions of people every day, almost 200 years after Hokusai designed it."],
g:[["Ukiyo-e","Japanese woodblock prints of the floating world"],["Woodblock print","A picture printed from carved wooden blocks"],["Japonisme","European fascination with Japanese art in the 1800s"],["Prussian blue","A deep blue pigment used in The Great Wave"]],
f:["Hokusai made The Great Wave off Kanagawa.","He used more than 30 artistic names.","The Great Wave is on Japan’s 1,000-yen note."]},

{id:"tolstoy",t:"Leo Tolstoy",i:"📚",w:"Leo Tolstoy",a:["Lev Nikolayevich Tolstoy","Count Leo Tolstoy"],born:"9 September 1828, Yasnaya Polyana, Russian Empire",died:"20 November 1910, Astapovo, Russian Empire",nat:"Russian",known:"War and Peace, Anna Karenina and his teachings of nonviolence",r:["russianlit","novel","gandhi","russiacountry"],
l:"Leo Tolstoy was a Russian writer widely regarded as one of the greatest novelists of all time. His epic War and Peace and his novel Anna Karenina are masterpieces of world literature, celebrated for their vast range of characters and deep insight into human life. A wealthy aristocrat, Tolstoy underwent a spiritual crisis in middle age and developed his own form of Christianity, preaching nonviolence, simple living and love. His ideas influenced Mahatma Gandhi and Martin Luther King Jr. He died at 82 at a railway station after leaving his home.",
d:[["1828","Born at Yasnaya Polyana"],["1855","Publishes his Sevastopol Sketches"],["1862","Marries Sophia Behrs"],["1869","Completes War and Peace"],["1878","Anna Karenina is published as a book"],["1910","Leaves home and dies at Astapovo"]],
s:[["A count’s childhood","Lev Nikolayevich Tolstoy was born in 1828 at Yasnaya Polyana, his family’s estate south of Moscow, into an old noble family. His mother died when he was two and his father when he was nine. He was raised by relatives and attended Kazan University but left without a degree."],["Soldier and writer","In the 1850s Tolstoy served in the army in the Caucasus and in the Crimean War. His Sevastopol Sketches, describing the siege of Sevastopol, showed the horrors of war realistically and made him famous. His first book, Childhood, had appeared in 1852."],["Teacher of peasants","Tolstoy opened a school for peasant children at Yasnaya Polyana in 1859, using free, progressive methods, and later wrote reading books for children. He believed deeply in the wisdom of ordinary people."],["Family life","In 1862 Tolstoy married Sophia Behrs, who was 16 years younger. They had 13 children, of whom eight survived childhood. Sophia copied his manuscripts by hand, including War and Peace several times. Their marriage later became deeply troubled."],["War and Peace","War and Peace, published from 1865 to 1869, follows aristocratic families during Napoleon’s invasion of Russia in 1812. With more than 500 characters, battles, balls and philosophical reflections on history, it is one of the longest and most admired novels ever written."],["Anna Karenina","Anna Karenina, published in parts from 1875 to 1877, tells of a married woman’s tragic love affair, contrasted with the search for meaning of a landowner named Levin. Its first line, that all happy families are alike but each unhappy family is unhappy in its own way, is world famous."],["Spiritual crisis","In the late 1870s, despite fame and wealth, Tolstoy fell into despair about the meaning of life. He found answers in the teachings of Jesus, especially the Sermon on the Mount, but rejected the authority of the church. He preached love, nonviolence, simple living and refusing to take part in violence, including by the state."],["Influence on Gandhi","Tolstoy’s book The Kingdom of God Is Within You, banned in Russia, deeply influenced the young Mohandas Gandhi in South Africa. The two corresponded, and in 1908 Tolstoy wrote A Letter to a Hindu, arguing for nonviolent resistance to British rule. Gandhi named a community near Johannesburg Tolstoy Farm."],["Conflict and excommunication","Tolstoy gave up meat, alcohol and hunting, dressed like a peasant and worked in the fields. He wished to give away his property, causing bitter conflict with his wife. In 1901 the Russian Orthodox Church excommunicated him for his teachings. He was nominated many times for the Nobel Prize but never won."],["The final journey","In October 1910, at 82, Tolstoy secretly left home before dawn to live as a wanderer. He fell ill on the train and died at the small railway station of Astapovo on 20 November 1910, as reporters from around the world gathered outside. He was buried in a simple grave in the forest at Yasnaya Polyana."]],
sp:["Death at a railway station","In the early hours of 28 October 1910, by the old Russian calendar, Leo Tolstoy, then 82, crept out of his house at Yasnaya Polyana, leaving a letter for his wife. Accompanied by his doctor, he boarded a train heading south, perhaps planning to live in a monastery or abroad. Soon he caught pneumonia and was taken off the train at Astapovo, a small station, where he was laid in the stationmaster’s house. News spread, and reporters, film crews and his family gathered. He died there a week later. The station was later renamed Lev Tolstoy in his honor."],
g:[["Epic","A long work about heroic or important events"],["Aristocrat","A member of the nobility"],["Excommunication","Being officially cut off from a church"],["Nonviolence","Refusing to use violence, even in resistance"]],
f:["Tolstoy wrote War and Peace and Anna Karenina.","He influenced Gandhi’s ideas of nonviolence.","He died at a railway station at 82."]},

{id:"cervantes",t:"Miguel de Cervantes",i:"🗡️",w:"Miguel de Cervantes",a:["Miguel de Cervantes Saavedra"],born:"c. 29 September 1547, Alcalá de Henares, Spain",died:"22 April 1616, Madrid, Spain",nat:"Spanish",known:"Writing Don Quixote, often called the first modern novel",r:["spanish","novel","spaincountry","shakes"],
l:"Miguel de Cervantes was a Spanish novelist, playwright and poet, the author of Don Quixote, often called the first modern novel and one of the greatest works of literature ever written. His own life was full of adventure: he was wounded at the Battle of Lepanto, captured by pirates and held for five years in Algiers, and later imprisoned for debts. His story of an aging gentleman who reads so many tales of chivalry that he sets out as a knight has been translated into more languages than almost any other book. Spanish is often called the language of Cervantes.",
d:[["1547","Born in Alcalá de Henares"],["1571","Wounded at the Battle of Lepanto"],["1575","Captured by pirates and taken to Algiers"],["1580","Ransomed and returns to Spain"],["1605","Publishes the first part of Don Quixote"],["1615","Publishes the second part of Don Quixote"]],
s:[["Early life","Cervantes was born in 1547 in Alcalá de Henares, near Madrid, the son of a barber-surgeon who struggled with debts. The family moved often. Little is known of his education, but he loved reading, even, he said, scraps of paper found in the street."],["Soldier at Lepanto","In Italy, Cervantes joined the Spanish army. On 7 October 1571, at the naval Battle of Lepanto, a Christian alliance defeated the Ottoman fleet. Though ill with fever, Cervantes fought bravely and was shot twice in the chest and once in the left hand, which was permanently maimed. He was proud of his wound all his life."],["Captive in Algiers","In 1575, sailing home to Spain, Cervantes was captured by Barbary pirates and taken to Algiers, where he was held for ransom for five years. He led four daring escape attempts, all of which failed, but each time took the blame to protect others. In 1580 he was ransomed by Trinitarian friars."],["Struggles at home","Back in Spain, Cervantes wrote plays and a pastoral novel with little success. He worked as a purchaser of supplies for the Spanish Armada and as a tax collector, and was jailed more than once over irregularities in his accounts. It was perhaps in prison in Seville that he first imagined Don Quixote."],["Don Quixote","In 1605 Cervantes published the first part of The Ingenious Gentleman Don Quixote of La Mancha. Its hero, an aging gentleman, reads so many books about knights that he loses his wits, renames himself Don Quixote and rides out on his bony horse, Rocinante, to right wrongs, with a farmer, Sancho Panza, as his squire."],["Windmills and giants","In the book’s most famous episode, Don Quixote sees windmills and believes they are giants, charging at them with his lance until a sail knocks him down. The phrase tilting at windmills now means fighting imaginary enemies, and quixotic means idealistic but impractical."],["The first modern novel","Don Quixote was an instant success. It mixes comedy and sadness, realism and fantasy, and shows characters who change and grow. Its playful storytelling, in which characters even discuss the book itself, makes it the model for the modern novel. Writers from Dickens to García Márquez have called it an inspiration."],["The second part","In 1614 an unknown author published a fake sequel. Cervantes responded in 1615 with his own second part, in which characters mock the fake version. He also wrote the Exemplary Novels, a collection of short stories, in 1613."],["Death","Cervantes died in Madrid on 22 April 1616 and was buried the next day. His death came days before that of William Shakespeare. UNESCO chose 23 April, a date linked to both writers and the Peruvian author Inca Garcilaso de la Vega, as World Book and Copyright Day."],["Legacy","Spain’s cultural institute, the Instituto Cervantes, which promotes the Spanish language worldwide and has a center in Manila, is named after him, as is the Cervantes Prize, the most important award for literature in Spanish. In 2015 researchers announced that they had found his remains in a convent in Madrid."]],
sp:["Searching for Cervantes","Cervantes was buried in 1616 in the Convent of the Barefoot Trinitarians in Madrid, run by the order of friars who had ransomed him from captivity. Over the centuries, the church was rebuilt and his exact grave was lost. In 2014, a team of scientists used ground-penetrating radar, forensic experts and historians to search beneath the convent. In 2015 they announced that they had found fragments of bones and a coffin marked with the letters M. C. among remains believed to include Cervantes and his wife. He was reburied with honors in the convent."],
g:[["Novel","A long work of fiction"],["Chivalry","The code and stories of medieval knights"],["Quixotic","Idealistic but impractical"],["Squire","A knight’s attendant"]],
f:["Cervantes wrote Don Quixote.","He was wounded at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571.","World Book Day, 23 April, is linked to his death."]}
);

