// People, part 15: a computer pioneer, explorers of the poles, the sky, space, the highest mountain and the sea, and two sporting legends.
window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"turing",t:"Alan Turing",i:"🖥️",w:"Alan Turing",a:["Alan Mathison Turing"],born:"23 June 1912, London, England",died:"7 June 1954, Wilmslow, England",nat:"British",known:"Founding computer science, breaking Enigma codes in World War II and the Turing test",r:["comp","turingtest","ww2","aihistory"],
l:"Alan Turing was a British mathematician who laid the foundations of computer science and artificial intelligence. In 1936 he described a theoretical universal computing machine, now called a Turing machine. During World War II, he played a leading role at Bletchley Park in breaking German codes made with the Enigma machine, work that helped the Allies win the war. After the war, he designed early computers and proposed the Turing test of machine intelligence. In 1952 he was prosecuted for his homosexual relationship, then a crime in Britain, and he died in 1954. He has since been honored as one of the most important scientists of the 20th century.",
d:[["1912","Born in London"],["1936","Describes the Turing machine"],["1939","Begins codebreaking work at Bletchley Park"],["1950","Proposes the imitation game, now called the Turing test"],["1952","Convicted of gross indecency for a homosexual relationship"],["1954","Dies at his home in Wilmslow"]],
s:[["A curious child","Alan Turing was born in London in 1912. His father worked in the Indian Civil Service, and Alan and his brother were raised partly by foster families in England. He showed a passion for science and mathematics from an early age, once cycling about 100 kilometers to school during a general strike."],["Cambridge","Turing studied mathematics at King’s College, Cambridge, and was elected a fellow at just 22. He was a keen long-distance runner, later nearly qualifying for the British Olympic marathon team."],["The Turing machine","In 1936 Turing published a paper, On Computable Numbers, imagining a simple machine that reads and writes symbols on an endless tape following rules. He showed that such a machine could carry out any calculation that can be described step by step, and that some problems can never be solved by any machine. This idea is the basis of all modern computers."],["Bletchley Park","When World War II began in 1939, Turing joined Britain’s secret codebreaking center at Bletchley Park. The German military used Enigma machines to scramble messages, with billions of possible settings that changed daily."],["Breaking Enigma","Building on the work of Polish codebreakers, Turing designed the bombe, an electromechanical machine that helped find each day’s Enigma settings. He led the team breaking the especially difficult German naval Enigma, helping the Allies track U-boats in the Battle of the Atlantic. Historians believe this work shortened the war and saved many lives."],["Early computers","After the war, Turing designed the Automatic Computing Engine, one of the first designs for a stored-program computer, and later worked on the Manchester computers, writing programs and even early computer music."],["Can machines think?","In 1950 Turing published Computing Machinery and Intelligence, asking whether machines can think. He proposed the imitation game, now called the Turing test, and predicted that machines would one day converse like humans. He is regarded as a founder of artificial intelligence."],["Patterns in nature","In 1952 Turing published a paper on morphogenesis, explaining how chemical reactions could create patterns in nature, such as the spots of a leopard or the stripes of a zebra. Biologists later confirmed many of his ideas."],["Prosecution and death","In 1952 Turing was convicted of gross indecency because of his relationship with a man, then a crime in Britain. To avoid prison, he accepted hormone treatment. He lost his security clearance. On 7 June 1954 he died of cyanide poisoning at 41; an inquest ruled it suicide, though some have questioned this."],["Recognition","For decades his wartime work stayed secret. In 2009 the British prime minister apologized for his treatment, and in 2013 he received a royal pardon. A 2017 law pardoned thousands of other men convicted under similar laws. Since 2021 his portrait has appeared on the Bank of England’s 50-pound note, and the Turing Award is considered the Nobel Prize of computing."]],
sp:["The bombe","Every day at midnight, the German military changed the settings of its Enigma machines, so codebreakers at Bletchley Park had to start again each day. Turing realized that likely words, such as weather reports or standard greetings, could be used to test possible settings. He designed the bombe, a machine about two meters wide filled with rotating drums that clicked through settings at high speed, stopping when it found possibilities consistent with these guesses. More than 200 bombes were eventually built, operated mainly by women of the Women’s Royal Naval Service, and they decoded thousands of messages a day."],
g:[["Turing machine","An imaginary machine that defines what can be computed"],["Enigma","A German cipher machine used in World War II"],["Codebreaking","Working out the meaning of secret coded messages"],["Morphogenesis","How living things develop their shapes and patterns"]],
f:["Turing helped break Enigma codes in World War II.","He proposed the Turing test in 1950.","He appears on the British 50-pound note."]},

{id:"amundsen",t:"Roald Amundsen",i:"🧭",w:"Roald Amundsen",a:["Roald Engelbregt Gravning Amundsen"],born:"16 July 1872, Borge, Norway",died:"c. 18 June 1928, Barents Sea (disappeared)",nat:"Norwegian",known:"Leading the first expedition to reach the South Pole",r:["antarc","arctic","norwaycountry","explor"],
l:"Roald Amundsen was a Norwegian polar explorer who led the first expedition to reach the South Pole, on 14 December 1911, beating the British expedition of Robert Falcon Scott. He was also the first to sail through the Northwest Passage across the Arctic and, in 1926, was among the first people to fly over the North Pole, making him the first person proven to have reached both poles. Amundsen learned survival skills from the Inuit and planned carefully, using dogs and skis. He disappeared in 1928 while flying to rescue fellow explorers in the Arctic.",
d:[["1872","Born in Borge, Norway"],["1897–1899","Takes part in the first expedition to spend a winter in Antarctica"],["1903–1906","Sails through the Northwest Passage"],["1911","Reaches the South Pole"],["1926","Flies over the North Pole in the airship Norge"],["1928","Disappears on a rescue flight in the Arctic"]],
s:[["Dreams of the poles","Amundsen was born in 1872 into a family of shipowners. As a boy he read about polar explorers and slept with windows open in winter to toughen himself. To please his mother, he studied medicine, but after her death he left to go to sea."],["First Antarctic winter","From 1897 to 1899, Amundsen served on the Belgian ship Belgica, whose crew became the first to spend a winter in Antarctica when their ship was trapped in ice. He learned how scurvy and cold could destroy an expedition."],["The Northwest Passage","Europeans had sought a sea route across the top of North America for centuries. From 1903 to 1906, Amundsen and six men sailed the small ship Gjøa through the icy Arctic channels, becoming the first to complete the Northwest Passage."],["Learning from the Inuit","During two winters in the Arctic, Amundsen lived near the Netsilik Inuit and learned how they survived: wearing loose fur clothing, using sled dogs and building snow shelters. These lessons were key to his later success."],["Race to the South Pole","Amundsen planned to reach the North Pole, but when Americans claimed to have reached it in 1909, he secretly turned south. Sailing in the ship Fram, he informed the British explorer Robert Falcon Scott by telegram that he too was heading for Antarctica."],["Reaching the pole","Amundsen set up base at the Bay of Whales. With four companions, Olav Bjaaland, Helmer Hanssen, Sverre Hassel and Oscar Wisting, and dog sleds, he set out in October 1911. They reached the South Pole on 14 December 1911 and left a tent with a letter for Scott."],["Scott’s tragedy","Scott’s team, relying partly on ponies and men pulling sleds, reached the pole on 17 January 1912, finding Amundsen’s tent. All five died on the return journey. Their story made Scott a hero in Britain, while Amundsen was sometimes unfairly criticized for winning."],["The Northeast Passage","From 1918 to 1920, Amundsen sailed the ship Maud along the Arctic coast of Russia through the Northeast Passage, becoming one of the first to sail through both Arctic passages."],["Over the North Pole","In 1926, with the American Lincoln Ellsworth and the Italian Umberto Nobile, Amundsen flew over the North Pole in the airship Norge, from Svalbard to Alaska. Because earlier claims to have reached the North Pole are doubted, he is considered the first person proven to have reached both poles."],["Disappearance","In June 1928, Nobile’s airship Italia crashed in the Arctic. Although they had quarreled, Amundsen joined the search, flying from Norway in a French seaplane. The plane vanished over the Barents Sea, and no trace of Amundsen was ever found. The American research station at the South Pole is named the Amundsen–Scott Station in honor of both explorers."]],
sp:["A letter left at the pole","When Amundsen’s team reached the South Pole, they pitched a small tent, which they named Polheim, and planted the Norwegian flag. Inside the tent, Amundsen left spare equipment and two letters: one to King Haakon VII of Norway and one to Robert Falcon Scott, asking Scott to deliver the king’s letter in case Amundsen did not survive the return journey. Scott found the tent and letters five weeks later. Though Scott and his companions died, the letter was found with their bodies, proving Amundsen had reached the pole first."],
g:[["Expedition","A journey made for a particular purpose, such as exploration"],["Northwest Passage","A sea route through the Arctic north of North America"],["Scurvy","A disease caused by lack of vitamin C"],["Airship","A large, lighter-than-air aircraft"]],
f:["Amundsen reached the South Pole on 14 December 1911.","He first sailed the Northwest Passage.","He learned survival skills from the Inuit."]},

{id:"earhart",t:"Amelia Earhart",i:"✈️",w:"Amelia Earhart",a:["Amelia Mary Earhart"],born:"24 July 1897, Atchison, Kansas, United States",died:"Disappeared 2 July 1937, central Pacific Ocean",nat:"American",known:"Being the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic Ocean",r:["flight","kiribaticountry","pacific","feminism"],
l:"Amelia Earhart was an American aviation pioneer, the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic Ocean, in 1932. She set many flying records, wrote books, helped found an organization for women pilots and became a powerful symbol of women’s courage and independence. In 1937, while attempting to fly around the world near the equator, she and her navigator, Fred Noonan, disappeared over the central Pacific Ocean near Howland Island. Their disappearance remains one of the greatest mysteries of the 20th century.",
d:[["1897","Born in Atchison, Kansas"],["1920","Takes her first airplane ride"],["1928","Becomes the first woman to cross the Atlantic by air, as a passenger"],["1932","Flies solo across the Atlantic"],["1935","Flies solo from Hawai‘i to California"],["1937","Disappears over the Pacific during a round-the-world flight"]],
s:[["An adventurous girl","Amelia Earhart was born in 1897 in Kansas. As a girl, she climbed trees, hunted rats with a rifle and kept a scrapbook of newspaper clippings about women who succeeded in jobs usually done by men. During World War I, she worked as a nurse’s aide in Canada, caring for wounded soldiers."],["Learning to fly","In 1920 Earhart took a ten-minute airplane ride in California and decided she had to fly. She worked several jobs to pay for lessons from the pilot Neta Snook and bought her first plane, a yellow biplane she called The Canary. In 1922 she set a women’s altitude record."],["Across the Atlantic as a passenger","In 1928 Earhart was invited to fly across the Atlantic as a passenger, becoming the first woman to do so. Although she said she felt like a sack of potatoes, as the pilots did all the flying, she became famous overnight."],["Solo across the Atlantic","On 20 and 21 May 1932, Earhart flew alone across the Atlantic from Newfoundland to Northern Ireland, battling ice, strong winds and mechanical problems, in about 15 hours. She was the first woman and only the second person, after Charles Lindbergh, to fly solo across the Atlantic."],["More records","In 1935 Earhart became the first person to fly solo from Hawai‘i to the US mainland. She also set speed records and flew solo nonstop across the United States. She received the Distinguished Flying Cross."],["Champion of women","Earhart helped found the Ninety-Nines, an organization of women pilots, and became its first president. She worked as an adviser at Purdue University, encouraging women to pursue careers in science and engineering, and spoke in favor of equal rights."],["Around the world","In 1937 Earhart set out to fly around the world near the equator, a journey of about 47,000 kilometers, in a Lockheed Electra with navigator Fred Noonan. Flying east from California, they crossed South America, Africa, India and Southeast Asia and reached Lae in New Guinea."],["The disappearance","On 2 July 1937, Earhart and Noonan took off from Lae for tiny Howland Island in the central Pacific, about 4,000 kilometers away. A US Coast Guard ship near the island heard her radio messages saying they were running low on fuel, but they never arrived. A huge search found no trace."],["Theories","Most experts believe the plane ran out of fuel and sank near Howland Island. Another theory suggests Earhart and Noonan landed on Nikumaroro, an uninhabited atoll now part of Kiribati, and died as castaways; artifacts found there have been studied but not proven to be theirs. Searches continue today."],["Legacy","Earhart remains one of the most famous aviators in history, a symbol of adventure and of women breaking barriers. Schools, scholarships, an airport and even a lunar crater are named after her."]],
sp:["Flying alone over the Atlantic","On the evening of 20 May 1932, five years to the day after Charles Lindbergh’s famous flight, Amelia Earhart took off alone from Harbour Grace in Newfoundland in her red Lockheed Vega. During the night, her altimeter failed, ice formed on her wings and flames flickered from a broken exhaust. She flew through storms and fog. Aiming for Paris, she landed instead in a pasture near Derry in Northern Ireland, after about 15 hours. A surprised farmhand asked if she had flown far; she replied, from America."],
g:[["Aviator","A pilot of an aircraft"],["Navigator","A person who plans and directs the route of a ship or aircraft"],["Solo","Done alone"],["Atoll","A ring of coral islands around a lagoon"]],
f:["Earhart flew solo across the Atlantic in 1932.","She disappeared over the Pacific in 1937.","She helped found the Ninety-Nines for women pilots."]},

{id:"gagarin",t:"Yuri Gagarin",i:"🚀",w:"Yuri Gagarin",a:["Yuri Alekseyevich Gagarin"],born:"9 March 1934, Klushino, Soviet Union",died:"27 March 1968, near Kirzhach, Soviet Union",nat:"Soviet (Russian)",known:"Being the first human to travel into space",r:["space","rocket","tereshkova","centralasia"],
l:"Yuri Gagarin was a Soviet pilot and cosmonaut who became the first human to travel into space. On 12 April 1961, his spacecraft, Vostok 1, launched from Baikonur in Kazakhstan, orbited the Earth once in about 108 minutes and returned safely. His flight was a triumph for the Soviet Union in the Space Race with the United States and made him one of the most famous people in the world. Gagarin never flew in space again and died in a training jet crash in 1968, aged 34. The anniversary of his flight is celebrated worldwide.",
d:[["1934","Born in Klushino"],["1941","German forces occupy his village during World War II"],["1955","Learns to fly at a flying club"],["1960","Selected as one of the first Soviet cosmonauts"],["1961","Becomes the first human in space"],["1968","Dies in a training jet crash"]],
s:[["Village childhood","Yuri Gagarin was born in 1934 in the village of Klushino, west of Moscow, where his parents worked on a collective farm. During World War II, German forces occupied the village, and the family lived in a dugout shelter. Two of his siblings were taken to Germany as forced laborers."],["Learning to fly","After the war, Gagarin trained as a foundry worker making metal castings. While studying at a technical school in Saratov, he joined a flying club and made his first solo flight in 1955. He then trained as a military pilot."],["Chosen as a cosmonaut","In 1960 Gagarin was selected from thousands of air force pilots as one of 20 men to train as the first Soviet cosmonauts. He impressed his instructors with his calm, intelligence and friendly manner. His short height, 1.57 meters, also suited the small spacecraft."],["Vostok 1","On the morning of 12 April 1961, Gagarin, aged 27, was launched from the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan aboard Vostok 1. As the rocket lifted off, he called out Poyekhali, meaning let’s go. The flight was controlled automatically, with Gagarin able to take control in an emergency."],["One orbit","Gagarin orbited the Earth once, reaching a height of over 300 kilometers. He reported that the Earth looked beautiful, with a lovely blue halo. The whole flight lasted about 108 minutes."],["Landing in a field","As planned, Gagarin ejected from his capsule at about 7 kilometers above the ground and landed by parachute in a field in the Saratov region. A farmer’s wife and her granddaughter were startled to see a man in an orange suit and helmet. He told them not to be afraid, that he was a Soviet like them."],["World hero","Gagarin became an instant hero. He toured dozens of countries, met world leaders and was mobbed by crowds. His flight shocked the United States, which sent its first astronaut, Alan Shepard, on a shorter flight a few weeks later, and helped spur the race to the Moon."],["After the flight","Because he was so valuable as a national hero, Soviet leaders were reluctant to risk Gagarin on another space flight. He helped train other cosmonauts, including Valentina Tereshkova, the first woman in space, and served as a backup pilot."],["Death","On 27 March 1968, Gagarin and his instructor, Vladimir Seryogin, died when their MiG-15 training jet crashed near the town of Kirzhach. The cause was long debated; investigations suggested the plane may have been caught in turbulence from another aircraft. He was buried in the Kremlin Wall in Moscow."],["Legacy","The anniversary of Gagarin’s flight, 12 April, is celebrated as Cosmonautics Day in Russia, the International Day of Human Space Flight by the United Nations and Yuri’s Night by space fans worldwide. A crater on the Moon and many streets, schools and statues bear his name."]],
sp:["Let’s go!","Before Yuri Gagarin’s flight, no one knew for certain what would happen to a human in space. Some scientists feared he might lose consciousness or even his mind. Just before liftoff on 12 April 1961, as the engines roared, Gagarin calmly said Poyekhali, Russian for let’s go, with a wave of his hand. The word became famous across the Soviet Union and the world. Every Russian crew launched from Baikonur since then has followed traditions inspired by Gagarin, such as signing the door of their hotel room before launch."],
g:[["Cosmonaut","A Russian or Soviet astronaut"],["Orbit","The curved path of an object around a planet or star"],["Space Race","The competition between the Soviet Union and the United States in space exploration"],["Cosmodrome","A launch site for spacecraft"]],
f:["Gagarin was the first human in space, on 12 April 1961.","His flight lasted about 108 minutes.","He launched from Baikonur in Kazakhstan."]},

{id:"tenzing",t:"Tenzing Norgay",i:"🏔️",w:"Tenzing Norgay",a:["Sherpa Tenzing","Namgyal Wangdi"],born:"May 1914, Khumbu region, Nepal, or Tibet (birthplace disputed)",died:"9 May 1986, Darjeeling, India",nat:"Nepali Sherpa; later Indian citizen",known:"Reaching the summit of Mount Everest with Edmund Hillary in 1953",r:["everest","himal","explor","nzcountry"],
l:"Tenzing Norgay was a Sherpa mountaineer who, with the New Zealand climber Edmund Hillary, became the first person known to reach the summit of Mount Everest, the highest mountain on Earth, on 29 May 1953. Born in the Himalayas, he worked for years as a porter and climber on many expeditions before his historic climb as part of a British expedition. He became a hero in Nepal, India and around the world, and later trained young mountaineers in Darjeeling. His achievement brought worldwide recognition to the Sherpa people, whose skill and courage make most Himalayan climbs possible.",
d:[["1914","Born in the Himalayas"],["1935","Joins his first Everest expedition as a porter"],["1952","Climbs to a record height on Everest with a Swiss expedition"],["1953","Reaches the summit of Everest with Edmund Hillary"],["1954","Becomes director of field training at the Himalayan Mountaineering Institute"],["1986","Dies in Darjeeling"]],
s:[["Early life","Tenzing was born in 1914 into a Sherpa family. Whether he was born in the Khumbu region of Nepal or in Tibet is disputed; he grew up herding yaks near Everest, which Sherpas call Chomolungma. He was given the name Namgyal Wangdi but later took the name Tenzing Norgay, meaning wealthy fortunate follower of religion."],["The Sherpas","The Sherpas are a people of the high Himalayas of Nepal, whose ancestors migrated from Tibet centuries ago. Adapted to high altitude, they became famous as mountain guides and porters. Most Sherpas are Buddhists who regard the mountains as sacred."],["Porter and climber","As a teenager, Tenzing moved to Darjeeling in India to seek work with mountaineering expeditions. In 1935 he joined a British Everest expedition as a porter. Over the next years, he took part in many expeditions, becoming one of the most experienced Himalayan climbers."],["The Swiss attempts","In 1952 Tenzing joined two Swiss expeditions to Everest. With the Swiss climber Raymond Lambert, he reached about 8,595 meters on the southeast ridge, a record at the time, before being forced to turn back. The two became close friends."],["The 1953 expedition","In 1953 a large British expedition led by John Hunt attempted Everest. Tenzing was its sirdar, the leader of the Sherpa team, and also a climbing member. After a first summit pair turned back, Tenzing and Edmund Hillary, a New Zealand beekeeper, were chosen for the second attempt."],["The summit","On 29 May 1953, after a night at a tiny camp at about 8,500 meters, Tenzing and Hillary climbed the final ridge, including a rock step now called the Hillary Step, and reached the summit at about 11:30 in the morning. They spent about 15 minutes there. Tenzing left offerings of sweets and biscuits in the snow."],["Who was first?","For years, people asked who stepped onto the summit first. Both men said they reached it as a team. In his autobiography, Tenzing later wrote that Hillary had stepped onto the summit a little ahead of him."],["Fame","News of the climb reached Britain on the morning of Queen Elizabeth II’s coronation. Tenzing was celebrated in Nepal and India, received the George Medal from Britain and many other honors. Hillary was knighted. Tenzing, who could not read or write, spoke several languages."],["Teaching","In 1954 Tenzing became the first director of field training at the new Himalayan Mountaineering Institute in Darjeeling, where he trained many young climbers. Hillary devoted much of his life to building schools and hospitals for Sherpa communities through his Himalayan Trust."],["Legacy","Tenzing died in Darjeeling in 1986. His son Jamling Tenzing Norgay climbed Everest in 1996. Today Sherpas are essential to Everest climbing but face great dangers; an avalanche in 2014 killed 16 Nepali guides. Tenzing’s achievement is a reminder of the Sherpas’ vital role in mountaineering."]],
sp:["The photograph on the summit","The most famous photograph of the first ascent of Everest shows Tenzing Norgay standing on the summit, his ice axe raised high, flying the flags of the United Nations, Britain, Nepal and India. It was taken by Edmund Hillary. There is no photograph of Hillary on the summit; Tenzing had never used a camera, and Hillary later said that the summit was no place to start teaching him. The photo of Tenzing became one of the most famous images of the 20th century."],
g:[["Sherpa","A people of the Himalayas known as mountain guides"],["Sirdar","The leader of the Sherpa team on an expedition"],["Summit","The highest point of a mountain"],["Porter","A person who carries loads"]],
f:["Tenzing and Hillary reached Everest’s summit on 29 May 1953.","Tenzing was a Sherpa from the Himalayas.","The famous summit photo shows Tenzing."]},

{id:"cousteau",t:"Jacques Cousteau",i:"🤿",w:"Jacques Cousteau",a:["Jacques-Yves Cousteau","Captain Cousteau"],born:"11 June 1910, Saint-André-de-Cubzac, France",died:"25 June 1997, Paris, France",nat:"French",known:"Co-inventing the Aqua-Lung and showing the undersea world on film and television",r:["pacific","coral","conservation","trench"],
l:"Jacques Cousteau was a French naval officer, explorer, filmmaker and conservationist who opened the underwater world to millions of people. In 1943 he co-invented the Aqua-Lung, the first successful scuba diving equipment, which allowed divers to swim freely underwater. From his research ship Calypso, he made award-winning films and television series about ocean life. Later in life he became a passionate campaigner for protecting the oceans, though some of his early films showed practices now considered harmful.",
d:[["1910","Born in Saint-André-de-Cubzac, France"],["1943","Co-invents the Aqua-Lung"],["1950","Acquires the ship Calypso"],["1956","His film The Silent World wins the top prize at Cannes"],["1966","His first major American television series is broadcast"],["1997","Dies in Paris"]],
s:[["Early life","Cousteau was born in 1910 in southwestern France. As a boy he loved machines and swimming and made his own films with a home movie camera. He joined the French Navy in 1930, hoping to become a pilot."],["Discovering the sea","In 1936 a serious car accident ended his hopes of flying. To strengthen his injured arms, he swam in the Mediterranean, and a friend gave him a pair of swimming goggles. Looking underwater for the first time, he was amazed and decided to devote his life to the sea."],["The Aqua-Lung","In 1943, during World War II, Cousteau and the engineer Émile Gagnan developed the Aqua-Lung, a tank of compressed air with a valve that supplied air at the right pressure as a diver breathed. For the first time, divers could stay underwater for long periods and move freely, without heavy suits or air hoses."],["Calypso","In 1950 Cousteau acquired Calypso, a former minesweeper, and turned it into a research vessel with an underwater observation chamber. For decades, Calypso carried Cousteau and his team to oceans around the world."],["The Silent World","Cousteau’s book The Silent World, published in 1953, sold millions of copies. The film of the same name, made with Louis Malle, won the Palme d’Or at the Cannes Film Festival in 1956 and an Academy Award, showing audiences coral reefs, sharks and shipwrecks for the first time."],["Living under the sea","In the 1960s, Cousteau’s Conshelf projects placed divers in underwater houses on the seabed, where they lived for weeks, to test whether people could live and work beneath the sea."],["Television","From the 1960s, Cousteau’s television series, such as The Undersea World of Jacques Cousteau, brought ocean life into homes around the world. With his red cap and French accent, he became one of the most recognizable people of his time."],["Criticism","Some of Cousteau’s early films, including The Silent World, showed practices now seen as harmful, such as using dynamite on a coral reef to count fish and killing sharks. As his understanding grew, he changed his views and became a leading voice for protecting marine life."],["Protecting the oceans","Cousteau campaigned against pollution, overfishing and the dumping of nuclear waste at sea. In 1973 he founded the Cousteau Society. He campaigned to protect Antarctica from mining, contributing to a 1991 agreement banning mining there for at least 50 years."],["Legacy","Cousteau died in Paris in 1997, aged 87. His films inspired generations of divers, marine biologists and environmentalists. Scuba diving, made possible by his invention, is now enjoyed by millions of people at reefs around the world, including in the Philippines."]],
sp:["The first breath underwater","In June 1943, in a quiet cove on the French Mediterranean coast, Jacques Cousteau strapped on the first Aqua-Lung, built with Émile Gagnan, and waded into the sea. As he breathed through the mouthpiece, air flowed smoothly, and he swam down, turning somersaults and gliding over the seabed. He later wrote that he felt like a man-fish, freed from gravity. That summer, he and his friends made hundreds of dives, exploring shipwrecks and caves. Their invention changed exploration of the oceans forever."],
g:[["Scuba","Self-contained underwater breathing apparatus"],["Aqua-Lung","The first successful scuba equipment"],["Conservationist","A person who works to protect nature"],["Minesweeper","A ship used to clear sea mines"]],
f:["Cousteau co-invented the Aqua-Lung in 1943.","His ship was called Calypso.","The Silent World won the top prize at Cannes in 1956."]},

{id:"muhammadali",t:"Muhammad Ali",i:"🥊",w:"Muhammad Ali",a:["Cassius Marcellus Clay Jr.","The Greatest"],born:"17 January 1942, Louisville, Kentucky, United States",died:"3 June 2016, Scottsdale, Arizona, United States",nat:"American",known:"Three-time world heavyweight boxing champion and civil rights figure",r:["olymp","vietnam","islam","drccountry"],
l:"Muhammad Ali was an American boxer, widely regarded as one of the greatest athletes of the 20th century. Born Cassius Clay, he won Olympic gold in 1960 and became world heavyweight champion three times. Famous for his speed, wit and boastful poetry, he called himself The Greatest. Ali was also a figure of controversy and courage: he joined the Nation of Islam, changed his name and refused to be drafted into the US Army during the Vietnam War, losing his title and years of his career. Two of his greatest fights took place in Africa and in the Philippines.",
d:[["1942","Born in Louisville, Kentucky"],["1960","Wins Olympic gold in Rome"],["1964","Becomes world heavyweight champion and changes his name"],["1967","Refuses induction into the US Army and is stripped of his title"],["1974","Regains the title in the Rumble in the Jungle"],["1975","Beats Joe Frazier in the Thrilla in Manila"]],
s:[["A stolen bicycle","Cassius Clay was born in 1942 in Louisville, Kentucky, in the segregated American South. At 12 his bicycle was stolen, and he told a police officer, Joe Martin, that he wanted to beat up the thief. Martin, who ran a boxing gym, suggested he learn to box first."],["Olympic champion","Clay won Olympic gold as a light heavyweight at the 1960 Rome Olympics, aged 18. Back home, he still faced racial discrimination, and according to one story he threw his medal into a river in anger, though this is disputed."],["World champion","In 1964 Clay, a big underdog, defeated the feared champion Sonny Liston to become world heavyweight champion. Before the fight he boasted that he would float like a butterfly and sting like a bee."],["A new name","Soon after, he announced that he had joined the Nation of Islam, a Black religious and political movement, and took the name Muhammad Ali. Many people refused to use his new name. He later followed mainstream Sunni Islam and Sufism."],["Refusing the draft","In 1967, during the Vietnam War, Ali refused to be drafted into the US Army, citing his religion and opposition to the war. He was stripped of his title, banned from boxing and convicted. For over three years he could not fight, at the peak of his career. In 1971 the US Supreme Court overturned his conviction unanimously."],["The Fight of the Century","In 1971 Ali fought the unbeaten champion Joe Frazier in New York in a bout called the Fight of the Century. Frazier won on points, knocking Ali down in the final round, Ali’s first professional defeat."],["Rumble in the Jungle","On 30 October 1974, in Kinshasa, Zaire, now the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Ali faced the powerful young champion George Foreman. Ali leaned on the ropes and let Foreman tire himself out, a tactic he called rope-a-dope, then knocked him out in the eighth round to regain the title."],["Thrilla in Manila","On 1 October 1975, Ali and Frazier met for the third time at the Araneta Coliseum in Quezon City, Philippines, in brutal heat. After 14 grueling rounds, Frazier’s trainer stopped the fight. Ali said it was the closest thing to dying he knew. The Thrilla in Manila is considered one of the greatest fights in boxing history."],["Later years","Ali lost his title in 1978, won it back the same year, becoming the first three-time heavyweight champion, and retired in 1981. In 1984 he was diagnosed with Parkinson’s disease. In 1996, his hands trembling, he lit the Olympic flame at the Atlanta Games, a deeply moving moment watched around the world."],["Legacy","Ali traveled widely as a goodwill ambassador and humanitarian. He received the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2005. He died in 2016, aged 74. He is remembered not only as a boxer but as a symbol of conviction, Black pride and standing up for one’s beliefs, though his harsh taunts of opponents, especially Frazier, are also remembered."]],
sp:["The Thrilla in Manila","At 10:45 in the morning on 1 October 1975, chosen to suit American television audiences, Muhammad Ali and Joe Frazier stepped into the ring at the Araneta Coliseum in Quezon City for their third and final fight. The temperature in the packed arena soared above 40 degrees Celsius under the lights. For 14 rounds, the two great rivals battered each other. Frazier’s eyes swelled almost shut, and his trainer, Eddie Futch, refused to let him continue. Ali, exhausted, collapsed after his victory. The fight made Manila famous worldwide, and the Araneta Coliseum still celebrates its place in boxing history."],
g:[["Heavyweight","The heaviest weight class in boxing"],["Draft","A system of compulsory military service"],["Rope-a-dope","Ali’s tactic of leaning on the ropes to tire an opponent"],["Underdog","A competitor expected to lose"]],
f:["Ali won Olympic gold in 1960.","He was the first three-time world heavyweight champion.","The Thrilla in Manila took place in Quezon City in 1975."]},

{id:"kipchoge",t:"Eliud Kipchoge",i:"🏃",w:"Eliud Kipchoge",a:["Eliud Kipchoge"],born:"5 November 1984, Kapsisiywa, Nandi County, Kenya",died:"",nat:"Kenyan",known:"Two-time Olympic marathon champion and the first person to run a marathon distance in under two hours",r:["kenyacountry","athletics","olymp","muhammadali"],
l:"Eliud Kipchoge is a Kenyan long-distance runner, widely considered one of the greatest marathon runners in history. He won the Olympic marathon in 2016 and 2021, set world records in the marathon in 2018 and 2022 and, in 2019, became the first person to run the marathon distance in under two hours, though in a specially organized event that did not count as an official record. Known for his calm, humble manner and simple training life in Kenya, he inspires runners worldwide with his motto, no human is limited.",
d:[["1984","Born in Nandi County, Kenya"],["2003","Wins the world 5,000-meter title at 18"],["2013","Runs and wins his first marathon, in Hamburg"],["2016","Wins Olympic marathon gold in Rio de Janeiro"],["2019","Runs a marathon distance in under two hours in Vienna"],["2022","Sets a marathon world record of 2:01:09 in Berlin"]],
s:[["Running to school","Kipchoge was born in 1984 in Nandi County in Kenya’s Rift Valley, the youngest of four children, raised by his mother, a teacher. Like many Kenyan children, he ran several kilometers to and from school each day."],["A coach next door","As a teenager, Kipchoge asked a neighbor, the Olympic runner Patrick Sang, for a training plan. Sang became his coach and mentor for decades, guiding his whole career."],["Track champion","At just 18, Kipchoge won the 5,000 meters at the 2003 World Championships in Paris, defeating two of the greatest runners of the time, Hicham El Guerrouj and Kenenisa Bekele. He won Olympic bronze in 2004 and silver in 2008 in the 5,000 meters."],["The marathon","In 2013 Kipchoge moved to the marathon, 42.195 kilometers, winning his first race in Hamburg. He went on to win most of the world’s major marathons, including London four times and Berlin five times."],["Olympic gold","Kipchoge won the Olympic marathon in Rio de Janeiro in 2016 and again in Sapporo, Japan, at the Tokyo Olympics held in 2021, becoming only the third person to win two Olympic marathons."],["World records","Kipchoge set a marathon world record of 2:01:39 in Berlin in 2018, breaking the previous record by more than a minute, and lowered it to 2:01:09 in Berlin in 2022. In 2023 the young Kenyan Kelvin Kiptum broke the record, but Kiptum tragically died in a car crash in 2024. In April 2026, Sabastian Sawe of Kenya ran 1:59:30 in London, the first official marathon under two hours."],["Under two hours","On 12 October 2019, in Vienna, Kipchoge ran the marathon distance in 1 hour, 59 minutes and 40 seconds, the first person to do so. The event used rotating teams of pacemakers and a car projecting a laser line to guide them, so it was not recognized as an official record, but it showed what the human body could achieve."],["Life at the camp","Despite his fame and wealth, Kipchoge lived for much of the week at a simple training camp in Kaptagat, Kenya, sharing chores such as cleaning and fetching water with other runners. He reads books on self-improvement and keeps detailed training diaries."],["Kenya’s running tradition","Kenya, especially the Kalenjin communities of the Rift Valley highlands, has produced many of the world’s best distance runners. Scientists point to factors such as high altitude, active childhoods, diet and a strong culture of running and role models."],["Legacy","Kipchoge’s calm smile while running, which he says helps him relax, and his motto, no human is limited, have made him a global inspiration. In 2024 he did not finish the Olympic marathon in Paris, but he remains one of the most celebrated athletes of his generation."]],
sp:["The 1:59 challenge","For years, scientists debated whether a human could ever run a marathon in under two hours. In October 2019, on a flat, tree-lined avenue in Vienna’s Prater park, Eliud Kipchoge attempted it. Forty-one world-class runners took turns as pacemakers, running in a V-shaped formation in front of him to block the wind, while an electric car projected green laser lines on the road to show the target pace. As Kipchoge crossed the finish line in 1:59:40, crowds roared and he pounded his chest. Millions watched online. It was not an official record, but it broke a barrier many had thought impossible."],
g:[["Marathon","A running race of 42.195 kilometers"],["Pacemaker","A runner who sets the speed for others"],["World record","The best performance ever officially recorded"],["Altitude","Height above sea level"]],
f:["Kipchoge won Olympic marathon gold in 2016 and 2021.","He ran a marathon distance in under two hours in 2019.","His motto is no human is limited."]}
);

