window.PEOPLE=window.PEOPLE||[];window.PEOPLE.push(
{id:"schweitzer",t:"Albert Schweitzer",i:"🎼",w:"Albert Schweitzer",a:["Albert Schweitzer"],born:"14 January 1875, Kaysersberg, Alsace (then Germany, now France)",died:"4 September 1965, Lambaréné, Gabon",nat:"Alsatian (German, later French)",known:"Founding a hospital in Lambaréné, Gabon, and his philosophy of reverence for life",r:["nightingale","teresa","bach","ethics"],
l:"Albert Schweitzer was a theologian, philosopher, organist and doctor from Alsace who, at the age of 30, began studying medicine so that he could serve people in Africa. In 1913 he founded a hospital at Lambaréné, in what is now Gabon, where he worked for most of the rest of his life. He developed a philosophy he called reverence for life, the idea that all living things deserve respect. He won the Nobel Peace Prize for 1952 and later campaigned against nuclear weapons. He was widely admired, though some later critics saw paternalistic colonial attitudes in his views of Africans.",
d:[["1875","Born in Kaysersberg, Alsace"],["1905","Decides to study medicine to serve in Africa"],["1913","Founds a hospital at Lambaréné"],["1915","Develops the idea of reverence for life"],["1953","Receives the Nobel Peace Prize for 1952"],["1965","Dies at Lambaréné"]],
s:[["Early life","Schweitzer was born in 1875 in Alsace, a region then part of Germany, the son of a Lutheran pastor. He grew up loving music and religion and began playing the organ as a boy."],["Scholar and musician","By his late twenties, Schweitzer was a respected theologian, writing an influential book on the search for the historical Jesus, and a famous organist and expert on the music of Johann Sebastian Bach, about whom he wrote a major biography."],["A new calling","At 21, Schweitzer resolved to devote himself to direct service to humanity from the age of 30. In 1905 he read an appeal for doctors in French Equatorial Africa and decided to study medicine, despite the shock of friends and family. He qualified as a doctor in 1913."],["Lambaréné","In 1913 Schweitzer and his wife, Hélène Bresslau, a trained nurse, went to Lambaréné on the Ogooué River, in present-day Gabon. They began treating patients in a chicken coop and built a hospital that grew to serve thousands, including people with leprosy."],["Interned in war","During World War I, as German citizens in a French colony, Schweitzer and his wife were interned and later sent to camps in France. After the war, he raised money through concerts and lectures and returned to rebuild his hospital in 1924."],["Reverence for life","In 1915, while traveling on a river in Africa, the phrase reverence for life came to him. He taught that all living beings have a will to live and deserve respect, and that ethics means caring for life in all its forms."],["Nobel Peace Prize","Schweitzer was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize for 1952 and used the prize money to build a village for people with leprosy at Lambaréné. He became one of the most famous humanitarians in the world."],["Against nuclear weapons","In 1957 Schweitzer broadcast a Declaration of Conscience calling for an end to nuclear weapons tests because of the dangers of radiation. He joined other campaigners in pressing for a test ban."],["Criticism","Some visitors and later historians criticized Schweitzer for paternalistic attitudes, describing Africans in ways shaped by colonial ideas, and for running his hospital in an old-fashioned way. Others defended his lifetime of service. His legacy reflects both admiration and debate."],["Legacy","Schweitzer died at Lambaréné in 1965, aged 90. The Albert Schweitzer Hospital continues to serve patients today, and Schweitzer fellowships support young people in health service. His idea of reverence for life influenced environmental and animal ethics."]],
sp:["Concerts for a hospital","To pay for his hospital in Africa, Albert Schweitzer relied on his skills as an organist. Between his stays at Lambaréné, he toured Europe giving organ concerts, especially of Bach’s music, and lectures, raising money for medicines and buildings. The Paris Bach Society even gave him a special piano with an organ pedal board, lined with zinc to protect it from termites and humidity, which was shipped to Lambaréné so he could keep practicing in the evenings after long days of treating patients."],
g:[["Theologian","A person who studies religious ideas"],["Humanitarian","A person who works to improve the lives of others"],["Leprosy","A disease affecting the skin and nerves, now curable"],["Reverence for life","Schweitzer’s idea of respect for all living things"]],
f:["Schweitzer founded a hospital in Gabon in 1913.","He was a famous Bach organist.","He won the Nobel Peace Prize for 1952."]},

{id:"romero",t:"Óscar Romero",i:"🕊️",w:"Óscar Romero",a:["Saint Óscar Arnulfo Romero"],born:"15 August 1917, Ciudad Barrios, El Salvador",died:"24 March 1980, San Salvador, El Salvador",nat:"Salvadoran",known:"Speaking out for the poor and against violence as Archbishop of San Salvador",r:["christ","hrights","lorenzoruiz","tutu"],
l:"Óscar Romero was a Roman Catholic priest who became Archbishop of San Salvador in 1977, during a time of growing violence and repression in El Salvador. Once seen as cautious and conservative, he became a powerful voice for the poor and for victims of human rights abuses, speaking out against killings, torture and disappearances in his sermons broadcast by radio across the country. On 24 March 1980 he was shot dead while celebrating Mass. He is honored around the world as a defender of human rights, and in 2018 Pope Francis declared him a saint.",
d:[["1917","Born in Ciudad Barrios, El Salvador"],["1942","Ordained a priest in Rome"],["1977","Becomes Archbishop of San Salvador"],["1977","His friend Father Rutilio Grande is murdered"],["1980","Is assassinated while celebrating Mass"],["2018","Is declared a saint by Pope Francis"]],
s:[["Early life","Romero was born in 1917 in a small town in eastern El Salvador. He trained briefly as a carpenter before entering the seminary, studied in Rome and was ordained a priest in 1942. He worked for many years as a parish priest and church official, known as a quiet, studious man."],["El Salvador in crisis","In the 1970s El Salvador was deeply unequal, with a small elite owning much of the land while most people were poor. Peasants, workers and students who demanded change faced repression from the military and paramilitary death squads, while leftist guerrilla groups also grew."],["Archbishop","In 1977 Romero was appointed Archbishop of San Salvador. Many expected him to avoid controversy. But weeks later, his friend Rutilio Grande, a Jesuit priest who worked with poor farmers, was murdered along with two companions. The killing deeply changed Romero."],["Voice of the voiceless","Romero began to speak out forcefully against violence and injustice. In his Sunday sermons, broadcast by the church radio station, he named victims, reported abuses and called for justice, becoming known as the voice of the voiceless."],["Pressure and threats","Romero was accused by critics of meddling in politics and supporting the left, while he insisted he was following the Gospel and condemning violence by all sides. He received death threats, and the church radio station was bombed."],["The final sermon","On 23 March 1980, Romero appealed directly to soldiers and police, urging them to obey God’s law and stop killing their own people: in the name of God, stop the repression. The next day, while celebrating Mass in a hospital chapel, he was shot through the heart by a gunman."],["Civil war","Romero’s murder was followed by a civil war that lasted until 1992 and killed some 75,000 people. A United Nations Truth Commission later concluded that the killing was ordered by Roberto D’Aubuisson, a former army officer who founded a right-wing party."],["Sainthood","After decades of debate, Pope Francis beatified Romero in 2015 and canonized him as a saint in 2018, recognizing him as a martyr killed in hatred of the faith. Many Salvadorans had long called him San Romero de América."],["Recognition","The United Nations marks 24 March, the anniversary of his death, as the International Day for the Right to the Truth concerning Gross Human Rights Violations and for the Dignity of Victims. Statues of Romero stand in many places, including Westminster Abbey in London."],["Legacy","Romero is remembered as a symbol of courage, nonviolence and solidarity with the poor. His life is often compared with that of other church figures who spoke out under authoritarian rule, and his words continue to inspire human rights defenders around the world."]],
sp:["The voice on the radio","Every Sunday in the late 1970s, people across El Salvador gathered around radios to listen to Archbishop Romero’s sermons at the cathedral in San Salvador, broadcast by the church station YSAX. His homilies could last more than an hour. Besides explaining the Bible, he read out the names of people who had been killed, arrested or disappeared that week, giving news that was often censored elsewhere. For many poor families, it was the only place they heard the truth about what was happening. Surveys found that his broadcasts were among the most listened-to programs in the country."],
g:[["Archbishop","A senior bishop in charge of a major diocese"],["Death squad","An armed group that carries out killings outside the law"],["Canonize","To declare someone a saint"],["Homily","A sermon given during Mass"]],
f:["Romero became Archbishop of San Salvador in 1977.","He was killed on 24 March 1980.","He was declared a saint in 2018."]},

{id:"wallenberg",t:"Raoul Wallenberg",i:"📜",w:"Raoul Wallenberg",a:["Raoul Gustaf Wallenberg"],born:"4 August 1912, Lidingö, Sweden",died:"Unknown; disappeared in Soviet custody after January 1945",nat:"Swedish",known:"Saving tens of thousands of Jews in Budapest during the Holocaust",r:["holocaust","ww2","swedencountry","hrights"],
l:"Raoul Wallenberg was a Swedish businessman and diplomat who, in 1944 and 1945, saved tens of thousands of Jews in Budapest, Hungary, from the Holocaust. Working at the Swedish legation, he issued protective passports, set up safe houses flying the Swedish flag and confronted Nazi and Hungarian officials to stop deportations. In January 1945 he was detained by Soviet forces and disappeared. The Soviet Union later claimed he died in prison in 1947, but his fate has never been fully explained. He is honored as one of the great rescuers of the Holocaust.",
d:[["1912","Born in Lidingö, Sweden"],["1935","Graduates in architecture from the University of Michigan"],["1944","Arrives in Budapest as a Swedish diplomat"],["1945","Is detained by Soviet forces and disappears"],["1963","Is recognized as Righteous Among the Nations"],["1981","Becomes an honorary citizen of the United States"]],
s:[["Early life","Wallenberg was born in 1912 into one of Sweden’s most prominent families of bankers and diplomats. His father died before he was born. He studied architecture in the United States and worked in business in South Africa and Palestine, where he met Jewish refugees from Nazi Germany."],["Hungary in 1944","In 1944 Germany occupied Hungary, its ally, and began deporting Hungarian Jews to the Auschwitz death camp. Within weeks, more than 400,000 Jews from the Hungarian countryside were deported, most of them murdered on arrival. Hundreds of thousands of Jews remained in Budapest."],["A mission of rescue","The United States War Refugee Board and Sweden, a neutral country, sought someone to organize a rescue operation. Wallenberg, aged 31, was appointed to the Swedish legation in Budapest and arrived in July 1944, with a mission to save as many Jews as possible."],["Protective passports","Wallenberg designed an official-looking protective passport, the Schutz-Pass, in Swedish colors with the Swedish coat of arms, declaring its holder under Swedish protection. Though of doubtful legal value, it was often respected by officials. Thousands were issued."],["Safe houses","Wallenberg rented dozens of buildings in Budapest, declared them Swedish territory and sheltered thousands of Jews there. He hired hundreds of staff, many of them Jewish, and provided food and medical care."],["Daring rescues","When Jews were forced on death marches toward the Austrian border or onto trains, Wallenberg sometimes drove after them, climbing onto trains and handing out protective passes, demanding the release of those he claimed were under Swedish protection. He negotiated, bribed and threatened officials."],["Saving the ghetto","In the final days before Soviet forces captured Budapest, Wallenberg is credited with helping prevent a planned massacre of the Jews in the Budapest ghetto by warning German commanders they would be held responsible. Tens of thousands survived."],["Disappearance","On 17 January 1945, Wallenberg went to meet Soviet commanders and was detained. He was taken to Moscow. In 1957 the Soviet Union claimed he had died of a heart attack in Lubyanka prison in July 1947, but witnesses reported seeing him later, and the full truth remains unknown."],["Honors","Israel’s Yad Vashem recognized Wallenberg as Righteous Among the Nations in 1963. He became an honorary citizen of the United States, Canada, Hungary, Australia and Israel. In 2016 Sweden formally declared him dead."],["Legacy","Wallenberg showed how one determined person could save thousands of lives. Streets, schools and monuments around the world bear his name, and his story is taught as an example of courage and moral responsibility in the face of genocide."]],
sp:["The Schutz-Pass","When Raoul Wallenberg arrived in Budapest in 1944, Swedish diplomats were already issuing some certificates to Jews with ties to Sweden. Wallenberg, trained as an architect and businessman, redesigned them to look as official and impressive as possible, in blue and yellow with the Swedish crown and stamps and signatures. He knew that officials respected official-looking documents. The new Schutz-Pass declared that its holder was under the protection of the Swedish legation. Wallenberg and his staff issued thousands of these passes, and they saved many lives at a time when a piece of paper could mean the difference between life and death."],
g:[["Holocaust","The genocide of six million Jews by Nazi Germany and its collaborators"],["Legation","A diplomatic mission, similar to an embassy"],["Righteous Among the Nations","An honor for non-Jews who saved Jews during the Holocaust"],["Neutral country","A country that does not take sides in a war"]],
f:["Wallenberg saved tens of thousands of Jews in Budapest.","He disappeared in Soviet custody in 1945.","He was recognized as Righteous Among the Nations in 1963."]},

{id:"farmer",t:"Paul Farmer",i:"🏥",w:"Paul Farmer",a:["Paul Edward Farmer"],born:"26 October 1959, North Adams, Massachusetts, United States",died:"21 February 2022, Butaro, Rwanda",nat:"American",known:"Co-founding Partners In Health and bringing high-quality health care to the poorest communities",r:["tb","hiv","rwandacountry","nightingale"],
l:"Paul Farmer was an American doctor and medical anthropologist who co-founded Partners In Health, an organization that brings high-quality health care to some of the world’s poorest communities. Beginning in rural Haiti in the 1980s, he and his colleagues showed that diseases such as tuberculosis, including drug-resistant forms, and HIV could be treated successfully in poor countries, challenging experts who said it was too expensive. He worked in Haiti, Peru, Rwanda, Russia and elsewhere and taught at Harvard University. He died in Rwanda in 2022, aged 62.",
d:[["1959","Born in Massachusetts"],["1983","Begins working in rural Haiti"],["1987","Co-founds Partners In Health"],["1990s","Shows that drug-resistant tuberculosis can be treated in Peru"],["2015","Helps found the University of Global Health Equity in Rwanda"],["2022","Dies in Butaro, Rwanda"]],
s:[["An unusual childhood","Farmer grew up in a large family that for some years lived in a converted school bus and later on a boat in Florida. He worked alongside Haitian farm workers as a teenager, sparking his interest in Haiti."],["Haiti","As a young man, Farmer went to Haiti, then one of the poorest countries in the Americas, and began working in the village of Cange in the central plateau. While studying medicine and anthropology at Harvard, he traveled back and forth, building a clinic with local partners."],["Partners In Health","In 1987 Farmer co-founded Partners In Health with Ophelia Dahl, Jim Yong Kim, Todd McCormack and Tom White. In Haiti it worked as Zanmi Lasante, meaning partners in health in Haitian Creole. It trained local community health workers to visit patients at home."],["Treating the poor","Farmer insisted that poor patients deserved the same quality of care as the rich, a principle he described with the phrase a preferential option for the poor, borrowed from liberation theology. His clinics provided free care, medicines, food and clean water."],["Drug-resistant tuberculosis","In the 1990s, in poor neighborhoods of Lima, Peru, Partners In Health treated people with drug-resistant tuberculosis, which experts had considered too costly to treat in poor countries. Their success helped change World Health Organization policy and lower drug prices."],["HIV treatment","Farmer and his colleagues showed that people with HIV in rural Haiti could take antiretroviral medicines successfully with the support of community health workers, helping convince the world to make HIV treatment available in poor countries."],["Rwanda","After the 1994 genocide, Partners In Health worked with Rwanda’s government to rebuild rural health care. It helped build a hospital in Butaro and the University of Global Health Equity, which opened in 2015, training health workers for Africa."],["Teacher and writer","Farmer taught at Harvard Medical School and wrote books about health, poverty and human rights, such as Pathologies of Power. His life was described in Tracy Kidder’s bestselling book Mountains Beyond Mountains."],["Disaster response","After the 2010 earthquake in Haiti, Farmer served as a United Nations deputy special envoy and helped build a large teaching hospital in Mirebalais. Partners In Health also responded to the Ebola epidemic in West Africa in 2014."],["Legacy","Farmer died suddenly in his sleep in Butaro in February 2022. Tributes came from around the world. He is remembered for proving that excellent health care is possible in the poorest places and for his belief that health is a human right."]],
sp:["Mountains beyond mountains","A Haitian proverb says deyò mòn, gen mòn: beyond mountains, there are mountains. It means that when you solve one problem, another appears. The writer Tracy Kidder used it as the title of his 2003 book about Paul Farmer, which described Farmer hiking for hours over Haiti’s mountains to visit a single patient. When critics questioned whether such effort was worth it, Farmer replied that every patient mattered. The book inspired many young people to study medicine and public health and to work in poor communities."],
g:[["Partners In Health","A global health organization co-founded by Farmer"],["Community health worker","A local person trained to support patients at home"],["Drug-resistant","Not killed by usual medicines"],["Medical anthropology","The study of health and illness in their social context"]],
f:["Farmer co-founded Partners In Health in 1987.","He began his work in rural Haiti.","He died in Rwanda in 2022."]}
);

